      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 24, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 24, 1914.</p>
        <p>4:0"....+444,..... o+o++ +++44 0+ 0 +44+o +4* .4. 4•4•4•44+ 0 4:. i. 4.9.:. +t) • 4.:• 4. tl• 4.4 +4* ++ *:+4.44")..40 0.4)........+404.4.. 4.0 0., 4•4•o++ 4. 4. 4.4•4. ++++ 4. 4* +4.4P •:• . 4.. • • WOMAN'S Worn., ...... .. D. rit .4. .. „...._ .</p>
        <p>if necessary, she inspired her fellow townsfolk with courage, by her calmness and dignity dur- ing the bombardment. She gave out supplies, organised help for the wounded, and by her work and example prevented a panic, and saved the lives of numbers of the inhabitants.</p>
        <p>The Queen of the Belgians. The sympathies of every woman in these isles goes out to the brave and devoted Queen of the Belgians, who has remained by the side of her heroic husband throughout the tragic happen- ings in Belgium. The daughter of a most benevolent Bavarian prince, she had a quiet and simple upbringing, for her royal father laid aside his rank and devoted himself to the study of medicine. He is one of Germany's cleverest oculists, and freely gives his services to the poor and afflicted. His noble daughter has, since her marriage to Sing Albert, followed closely the example and teaching of her father, and is one of the most learned and intelligent of the Royal ladies of Europe.</p>
        <p>France's New Capital. The Belgian Court and Government are now established at Havre, which, like Bordeaux, becomes temporarily one of the capital cities of Europe, but in one sense Belgium as a kingdom and nation has, for the time being, ceased to exist. The women of Bordeaux are noted for their good looks and fresh complexions, said to be due to the soft breezes of the Atlantic. They have dark eyes and hair, and beautiful white teeth, and the working classes wear a red flan- nel hood, that is particularly becoming. The theatre in Bordeaux is one of the handsomest buildings in Europe, and Napoleon Bonaparte regretted that he could not remove it to Paris.</p>
        <p>The Success of the Straight Bod ice. Before war broke out, or was even dreamt of by ordinary mortals, experts had decided that the modes of the early eighties were to reign this autumn. People old enough to remember the eesthetic craze of those days had looked forward to very attractive and artistic styles, nor have they been altogether disappointed, although only a few adaptations of 1880 modes have made their appearance. These all show a marked tendency towards greater simplicity than would have been the case had peace continued to prevail. The</p>
        <p>long bodice, which bears but little resemblance to its prototype of Victorian days, is deservedly a success. It has proved far less trying than was anticipated, and it shows an unbroken line at the back, which is distinctly becoming to short women, especially if inclined to embonpoint, as well as to the moderately tall.</p>
        <p>The All-in-One Frock. This bodice fastens down the front, is high to the throat, has no revers or accessories of any kind, and is often allied to a pleated skirt minus a tunic. Being all in one piece, it is very easily put on, for there are no superfluous buttons or hooks, and in this guise it is simplicity itself. Its only adornment is the sash, which generally starts from the side, where it is rather high. It is then crossed in front and knotted rather low. This gives a slim effect to a stout figure. The newest sashes do not encircle the waist. They start at the sides, cross in front, and are then drawn to the back, where they are knotted often as low as the knee. All the new models for afternoon wear, as well as the type of gown known as the " little frock," fasten in front, evening dresses forming the only exception to this sensible rule. The fact that every woman works more or less for the soldiers and sailors, accounts for the popularity of the all-in-one : frock, generally carried out in serge, but also ' frequently in beige cloth. The Children's Winter Garments. Children's clothes always reflect the fashions worn by their elders, and long-waisted dresses have found their way into the schoolroom and nursery. Frills or pleats below give the basque or tunic effect. The military note is more obvi- ously struck in the long coats of adults than in any other garments intended for outdoor wear, : and coats of this type are very popular for both ; boys and girls. Pliable black velvet-felt, or , chenille hats, black shoes, stockings and gloves, are worn with coloured tailored suits, or with ,oloured coats. The collars. and belts on over- coats of the military type for children are dis- tinctive in character, and have emblems made of gilt or silver braid. A hat trimming to be worn with one of these garments resembles the chin-straps of the old-fashioned helmets. Although made of metal, it is very supple, and can he arranged in a bow or cockade at the side, lafter being passed round the base of the crown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The King and His Soldiers. His Majesty King George spends as much time as possible with his soldiers in their various training camps, which he inspects; or, accompanied by Her Majesty, he visits the wounded in the many hospitals prepared for the reception of our sailors and soldiers. During his recent visit to Aldershot, Princess Mary accompanied the King on his morning rides. Queen Alexandra's present sojourn in London is the longest she has made in the capital since she came to this country, but the King and Queen, who are in need of a change, have been spending a few days at Sandringham.</p>
        <p>Women and Royal Honours. The Queen has a scheme in contemplation, which may, if carried out, include women in the next list of Royal honours. So many mem- bers of the weaker sex have distinguished them- selves by special service to the country in these times of stress and warfare, that some public acknowledgment is deemed only a fitting recog- nition of the good work which flows in many channels. ,The Duchess of Westminster, fox instance, has equipped a hospital for the wounded at the front, which is perfect in every detail. It was taken to France in Sir Thomas Lipton's yacht Erin. The Duchess herself is at the head of the hospital, to which ten doctors, twenty nursing sisters, and sixty-two orderlies are attached. Madame Macherez. _</p>
        <p>...--...- ...__.._. The name of a brave Frenchwoman will be imperishably associated with the town of Sois- sons, which suffered so cruelly from the Kaiser's Huns. In the absence of the municipal authori- ties, the German officers were received by this lady, Madame Macherez. the wife of the senator of l'Aisne. Quite prepared.to sacrifice her life,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Use a wire egg whip when beating whites of eggs if bulk is desired. The mistake of giving plants too little water is not so bad as giving them too much. To prevent a lamp smoking, soak the wick in strong vinegar and dry thoroughly before using. A feather brush is preferable to a cloth for dusting gift picture frames, as the cloth wears and deadens the gilt. A little vinegar placed in the rinsing water on washing day will prevent the hands from becoming rough and chapped. If moths are in a carpet, spread a damp towel over the part and iron it dry with a hot iron. The heat and steam will kill the worms and eggs. Hard boiled eggs should be plunged into cold water as soon as they are taken out of the sauce- pan. This will prevent the dark rings from ap- pearing round the yolk.</p>
        <p>Savoury Balls. Quantities of bread crumbs and mashed pota- toes, one hard-boiled egg cut small, a little onion chopped very fine, chopped parsley and thyme, pepper and salt, a little piece of butter melted. Bind all together with beaten egg, form into balls, dip into egg and bread crumbs, place in pie-dish and bake. Serve with brown gravy.</p>
        <p>Macaroni Cheese. Boil one ounce macaroni in salted boiling water thirty minutes, dr:tin, place in a buttered dish, with alternate layers ef one and a tall ounces grated cheese, cover with Eread-ct:umbs, place a few pieces of butter on the top, and bake.</p>
        <p>For Rough Hands. The following paste will make the roughest hands smooth if constantly used: Mix one quarter pound of unsalted lard, after clarifying and dipping in rose-water, with the yolks of two eggs and a large spoonful of honey. Add as much paste of almonds, well pounded in a mor- tar, as will work this compound into a paste.</p>
        <p>To Clean Marble. To clean marble mix well together two parts of soda to one part Powdered pumice-stone, and one part finely-powdered chalk. Mix these into a paste with a little water, and rub well all over the marble. If possible, leave the paste on the marble overnight, and the next morning wash off with soap and water, dry and polish with a soft, clean cloth.</p>
        <p>To Remove Dampness and Mould. When dampness in a cellar or basement be- comes objectionable it may be removed by anhy- drous calcium chloride. One lb. of the salt is sufficient for a large cellar. It may be placed in old tin cans. The. moisture absorbed from the air collects in the cans. This, however, is not poured away, but is evaporated on a strong Bre, and filo salt may then be used again. Unslacked lime is best suited to exterminate mould. It is blown in the shape of a fine powder, into the crevices and on the walls, which must be damp. —H. A. Doerner, Colorado Agricultural College.</p>
        <p>Storing Fruit. Those who have well-appointed fruit rooms or other suitable buildings where they have been in the habit of storing large quantities of fruit will not need advice. Others with but little experience in such matters are often puzzled as to what to do for the best. To these I give the following brief hints: Apples may be success- fully stored in any frost-proof building. Line the floors and side walls with a layer of clean straw, and t arefully cover the prepared space with layers of sound fruit to a depth of 12 or 15 inches. Give all the ventilation possible for a couple of weeks, then close the building and keep it dark. During very severe frosts cover the surface of the heaps with straw. For small quantities store in boxes, or barrels lined with paper. Leave the tops of the receptacles un- covered except during severe frosts. Specimen fruits should be exposed for a week, then wrapped in paper and packed in boxes or drawers. Late varieties keep splendidly packed in boxes, with layers of fresh peat-moss be- tween the fruits.—" Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE NED. "3 &gt;Q42•o,2o(wxsako:24vo=2,osa-2,04c2A</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE NED. "3 &gt;Q42•o,2o(wxsako:24vo=2,osa-2,04c2A</p>
        <p>ME DEAR CHILDREN,— I know that you ard all willing to help our country. I have already mentioned the good • you will do if you take an interest in your lessons at school, and so make yourselves better educated than the children in Germany. Yet we will not beat the Germans with education alone; we must be healthy. The health of the nation depends upon every Man and woman, boy and girl, taking care of their own health. That is my point this week. All my nephews and nieces want to grow into healthy men and women. But you cannot ex- pect to be strong if you don't take care of your- selyes. It is the little things that matter in life. A good many illnesses can be traced to bad teeth, go don't you think it would be well to look after them? They should be cleaned at least once a day. If you practise this, it will grow into a good habit, and when you grow up you will be thankful that you took the advice. Be sure to read the report of how Nelson day was observed in the schools this week. Your loving</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>A VERY OLD TREE. , At the foot of Mount Etna there is tree which is said to be over 2,000 Years old, cliesinu ltt is nearly 214 feet in circumference.</p>
        <p>THE SILK HAT. There is an account in "The Times" of Jan,. ary 16, 1797, of the man who first tried the ea_ periment of wearing a silk hat. The report in the newspaper reads : John Hetherington, haberdasher, of the , Strand, was arraigned before the Lord Mayor yesterday on a charge of breach of the peace and inciting a riot, and was required to give bonds for the sum of £5OO. It was stated in evidence that Mr. Hetherington, who is well connected, appeared on the public highway wearing,upon his head what he called a silk hat, - which was offered in etidence ; a tall structure, having a shiny lustre, calculated to frighten timid people. As a matter of fact, the officers of the Crown stated that several women fainted at the sight, children screamed, and a son of Cord- wainer' Thomas was thrown down by the crowd which had collected, and his right arm broken. Atv-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>Tact is the leaven that saves flattery from falling flat. Every woman lives to congratulate herself that there was some man she didn't marry.</p>
        <p>Ner try to make a mun feel at home if you know him to be henpecked.</p>
        <p>Most men would rather take their chances with a plague than with a shorthaired woman.</p>
        <p>The Bachelors Explained. At a wedding breakfast the bachelors present were urged to state their reasons for remaining single. Among those given were the follow- ing " I am like a frog in the fable, who, though he loved the water, would not jump into the well because he could not jump out again." " I am too selfish and honest enough to admit it." "I prefer, on the one hand, liberty, refresh- ing sleep, the opera, midnight suppers, quiet seclusion, dreams, cigars, a bank account and club to, on the other hand, disturbed rest, cold meat, baby linen, soothing syrup, rocking horses, bread pudding and empty pockets." "I have a twin brother, and we have never had a secret from one another. He is married."</p>
        <p>When Mother Made the Bread. The bride resolutely dried her eyes on her lace-trimmed handkerchief. "So my muffins are rfot like mother used to make," she said significantly. "Well, to- morrow he shall have jnuffins like mother's." "Now you have hit it!" exclaimed the young husband enthusiastically. "(These muffins are exactly like mother used to make when I was a little chap. How did you do it, dearest?" "I will give you the recipe," replied the bride coldly. "I used oleomargarine for but- ter, cold-storage eggs, put alum in the flour and added chalk and water to the milk—for, if you will recall, your mother lived before the pure food laws."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>JAPANESE CHILDREN'S WRITING. Nearly all the Japanese children are taught to write with both hands. Generally the better writing is made by the left hand, but by using the right hand they can write much more quickly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CABIN BOY.</head>
        <p>THE CABIN BOY.</p>
        <p>Some years ago a 14" year old boy ran away to sea. As it happened the food on the boat was very bad indeed, and the sailors could not eat it. What were they to do? None of the men were brave enough to men- tier the matter to the captain, who was an old man. They held a secret meeting, and they de- cided that a deputation should be sent to the man. _</p>
        <p>However, none of the sailors were daring enough to voice the complaint, but the cabin boy, who was the youngest person on board, came to the rescue. He suggested that the whole of the crew should go to the captain. The men agreed to this, but when they were in the skipper's presence not a single man had the courage to speak. Once again, therefore, the cabin boy came to the rescue, and addressed the captain so cleverly that the ship's master agreed to the ' men's demands without a struggle. That cabin boy would have made a brilliant naval officer, but he did not stick to the sea. Instead he went in for the 'Taw, in which station of life he has been a great success. That cabin boy was Rufus Isaacs, now Lord Chief Justice.</p>
        <p>THE ELEPHANT, THE BOY, AND THE ARTIST. There was once an artist who promised to paint a picture for one of his customers. The artist lived in London, and when the first bright afternoon came along he went to the Zoo, thinking that he could paint a picture of one of the elephants as it was feeding. He waited for many hours, but was unable to commence his picture, for all the elephants were walking about the whole of the time. On the following day he paid another visit to the Zoo, and this time he took a little boy with him. The boy carried a large bag full of apples, and the artist told him that he had to throw the apples one at a time to one of the elephants, so that the animal would lift up its trunk in the , air and catch the apples in its mouth. The boy began to throw the apples and the artist began to paint the picture. After" this had been going on for half an hour, the boy said, "Half of the apples have already gone. Should I go for some more?" The artist turned round to him and said, "I cannot afford to buy any more, so, instead of</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTII. Useful Information in Popular</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTII. Useful Information in Popular</p>
        <p>Language.</p>
        <p>Fruit as Food. Fruit, fresh or stewed, is always a valuable food, even though it may not contain a large amount of nutrition. The pleasant salts and acids have a gentle medicinal effect upon the stomach, bowels, and liver, which is most con- ducive to health. But it is not necessary to get the expensive fruits. The apple, the king of fruits, is a most wholesome and delicious fruit, and can be taken fresh, baked or stewed. Re- member the old adage, "An apple a day keeps the doctor away." The banana is a more nutri- tious food, and possesses many virtues. There is also the date, a veritable food for the gods, and the fig, equally nourishing and sustaining. These are only a few of the many fruits, home grown or foreign, which are readily obtained at comparatively small expense.</p>
        <p>Baby's Welfare. Cows' milk for calves, mothers' milk for babies. God pity the baby who cannot get mother's milk. Out of every hundred babies in the United States twenty-five die each year. You wouldn't take a crowbar to adjust the delicate mechanism of a watch. Then why use drastic and dangerous drugs co adjust the delicate inter- nal mechanism of a baby? Milk stations are cheaper than hospitals, and will save the lives of more babies. Wily not put the baby befo-r he window in the best room in the house, and, if necessary, put the geraniums in the closed and darkened room? If sunlight and _air are .good</p>
        <p>for flowers, why not good for babies? Throw soothing syrup to the fishes. As Dr. Holmes said, "It may be hard on the fish," but it at least will save the baby from harm.--" Bulletin Indiana State Board Record of Health."</p>
        <p>Croup. This disease of childhood occurs chiefly during the second and third year, in which swelling and partial blocking occur in the throat, caus- ing difficulty in breathing and a suffocating feel- ing. The attack generally comes on at night, following a chill caught during the day or pre- viously. Breathing is hoarse and choking; the voice is thin; there is a spasmodic cough, and the air passes in with A harsh, loud noise. The child is frightened and excited at first, but later gets livid and feeble. Still later pallor, sweat- ing, and great struggle for breath comes on, and may last for an hour or several. After this ~the symptoms abate, and the child falls asleep, but there is the danger that the larynx may be. come blocked, in which case death ensues in a few, minutes. Treatment: Put the child in a hot bath, adding a tablespoonful of mustard. A tent should be made with a blanket, so that he may inhale the steam. When he hi put back to bed a tent should he formed over that, and the steam kettle started. To the water in the kettle add one tablespoonful of tincture of ben- zoin, or some other soothing fluid. An attack is `often checked by giving a teaspoonful of ipecacu- anha wine every ten minutes until vomiting takes place. After an attack the general health should be attended to, and where the attacks are of a spasmodic nature a course of bromide may be advisable, but this must be left in the doctor's hands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>throwing an apple every time, just throw one occasionally, and then our supply will last much longer." Jim, for that was the boy's name, did as he was told. Another hour passed and then he said "All the apples have gone." The big elephant seemed to understand what Jim said, because it walked away to a tank And began to drink it. However, it came back again, as though it wanted to have a last look at the man and the boy, but when it was very near to them it covered them with water, which it had stored in its trunk. _ _ .</p>
        <p>The picture was spoiled and tin., artist and Jim were wet through. This was how the elephant treated them because they (lid not throw an apple every time, and had tried to deceive him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>POTATOES v. WHEAT.</p>
        <p>. _ If we are to break up grass land in the interest of the nation, is not the potato crop the most suitable? Not only does it revel in the turfy humus, in which old pasture is so rich, but it is in itself the finest possible pre- paration for a wheat crop. So far as my ex- perience has gone, the best possible crops have been grown after potatoes. It would seem sensible, then, to devote a certain amount of old pasture to potatoes, which would set at liberty for immediate sowing with wheat an equal acreage yearling seeds which would otherwise have been, intended for potatoes.' There is, however, another point. In view of contingent probabilities, would it not be more wise to increase the potato area itself? for every acre, whether used for direct human consumption or as food for animals, is of far greater value to the nation than an acre of wheat. There seems to be a reasonable pro- bability that we shall get all our bread from abroad, although we may have to pay a little more for it; but if we found our supplies com- ing a little short in twelve months' time, an increased acreage of potatoes, representing an additional million tons, would do much to keep the wolf of hunger from the British door. —" Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>BUYING HORSES FOR THE ARMY.</head>
        <p>BUYING HORSES FOR THE ARMY.</p>
        <p>This is how a North-country farmer describes how his horses were purchased for the Army :</p>
        <p>"Two o' them officer chaps an' another feller— a bossy leeaking little chap, mebbe he was a vet., but Ah can't say yan road or t' other— drove up iv a motter car. They looped out ez if t' Germans was efter 'em, an' began traadin' straight away. Fost t' little hossy feller gat on tiv t' hosses an' galloped 'em fer ther wind (to see they were sound in wind), then yon o' t' officers gat on an' did t' seeam. Then yan on' 'em waffed his hand i' front o' titer ees to see if they were blinnd, or cock-eyed, or owt ; then he thumped .'em ther ribs, and made out as if he was gahin' to fetch 'ern a most awful bat. wiv a stick, to see if he could mak"em grunt. littet all this they assed each yan hoo much they vallyed ther hosses at, an' wivoot onny haggling, or barterin', they said 'We'll give you seea mich,' and began to write papers clot. There's yan paper fer t' bank and yan fer t' stationmaister, but neeabody seems ii reetlins Iznaw -which is which. When they'd gitten t' papers made oot they steead a drink, cicked np then beeaks and gloves, louped inti ther cars. an' were off ti some other spot ti buy scine main It was t' fastest, sharpest boss deal what's ivver been i' this countryside, mark my wods."—" Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WHEAT STRAW WORM.</head>
        <p>THE WHEAT STRAW WORM.</p>
        <p>A new pest has proved somewhat expensive to farmers of Washington, U.S.A., in the wheat straw worm, which is a small, yellow worm about one-quarter of an inch in length, and when present can easily be found by cutting through a straw lengthwise. They are gener- ally found near a joint. The adult is a small, black fly, 'which punctures a tiny hole in the wheat straw near a 'joint and lays the eggs which develop into the small yellow worm, which is common now. The worm lives over winter in the straw, either in the stack or in the field, and then develops into the adult in the following summer. The eggs are laid about the time the wheat begins to head.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>FARM PRODUCE FOR HIS MAJESTY'S</head>
        <p>FARM PRODUCE FOR HIS MAJESTY'S</p>
        <p>FORCES.</p>
        <p>. - Complaints having reached the Board of Agri- culture and Fisheries that the arrangement under which notices were circulated through- out England and Wales, inviting farmers to make returns of the quantity of produce they have to sell at fair market price for the use of His Majesty's Forces, is not working quite sat- isfactorily, the Board are taking steps, in con- sultation with the War Office, to constitute in each county, or group of counties, a Farm Pro- duce County Committee. The committees will consist of leading farmers nominated by the Presidents of the Royal Agricultural Society of England, the Central and Associated Chambers of Agriculture, the Farmers' Club, and the National Farmers' Union. These committees will invite farmers to sub- mit samples and to qr -3 prices for the various classes of farm produce which they wish to sell for the use of His Majesty's Forces, and it is hoped that they will be in a position to take up their duties in some districts in about ten days' time.</p>
        <p>WAR AND THE MILK TRADE. Speaking at a meeting of the Chelford branch of the Cheshire Milk Producers' Association, the Secretary (Mr. James Sadler) said that al- ready there was, and would be for some months, a less demand for milk, particularly in the Manchester and Lancashire district, through the growth of unemployment, though up to the present the demand in London appeared to he normal. On the other hand, the congregation of large numbers of men in camps in various parts of the country created a number of special kinds of markets, where extra milk was re- quired. Taking all circumstances into consid- eration, it must mean a considerably decreased demand for milk. That was going to make it difficult for the 4maller milk distributors, and that aspect of the question had to he consid- ered. The farmer's position for the moment was fairly good, inasmuch as he had been able to gather in an abundant harvest, so that they had plenty of home-grown produce. The quality of it was fair. In some cases there had been damage owing to the unsuitable weather for getting in the hay. That was not the only thing they had to consider. Cows, especially the better quality of cows, were dear, and so were purchased feeding stuffs. When meal was round about a pound a bag, and cotton cake .t 9 les. a ton, it gave one thought before large orders were given to produce milk under present conditions. Suppose there should be a big demand by and by for fodder for the Army, the price would go up, and it might easily be that it would pay the farmer better to sell his fodder at a good price than to produce with it milk to be sent to a doubtful market. The Council of the Association had considered the matter care- fully, and had come to the conclusion that under all the circumstances it might be better for the members of the Association who had not made contracts and who were free, instead of making the usual six to twelve months' con- tracts to keep themselves fairly free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>MICHAELMAS DAISIES. The great charm of , these (says a writer in "Farm and Home") lies in their freedom and grace, the spreading shoots bearing a wealth of bloom. Yet how often do we see the plants bunched up like so many sheaves of corn. When tying up starworts the best way is to allow three or four stakes to each clump, fixing a piece of strong string from one stake to the other loosely, in order that the stems may not fall about and thus become broken. The taller starworts look well when planted among shrubs, which not only answer as a support, but the slender flowering shoots spreading over the green leafy growth produce a fine colour effect.</p>
        <p>A FARMER'S WAY OF STORING CELERY. I Our way of storing celery is quite simple. We take a long, narrow box, about as deep as the plants are tall , , and set it in the cellar out of the way. Then, scattering a couple of inches of fine soft earth at the bottom, we carefully PrY the plants out with a bar, leaving as much soil on the roots as we can, and stand them up in the box close together. The roots ought to be well covered with earth. When the box is full we lay a piece of burlap or some old sacks on top, and the celery keeps well and is good far beyond New Year. "American Farm Journal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LITTLE GIRL'S BRAVE ACT.</head>
        <p>A LITTLE GIRL'S BRAVE ACT.</p>
        <p>Alfred 'Moiled, a naval reservist, stationed at Devonport, fell from a train as it was passing Dawlish on Saturday, and received injuries from, which he is not expected to recover. He fell on the rails of an adjoining track, and a little girl who saw him dragged him to the footway, as another train was due.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>DERFECTLY healthy children are generally happy; but a very slight stomach trouble is often enough to make them ,miserable. That is the time to try a Steedman's Powder. You will find that it acts like magic. Steedman's Powders contain no poison or narcotic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>UNDESIRABLE IMPORTATION.</head>
        <p>UNDESIRABLE IMPORTATION.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Farmer Summoned •at Lymm. MORE POTATO WART.</p>
        <p>A case of considerable interest to farmers and others who grow potatoes was heard at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, when Joseph Stringer, farmer, Bradley Brook, Grap- penhall, was summoned for failing to notify the proper authorities of the presence of wart disease amongst potatoes growing on his land. Defendant pleaded that he did not know, it was necessary to notify. , Inspector Snell, of the Board of Agriculture, said he did not wish the Bench to believe that that case was an aggravated one. The case was discovered the same day as the last case was tried by the magistrates. With regard to the defendant saying that he did not know that he had to notify, he (the Inspector) would 1 point out that large posters had been issued by the county authorities and had been put up all over the county. The disease was so palpable that anyone having it among his potatoes should make inquiries as to what it was, even if he did not know. It was not a disease that was difficult to see. The Board found that when farmers in some parts of the country noticed the disease they sent to them to discover, what it was. The Board wished him to say that they considered the offence a serious one, for farmers in other parts of the country must be protected. Cautious South Africa.</p>
        <p>The Inspector mentioned a case in which, a short time ago, a farmer in the Holmes Chapel district was going to send a large consignment of potatoes to. South Africa. He had them ready packed, but the Board of Agriculture could not give him permission to send them away as the South African Government stipu- lated that potatoes must not be imported into that country if grown within five miles of any- where where the disease was found.</p>
        <p>Jonathan Webb, a sub-inspector of the Board of Agriculture, said that after attending the last Court and giving evidence in a similar case, he went to a field in the occupation of defendant in the , vicinity of Cherry-lane Cottages, Lymm, and there found that wart disease was very prevalent. He saw the de- fendant, and subsequently he received instruc- tions about a farmer named Stringer having sold diseased potatoes to a greengrocer in War- rington. Witness made inquiries and found that the greengrocer had purchased potatoes from the defendant. CAME FROM THE CONTINENT.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. Watkin) : Is this a new disease? _</p>
        <p>Inspector Snell: It first came to the notice of the Board in 1898. If you get amongst some of the older people they will say that it has been in existence for 40 years, but, of course, the Board cannot confirm this. It is supposed to have been first imported to here from the Continent—it has been suggested from Ger- many. (Laughter.) The disease has to spread in the ground before it is effective, so that it is possible for seeds affected with the disease to be set this year and for it to re-appear in two years' time—leaving a clean crop in the mean- while.</p>
        <p>The defendant said that he saw the disease' on the potatoes, but did not know what it was of that it had to be reported: If he had known he certainly would have sent a notifica- tion. He had been farming for about 40 years. Defendant (continuing) said he got his seed potatoes from Scotland and planted them in one field last year and in another this. He saw lumps on the potato tops (wurzels), but did not know they were anything harmful. He had never heard anything about the disease, and he did not remember having seen it before during the whole 40 years he had been working on the land. There were plenty of farmers in the district who did not know that the dis- ease had to be reported. A MAGISTERIAL WARNING.</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman said the Bench were going to take a lenient view of the case and the_de-</p>
        <p>fendant would be fined Is. and costs. That would be the last case with which the magis- trates would deal lightly, and they hoped that</p>
        <p>ihe-Pres;-would let it be known that the dis-</p>
        <p>ease was a very serious one. Farmers not only did harm to themselves, but to their neighbours within five miles, if they failed to report the disease. In future the Bench would certainly deal severely with offenders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>NO LADY DOCTOR.</head>
        <p>NO LADY DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>Guardians Appoint One of the Sterner Sex. - ABSENTEE MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday, Mr. A. J'. Jolley presiding.</p>
        <p>SYMPATHY WITH A SICK MEMBER. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell mentioned that one of the_oldest members of the Board, the Rev. T. R. Matthews, of Earlestown, was lying dan- gerously ill and might never resume his duties. On the proposition of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Flanagan, it, was decided to send an expression of the Board's deep sympathy to the reverend gentleman. FEVER IN THE COTTAGE HOMES. It was reported that at a meeting of the House Visiting Committee it was decided that owing to the outbreak of scarlet fever at the Cottage Homes, any children of school age and not affected by the disease should be sent to•public elementary schools for the time being.</p>
        <p>IMPROVED RELIGIOUS TONE. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said that the Board ought to know, after the question asked at the meeting of the House Committee, why there was such a distinct improvement in the reli- gious tone of the house. The services were now a comfort, pleasure and joy to the in- mates. The Chaplain had stated that it was due; for the most part, to the good influence of the Master and Matron. He thought the Board would all be pleased to hear that, and that they should give honour to whom honour was due.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather said that there was not only an improvement in the religious tone of the house, but the moral tone had also undergone a great change for the better. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>"RISE" FOR AN OFFICIAL</head>
        <p>A "RISE" FOR AN OFFICIAL</p>
        <p>It was decided that the salary of Mr. W. H. Bell, second assistant clerk, be increased by .1;20 per annum. , DUEL OF THE SEXES—MAN WINS! Two candidates—a lady doctor and a gentle- man doctor—appeared before the Board,,in sup- port of their applications for the position of assistant resident medical officer at the Work- house.</p>
        <p>After the doctors had been interviewed, the Chairman spoke in favour of the gentleman, arguing that a man could deal with certain , cases better than a lady. There were many cases with which it was undesirable that a lady should deal. A lady doctor might be all right where there was also a male doctor, but in a Union Hospital in an industrial centre it was not desirable that a lady should be in sole charge. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: What are the quali- fications'of the candidates? The Chairman: They are both about the same. It was decided to appoint Dr. J. R. Jagger, who is the son of a former schoolmaster at Haydock. Mr. McKinley said be thought it was time the Board left the "Lady Doctors Invited" out of their advertisements. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>-- GUARDIANS WHO HAVE GONE AWAY. Mr. Brooks 'referred to Guardians who had removed from the district and said he thought they should resign their seats on the Board if they could not fulfil their duties. In his opinion, after six , months had expired, theY should cease to be members. The Chairman: Are there any cases? The Clerk: There are no members who have gone beyond six months without attending. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: When some people get fully and comfortably seated they system- atically neglect thEir duties. It is not fair to leave all the work and responsibilities to the few. Mr. Brooks: That is so. Miss Broadbent: Hear, hear. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: People who have made promises to the electors and find that they cannot carry out their duties ought to resign. Mr. Brooks: It is not from any personal spirit that lam bringing this forward. The mem- bers to whom I have referred did most valu- able work whilst they *ere here. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
