      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>SPY RAMIFICATION.</head>
        <p>SPY RAMIFICATION.</p>
        <p>World-Wide Gernsan Activity Causes</p>
        <p>Us 'Unrest.</p>
        <p>RIOTS IN LONDON.</p>
        <p>Rapidly accumulating evidence in all parts of the world sixtws that Germany had been pre- paring for war long in advance of events that led up to actual hostilities. There is evidence that in both Belgium and Northern France concrete beds were laid long ago to accommodate their heavy guns when " the day" arrived. Arrange- ments had been made for the coaling of their war ships in Africa and doul)tless elsewhere, and at the present moment spies are scattered over the countries involved, including England, who keep Berlin informed of ari- that is going on. It is natural that in this country we should be on the look-out for signs of intrigues on the part of those Germans and Austrians who live amongst us. In London and Scotland German buildings with concrete foundations have been subjected to attention on the part of the authorities; and in London, German shops have been attacked by the populace—a mode of pro- cedure which in the interests of the good name of the British people is to be deprecated. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>An order has been made for the arrest of enemy aliens of fighting age.</p>
        <p>CONCRETE BUILDING AT WILLESDEN.</p>
        <p>Last week-end the police made a raid upon the premises of C. G. Roder (Limited), music engravers, Victoria-road, Willesden junction, and seized 20 Germans who were in the employ- ment of the firm.</p>
        <p>The premises are within a stone's throw of the Great Western main line and within ten min- utes' walk of Willesden junction, on the London and North-Western main line. They are situ- ated on high ground and give an uninterrupted view as far as the Crystal Palace over a great part of the London area. The bUilding consists of oue storey only and is of remarkable strength. The floor and foundations are of thick concrete, 'while the roof of the front part of the premises is also of concrete from 10in. to 12in. thick and supported by iron girders from 2ft. to 3ft. thick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RAID ON GERMAN FACTORY IN EDINBURGH.</head>
        <p>RAID ON GERMAN FACTORY IN EDINBURGH.</p>
        <p>—— Following upon a visit by the police, the mili- tary authorities on Saturday made an inspection of a certain building in Edinburgh. It was discovered that the floors of the building, which is one Of three storeys, are built of concrete. There is also a large concrete yard, and a flat • roof sufficiently solid to carry heavy guns. If such were mounted there, it is stated, they would command the city of Edinburgh, the Firth of Forth, the Forth Bridge and the East Coast and Waverley routes. Apart from the nature of the building, nothing of a suspicious character, was discovered. A number of aliens were employed on the premises, but they were removed to a detention camp some time ago, when similar steps were taken in Edin- burgh against all alien enemies of a certain age. Nothing of a compromising nature was dis- covered, and the military apparently did not consider it necessary to take any steps.</p>
        <p>CONCRETE PLATFORMS IN USE. rn the vicinity of Achiet, some eleven miles south of Arras, British troops, after having suffered heavily from carefully concealed artil- lery, discovered the enemy, made a brilliant charge, and having carried the position, found that the enemy's guns had been mounted on concrete platforms evidently prepared long beforehand. Expert opinion, based upon care- ful scrutiny, is that the platforms were con- structed at least 18 months ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SPIES SPREAD FALSE NEWS.</head>
        <p>SPIES SPREAD FALSE NEWS.</p>
        <p>While Berlin is spreading false news through- out the world, German spies in 'England fre- quently attempt to spread alarm here by start- ing rumours of British disaster. The latest rumour was that of last Saturday, which stated that more British cruisers had been sunk.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau issued the following state- ment at 1.50 p.m. on Saturday : Mere is no truth whatever in the rumours that any vessels of His Majesty's Navy have been sunk or have otherwise met with disaster other than those about which official announce- ments have already been made. There is room for belief that the rumours had a German source. Similar faked stories of disasters to the British arms are known to have been so inspired with the fatuous idea that the spirit Of the British nation would be seriously affected.</p>
        <p>' LOSS OF PEGASUS DUE TO SPIES. • According to news gleaned from Zanzibar in reference to the disabling of H.M.S. Pegasus, Germans there paid an Indian dhow 200 rupees to proceed to Maria Island, behind which the German cruiser,Tionigsberg was sheltering, and give the enemy news of the whereabouts of the British warship. '</p>
        <p>POSING AS BELGIANS. Forty German spies are said to have been detected among the Belgian refugees who have landed at Dover. Stringent orders have been issued to prevent the landing of refugees at ports on the East Coast. Fear of German espionage has no doubt been the motive of the Home Office in ordering the removal of a num- ber of Belgians housed at Hull.</p>
        <p>MOBILISED SPIES DIRECT GERMAN ARTILLERY.</p>
        <p>An interpreter attached to one of the Head- quarter Staffs writes _ _</p>
        <p>"I have less admiration for the accuracy of the German artillery fire than I should have if it were not obvious that a lot of it is done by spies with. telephones. In some places the gunners never feel for the range, and locate batteries first shot which our aeroplanes can't see; and wherever the Headquarters Staff moves they locate it at once almost. This must be due to spies, of which there are innumerable quan- tities even if one deducts nine-tenths as exag- geration. The French have no means to cope with this, as all the police is at war. I hope this isn't the same in England. The Germans walk about the British Army in French or Belgian uniforms, and, of course, an ordinary Englishman can't know the difference between a German talking French and a Frenchman. To the French, of course, they go in khaki. This is inevitable where there are allied armies, but the state of perfection to which they have brought their spy system beats all belief. They have, of course, a large number of resident local spies, too, the whole thing organised en Moe, a regular 'mobilisation,' in short."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>FACE A MASS OF PIMPLES.</head>
        <p>FACE A MASS OF PIMPLES.</p>
        <p>40, Church-road, Northfield, Birmingham, Eng. —"My trouble began with little white pimples , and when I rubbed them something like water ; came out of them. They used to itch and burn fearfully and I had no sleep for a good while. •They somehow seemed to spread all over my body and face, causing disfigurement on my face which was nothing else but a mass of itching pimpres. My hands were nothing but a mass of sores, and I had to be very parti- cular what I handled or I would have had to cease work. I tried ointments of all kinds and blood Mixtures, which were of no avail. Then I used the Cuticura Soap and Ointment and am pleased to say that they have completely cured my complaint. Now I can undertake all my work without thinking of my bands being sore and I have got a clear skin!' (Signed) Thomas Harrison, Jan./ 22, 1914. Although Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists throughout the world, a Rumple of each with 32-p. Skin Book will be sent post- tfree upon request. Address post-card; P. New- bery &amp; Sons, 27, Charterhoilse-square, London, B.C.</p>
      </div>
