      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>TIIE ROME IN WAR TEI</head>
        <p>TIIE ROME IN WAR TEI</p>
        <p>Suggested Formation of Materna</p>
        <p>Centres.</p>
        <p>Mr. Herbert Samuel on Monday receei. deputation representing 40,000 working wrrei- who urged him in his twofold capacity as,-.`ji;sit' dent of the Local Government Board and to man of the Government Distress Connnit'ire, encourage the formation of maternity en' to to recommend the representative committee9d adopt a scheme for providing nourislinionin medical aid at confinements and nic'l"figg helps, and to make grants for these Ptir' from the Prince of Wales' Fund. 014 Mr. Samuel made a sympathetic repll, said he would send a further communicatilleit all the representative committees drawing toot particular attention to the claims of (41'-et and nursing mothers.</p>
        <p>SOLDIERS' WIVES. War Office Ansi the Separation Allowancli A War Office circular was issued on Sati dealing with the separation allowances allotments to soldiers' wives. It stated</p>
        <p>The War Office wishes it to be geflvt understood that the order making an by the soldier for the support of his tpoir compulsory is not withdrawn, but is te.- arily suspended in certain cases. the inue When the soldier has objected to cont of full allotment, the wife has been invited tt3jje whether she is content to accept what here per band is willing to give, and, if not, to stat means. If she is not content, inquiry will be nia 00V to whether her income without the 'full !bile ment is up to the standard scale. 3feaniot. the Paymaster has orders to pay the full litiSit ment as soon as he hears from her o,ools effect, including anything that may have been abated. I are All persons to whom women may aPPJ ore requested to explain this to them, and to ee,cth the immediate return to the Paymaster 0 form that was sent to the woman to fill that thew, may be no delay in payment</p>
