      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>CHURCHES AND THE WAR.</head>
        <p>CHURCHES AND THE WAR.</p>
        <p>"SCIENTIFICALLY DUPED!" Rev. H. Ogden and Victimised</p>
        <p>German Democracy. At the Golborne-street Baptist Church on OlidaY evening, the Rev. H. Ogden eontinned "la discourse on " General Bernhardi," taking kr he text the words: "He had compassion 4Pon the multitude." After reviewing the Position arrived at the previous Sunday even- the preacher said that in spite of differ- es of opinion then expressed, in converse- „on following the sermon, as to the guilt of "la German democracy, he still adhered to his rerious convictions. There was no other force ill Germany that could make itself felt. Even Church, in this critical period, had proved I'o' singularly futile were the efforts of organ- red Christianity. Self-interest, as expressed Ti Commerce, had been unable to make itself . For once, the Kaiser had turned a deaf 'ea,r to Herr Bailin. Only social democracy, , the weight of its numbers, could influence to Kaiser; and a clear warning, to the German Zutliorities, as to what the attitude of the „I°eisl Democrats would be in case of the de- -atration of war, would have restrained the leaders. , "Prepared to be Used." they were apt to believe that the war had Z°la, e suddenly; but, when armaments were ,ing piled up, Mr. Asquith said that those 110, aments were being prepared to be used, I,lla a Powerful "peace" party in Great Britain never been deceived by the maxim : "To P,l'eserve peace, be prepared for war." The pidosophy of that maxim was now discredited many generations. The democracy of Great ritain, and its leaders—all lovers of peace— 'ad combined to reduce armaments. If the 4e. rman democracy was anxious 'to preserve tna ional peace, why had not all the weight (21 its power been thrown into the scale, in Lavour of an understanding with England? ' Educational Policies. tjlernhardi quite recognised that the policy of e_le Past in regard to elementary schools must continued and perfected. He scoffed at the 1e spent in religious education, and wanted ‘'‘4ll(Ation in the elementary schools to be part 71 a Systematic whole. This perfect scheme, in ernhardi's mind, was the preparation of a ;Didier from the kindergarten. to the barracks. Ilas he was determined that, whatever in- nee the Brotherhood of Social Democracy Ight have upon Germany, the young German ',Pal should, by elementary training, be filled "Itil large ideas of German expansion and RlPNession. One could only believe that the InlPotence of the German democratic party had f)een wrought by the training of their elemen- '4Y schools.</p>
        <p>World-Power or, Downfall? ,liernhardi's remarks on " World-power or nvnfall" revealed a determined opposition to "titish ideas and British power. He believed d th' t England was only awaiting a favourable °l)Dortunity of smashing the German Navy, !lid that the British Government were only 43waiting a favourable pretext and a favourable Opartunity of annihilating German commerce. had that desire been in the mind of the aritish Government, contemporary history re- led the fact that they had had abundant °Dlkortunities of seeking a quarrel with Ger- b7,4113% Either Bernhardi had been wantonly led by the articles of "The Times" and aily Mail," or else he had purposely refused " see the British standpoint, in order that he Zight cultivate animosity amongst the Ger- 4,s4ns• He would not hear of any alliance or 'angement with this country, but said that a sPecific agreement with England is, after all, will-o'-the-wisp which no serious German Otesman will trouble to follow." He belonged u that school of mighty German influence 4lich refused to consider any entente except LI a one as would be humiliating to this conntry.</p>
        <p>Germany's Natural Ally. . Nor was Bernhardi very scrupulous concern- his country's allies. He spoke of Turkey la such words as these: "She is our natural It is emphatically to our interest to keep ill close touch with her." He continued to urge 4Pon the German people and their Government ge necessity of preserving good feeling between li"key and Italy. "The sick man of Europe" a.'l long been the burden of European politics. 14 cruelty 'could not be measured—the mas- It 'king- of Armenia were still fresh in their t,,'—whilst his hatred of Christianity was Fi phatic to doubt. And yet Bernbardi, king, as he said, for a Protestant country, in-5,11'4d Turkey as a "natural ally"! He was 1i7,.."7415, also, to excite political rivalry between and Japan, for, if they were antagonists, 01-}e could retain her possessions in Asia! [OPediency, and yet again expediency," was keynote of his policy. Bernhardi forgot aaL the day might arrive when the yellow would unite; and then, if ever, that day those races would not forget that strove to keep them in antagonisrll, in 'er that she might share their continent. It The Day of Reckoning. was clear that the German democracy had scientifically duped : and, when the day of .I,e'lbuiling came, lie hoped and trusted that the nalties would be paid, not so much by the 'Man people as by those who had connived . ht t heir "instruction "—the penalties of this hrr must fall upon the heads of the Kaiser and advisers!</p>
        <p>1.406</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>I MEMORIAL HYMN. Thos _Por e Who Have Fallen in Battle.. I Woe: "Mansfield," Hymns A. &amp; M. 499.</head>
        <p>I MEMORIAL HYMN. Thos _Por e Who Have Fallen in Battle.. I Woe: "Mansfield," Hymns A. &amp; M. 499.</p>
        <p>Lord of life, the Source and Giver. Souls of all that live are Thine, nallowed be Thy name for ever, Love divine: 3:,,esns rose in Easter gladness X oln the tomb in which He slept, Yet beside a grave in sadness Jesus wept. Lord, amid our bitter sorrow, had and burdened by our loss; Strength and comfort may we borrow From Thy cross. Though our loved ones fall around us, Torn and wounded in the fight. I"°ve's almighty arms surround us Through the night. Saviour, in Thy gracious keeping. Ilea the souls of those who died, Gathered home, in safety sleeping</p>
        <p>_ Near Thy side. _ln Thine arms, 0 Lord, we leavesthem, ,'-otnrades in the battle slain; 14 Thy mercy, Lord, receive them</p>
        <p>Free from stain. isSaV our of the dead and dying. -0,,4d, 0 bid, the conflict cease! 41ae8e our dead are eabxdy lying</p>
        <p>Wrapt in peace. - - of life, that faileth never, 'lre and death alike are Thine; liallowed be Thy name for ever, Love divine. ALFRED CORNEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>TEE DANGEROUS DAWN OF</head>
        <p>TEE DANGEROUS DAWN OF</p>
        <p>WOMANHOOD.</p>
        <p>`ll'k4tfri! Upon the threshold of womanhood often 4tte,-,lnto a decline in spite of all care and g IveaCiuu• Even strong and lively girl becomeist l he iurilt depressed, irritable and listless. re (If womanhood—a crisis in the life of stA,l'3'' girl—and prompt measures should i&gt;e 4(1 tti: t 0 keep tlfe blood pure and rich with the of health. 416 the blood is not in a healthy condition at critical stage, the body becomes . ll- tisiled and grave disorders may result. If o‘lealth of the body is not maintained by I.4llrl'ol' of pure, strong blood, all sorts of evils Fi‘4.t4lw.,". knesses .and these may lead to t,'sved weease. Dr. Williams' .Pink Pills have thousands of young girls from what tleadt have been life-long invalidism of early t el. fi!ssl-3 L:, are a blood-builder of unequalled rich- -4c L'lvogthening weak nerves and producing supply of red, healthy blood which soir„iti beeds to sustain her strength. pr. Lver 211s' Pink Pills have proved their value ‘Nlti-Ild over again to young women whose failing. dealer can supply Dr. Williams' Pink sv• ttillis,,:ur Pale People; if preferred, Dr. OldrTus' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, 1;11, Will send them direct for 2s. 9d. a box ,kllt.°xes,for 13s 9d.). A book entitled "Plain I,l4iktirt"°Viromen." will be sent free to any is. Nil twno writes to Hints Dept., 46,1101b0rn zit s •rtonclon, for a copy; it contains much '6 information. Business as usual.</p>
