      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>1 0 i en' #%*</head>
        <p>1 0 i en' #%*</p>
        <p>SCHOOL PLAYFIELDS.</p>
        <p>Making Provision for Athletic Secondary Scholars.</p>
        <p>WHAT ABOUT THE POORER CHILDREN?</p>
        <p>At their monthly meeting, held on Monday evening, the Warrington Education Committee considered a recommendation by the School Management Committee to the effect thit the offer of Mr. William Powell to let to the com- mittee a piece\ of gtound for the purpose of an athletic field for the use of the scholars at the Secondary School be accepted, the clear rental of the same to be A:6O per annum, on a seven years' agreement. It was further recommended that the landlord of the field at present used by the school be given notice to terminate the tenancy.</p>
        <p>The itiron (Alderman Peacock) said that £6O seemed to him to be rather a large amount for rent for a field alone, and unless the committee were getting something more than just the use of the ground he would have to move that the matter be referred back to the School Manage- ment Committee. He did not think that £6O a year should be spent on a playfield for any school, whether secondary or elementary, unless there was some adequate consideration for so large a sum. There were other schools in the town with larger numbers of scholars that were not able to avail themselves of special fields and other facilities for their games. Mr. JARMAN thought the field which the com- mittee proposed to rent was inconveniently situated. With the Mayor, he thought that £6O a year—tied up for a period of seven years, and thus amounting to a total rental of £420, which was equal to a purchase price of £1,200— was a great deal too mach to pay for a playing field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ONLY DRAWBACK.</head>
        <p>THE ONLY DRAWBACK.</p>
        <p>Cann STEVENSON said , that the rent of the present playing field was £35, but on top of that was the cost of keeping it in order. The field the sub-committee proposed to rent was very much larger than the one at present in use and much more convenient, besides being in a better situation; and the £6O rent included the whole of the expenses incidental to keeping the field in order. Beyond that £6O the com- mittee would not have a penny to pay, and he thought that, in the long run, it would prove more economical than the present playing field.</p>
        <p>Th 6 only drawback was that the field was some little distance away, but it. seemed to be impos- sible to get one nearer. Mr. liesoN emphasised the fact that the pro- posed rental included the laying out and main- tenance of the playing field, whereas those things had to be paid for over and above the rental of the present field. Incidentally he mentioned that the new field would, amongst other amenities, afford room for seven tennis courts.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. UNSWORTH thought the rent proposed ex- traordinarily cheap. Councillor JENKINS said that the field the committee were asked to rent was more con- veniently situated than that at present occu- pied, and the extent of it was very much more than seven acres. Though they called it a playing field, there were really a number of fields, separated in such a manner that the playing of football would not interfere with the hockey matches. If the rental suggested covered all the expense—and the sub-committee were assured that it would—it was cheap, and in return for it they would be getting a nice Piece of ground with a garden round it, situ- ated in a splendid Position, and not two minutes farther away than the present ground. The price was very reasonable, and if the owner acted in good faith the field would prove a great boon to the school in question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CLAIMS OF THE POORER CHILDREN</head>
        <p>THE CLAIMS OF THE POORER CHILDREN</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON pointed out that the present ground cost the committee last year a little over .tlOO, so that the rental of £35 by no means repre- sented what was really being spent. It was, of course, a drawback to have to send the young people from the Secondary School so far out, but it was impossible to get land anywhere nearer. It was an admirable thing for the children of the Secondary School that they should have provided for them such magnificent playing accommodation, but, personally, he .could not help. remembering that they had in the elementary schools children who lived crowded together in the poorer, slummier dis- tricts, who had no opportunities or facilities whatever for decent recreation.</p>
        <p>One hardly dared venture to suggest it at the present moment, but, still, he could not help thinking that if something could be done to provide playing fields within the reach of the children of the elementary schools, especially those in the poorer districts, they would be doing very much to win the respect, admira- tion and gratitude of the rising generation. The MAYOR moved, as an amendment, that the minute be referred back with a view to the Town Council being approached on the subject of laying out suitable land in -Victoria Park. The rental of £6O a year seemed to him to be a large amount to spend on a playing field for the benefit of some two hundred scholars. It would not be any injustice to the scholars of the Secondary School to ask them to continue, for the time being, to use the present playing field, and, in the meantime, the committee might consider the question of going in for the bigger scheme, and see if they could not do something for the children in the elementary schools as well, by setting apart patches and grounds on the pew park extension for which the Council recently paid £4,150. Mr. _FAIRCLOUGH agreed with the Mayor. It seemed to him that if they hired ground of tile kind suggested by the sub-committee they would have to lay sods, ezc., in order to put it into proper condition. The CHAIRMAN replied that the whole of the work involved in laying out the field for the purposes for which it was to be used was in- cluded in the annual rental, under the terms of the agreement. _ _ . _</p>
        <p>Mr: ittal-R-05174G;.: You will not be satisfied with the cricket pitch, to start with You may think so. but I don't.</p>
        <p>_ The CHAIRMAN: I don't think that is quite fair. We do not propose to commit the committee to a single penny of capital expenditure. Mr. FAIRCLOUGA said he believed in having the school playing field on the town's own ground, and therefore he seconded the Mayor's amend- ment.</p>
        <p>*The CHAIRMAN, in answer to questions, said that the field which it was proposed to rent had, under the terms of the suggested agreement, to be prepared to the satisfaction of the Head Master of the Secondary School, the necessary tools and labour being provided by the landlord.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>TERMS OF THE AGREEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE TERMS OF THE AGREEMENT.</p>
        <p>Councillor ARCHER said he was in agreement with the mover of the amendment for the simple reason that the Town Council made absolute provision for the youth of the town to indulge in any athletic exercises they might wish. The CHAIRMAN: Where?</p>
        <p>Councillor ARCHER: In the Victoria Park. The CHAIRMAN: They have done nothing of the sort. They have no power to lay any park out for the benefit and use of the scholars. It is absolutely prevented.</p>
        <p>Council'lo; ARCHER : What is to prevent us finding accommodation for the Secondary School scholars in Victoria Park? The CHAIRMAN: The terms on which the land was purchased. The Chairman then went on to read the draft agreement, which set forth, inter aim, that the Corporation are to have sole use of the ground whenever they require it, amithat it is to contain six tennis courts and two cricket pitches during the summer season, and two hockey pitches and two football pitches during the winter, the owner to undertake to keep the same in perfect playing order throughout the year, to the satisfaction of the Head Master of the Secondary School._</p>
        <p>Councillor ARCHER: That does not commit us to anything as regards Victoria Park! The Town Council of Warrington are making ample provision. , The CHAIRMAN: I tel you the Corporation has no power to restrict any part of that property for the use of any school. It is for the general use of the inhabitants of the town. The MAYOR said that, having heard the terms of the proposed agreement, he withdrew his amendment on the ground that the committee were gettirig more than they ought to get. (Laughter.) Dr. JOSEPH expressed himself as in sympathy with what the Mayor had said concerning the need of a playing ground for the children of the elementary schools. Councillor Ossonris considered the committee were getting an excellent bargain. The minute under discussion was then adpoted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 24, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 24, 1914.</p>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL Ing ALLIES.</p>
        <p>Should "Co-op" and Trade Union Go Hand-in-Hand?</p>
        <p>SPEECH BY KR. J. R. OLYNEB, X.P.</p>
        <p>For the 'purpose of discussing the relation- ship between the Co-operative movement and Trade Unionism, a conference of local trade unionists and co-operators was held in the Co- operative Hall on Saturday aftern,)on. Mr. H. Wardman presided, and Mr. J. R. Clynes, M.P., was the principal speaker. The Chairman explained that the conference had been arranged before the outbreak of the war, and the Education Committee of • the Co-operative Society, who had considered the matter, had come to the conclusion that if the conference was thought necessary when there was no war, it was equally desirable that it should be held in this time of crisis. That it was necessary that trade unionists and co- operators should come together was, to his mind, a proposition that could not be denied. After illustrating how by, combination and joint action the workers had obtained better conditions, the Chairman went on to say that much had been accomplished by co-operators and trade unionists. Looking back on the his- tory of his movement, the trade unionist could see a long list of achievements of which he could rightly and thankfully be proud. He could see that his wages had been levelled up to something like a decent standard of living, and that improvements had been brought about in various other directions. Whilst that had been so from the trade unionists' point of view, so, also, had it been with the co-operators. The progress made by the co-operative movement had been phenomenal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Much Done, But More To Do.</head>
        <p>Much Done, But More To Do.</p>
        <p>But, whilst much had been accomplished, much more remained to be done. There were many things which the trade unionist and the co-operator had in icommon, and gatherings such as that served to educate the masses on the subject, so that greater strides might be made in the future than in the past. (Hear, hear.) It might be true that in the past the co-operative movement had not been all that might be desired from a trade union point of view, but they could not get away from the fact that to-day it stood out pre-eminently as the pioneer of trade union principles. (Ap- plause.) He would give them specific instances of 'how the co-operative movement had helped the workers during the past few years. During the coal strike, whilst' the bakers were knocking loudly at the door of' that movement, they kept the price of bread at so low a level that the poor might have something to eat. During the present war, when prices were rushed up to such an abnormal height, the Co-operative Society kept their prices at the normal level. Whilst there was a great agitation outside for the price of bread to be increased, the com- mittee of the local society, backed up by the manager, refused to be a party to any such thing. If they had been they would have been piling £lOO per week into their coffers at the expense of the people in the town. "If we can solve any of the difficulties between trade unionism and the co-operative movement," concluded the Chairman, "and get trade unionists to take a greater part in this move- ment, we shall find that the two organisittions will do much to lift up the people to a higher plane economically, and so help them to work out their own economic sajvation and go on their way rejoicing." (Atiplause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>MODERN WAR METHODS WOULD SHAME SAVAGES.</head>
        <p>MODERN WAR METHODS WOULD SHAME SAVAGES.</p>
        <p>'Mr. Clyzies referred to "the calamitous events taking place in Europe at the: present time," and said: "We may talk a good deal of the progress and the onward march of man, but, setting aside the rights and wrongs of this war, or the question of who is to blame and who is not, I say that the very manner in which the war is being conducted is proof of how far we are still below that level of civilisation to- wards which we thought we were travelling some years ago. The sinking beneath the water of these murderous mines, the dropping of bombs from aeroplanes and airships, and the resorting to methods of warfare on which the savage of former days" would have turned his back, show how much work lies before us in the way of reform, and that there is yet a need for the greatest exertion to improve the moral and mental side of mankind." (Hear, hear.) _ _</p>
        <p>After showing what had been done for social progress by the trade unionist and co-operative movements, Mr. Clynes said there was a clear and almost fundamental difference between the activities of the two movements towards the existing social oreler and capitalist system. To Improve, Not Destroy, the Social Order.</p>
        <p>Trade unions, in themselves, did not attack the existing social order, nor the existing wage system. They sought for improvement, but did not demolish the system nor crumble it by undermining. Although, on the other hand, the co-operative movement fell far short of the</p>
        <p>ideals of those who established it, yet the gradual displacement of forms of competition and private capitalism showed that that movement was attacking the existing social order and sought its ultimate overthrow. (Hear, hear.) Neither trade unionists nor co-operators should seek selfishly to gain from the other; the gains could be mutual and the motives high—not low, nor a discredit to those nho tried to bring them about. (Hear, hear.) The co-operative movement ought to be the ally of trade unionism in general industrial warfare. There were some who were suspicious of trade union and Labour motives, and who said that they would not enter into politics. It was no use saying that. (Hear, hear.) Judge-made laws had compelled the trade unions to seek redress in Parliament. When talking of the Labour party they were talking of trade unions, and, when anyone said they would have noth- ing to do with politics, it meant that they could scarcely ever hope to have anything to do with trade unions at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Political Alliance Suggested.</head>
        <p>Political Alliance Suggested.</p>
        <p>There ought to be a practical alliance between trade unionists and co-operators, and, if neces- sary, in matters of a political character also. If co-operators liked to stay out of politics they would not find the private traders following their example. The latter were represented in the House of Commons, and on every other body in the country which controlled national affairs. Surely the co-operators did not desire the private traders to have more power than they had, and by joining with the trade union movement they would be better able to achieve their objects. (Applause.) Mr. H. Sinclair opened the discussion, and said that two-thirds of the trade unionists in Warrington were members of the Co-operative Society. A great deal could be done to the mutual advantage of each organisation by means of a closer alliance between them. (Ap- plause.) Mr. N. Cook said that he had been a member of the Co-operative Society for 35 years, and he never knew that the trade unionists and co- operatorq weoe amalgamated to any extent. The co-operative movement had made rapid strides, independent of trade unionism, b'ut, if by joining together, there would be greater strength, it would be all very well. However, he failed to see how a complete amalgamation of the two bodies could be brought about, be- cause the profits of the C.W.S. were so much more than those of the trade_ union.</p>
        <p>—.- Mr. Clynes pointed out that he had only sug- gested that arrangements should be made for the alliance of the two bodies. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Dukes did not think that the general body of the people in either movement had got hold of the general principles of either body. They should get down to the real principles of the proposal. Trade unionists in Warrington had received valuable assistance from, the C.W.S. in recent \fights.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MORE EDUCATION WANTED.</head>
        <p>MORE EDUCATION WANTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Trow said that what was wanted ,was education. Many people did not trouble to consider under what condition their goods were made. _</p>
        <p>A lady member of the Co-operative Guild agreed with the last speaker, and said that in their Guild they educated the women as to the harm done by purchasing " sweated " goods. Mr. W. Wright said it would be better if there were a little more faith and less suspicion in the two organisations. _</p>
        <p>Mr. E. England said the fusion would be beneficial. Mr. Helsby believed in workers working for workers. Mr. Clynes said that an entente of a slender but well-defined character was what he desired. On the proposition of Mr. Jones, seconded by Mr. Dukes. the following resolution was passed: "That this meeting considers it the duty of the State to make adequate pfovision for the wives and dependents of soldiers and sailors fighting on our behalf, and we consider the following to be the minimum of any sum to be decided upon as adequate provision: £1 per week for the widow of any soldier or sailor killed; £1 per week for the mother or depend- ent of any soldier or sailor killed; .21 per week for the wife of every soldier or sailor engaged in fighting; and otl per week for every soldier or sailor maimed in the fighting; the money to come out of super-tar...l'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BELGIAN REFUGEES' WEDDING IN</head>
        <p>BELGIAN REFUGEES' WEDDING IN</p>
        <p>LONDON.</p>
        <p>Two Belgian refugees were married at Dul- wich on Saturday. Before the war they lived in Brussels, and the marriage, which was shortly to have taken place there, was prevented by the entry of the German soldiery. The man, who bears the very unßelgian name of Jones, is an Englishman, who has lived in Belgium prac- tically the whole of his life. His wife is a Bel- gian, and as she was unable to reach her par- ents, who had been driven from their home through the German invasion, she escape 4 with her lover from Brussels after the enemy's occu- pation of the Belgian capital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>TERRITORIALS AND BERNHARD!.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS AND BERNHARD!.</p>
        <p>Adult School Lecture in Novel Conditions.</p>
        <p>GERMAN DELUSIONS.</p>
        <p>There are about a dozen Adult School mem- bers with the "Warringtons" in camp, but the character of their military duties, the un- certainty of their movements, and the fact that they are billeted in different parts of the town have made it difficult to have regular school meetings. Mr. Harold Gray, the presi- dent of the Bewsey School, being in the neigh- bourhood on business, spent the week-end, October loth-llth, with the "boys," and arranged a most successful school. Sixteen were present. The meeting was held in a bakery, which had for the occasion been fitted up with chairs and tables. No Adult School would be complete without the inevitable lee- turette. This was given by one of the local ministers, the Rev. Walter Gerrard, who spoke on "The Dogmas and Delusions of Bernhardi." The speaker said that the first dogma laid down by Bernhardi was that attitude of mutual sus- picion is necessary between different nations. The standard of morality by which other nations are judged is so low that those who think such evil of their neighbours cannot be very good themselves. Evil-doers are always evil-dreaders. "A pacific agreement with England," Bernhardi says, "is, after all, a will-o'-the-wisp, which no serious German statesman would trouble to follow." Another sample of this immoral cynicism: "The political rivalry between the two nations of yellow race—China and Japan—must be kept alive," the reason being that if the two joined hands Germany might find it difficult to main- tain her position. Such teaching says that in order to preserve power and prestige in national life it is necessary not only to har- bour it in one's own national life, but also to foster and foment in other nations the lowest sort of suspicion. The same policy has been pursued in the Balkans, where State has been set against State by underground methods of diplomacy. _ _ _</p>
        <p>. - The second delusion is that a benevolent love of humanity is incompatible with patriotism or love of country and race. Bernhardi says again: "The love which a man showed to an- other country as such would imply a want of love for his own countrymen." A theory such as this, run out and traced to its consequences, gives the following as a result applied to Britain:—</p>
        <p>"There is another danger which concerns England . . . and directly threatens her vitality. This is due to the Nationalist move- ment in India and Egypt, to the growing power of Islam, to the agitation for independence in the great Colonies, as well as to the supremacy of the Low-German element in South Africa." No comment is needed on this; the Colonies have answered it. _ _</p>
        <p>The third delusion is that "Might is Right." "The end-all and be-all of the State is power, and he who is not man enough to look this truth in the face should not meddle with politics." Again: "Among all political sins, the sin of feebleness is the most contemptible, it is the political sin against the Holy Ghost." Again: "Might is the supreme right, and the dispute as to what is right is decided by the arbitrament of war. War gives a %biologically just decision, since its decisions rest on the very nature of things." This doctrine is applied to other nations thus—" France must be so completely crushed that she can never again come across our path." Again: "The newer idea [that a weak nation has the same right to live as a powerful and vigorous nation] represents a presumptuous encroachment on the natural laws of development." The fourth fallacy is that Christianity sanc- tions war as one of the highest and noblest factors in the progress of mankind. Bernhardi seeks in his book to occupy a strong position in its assertion that war is a most ennobling thing in the life of nations. The address, of which the above is the mere outline, was listened to with close attention by the soldiers, and gave them a clear ,idea of the forces and philosophy which have caused the war. After school the men were entertained to a splendid tea by Mr. Allan, the owner of the ,building in, whithi they met. When the inner man Was satisfied the evening was Spent in a real homely Adult School "free-and-easy." The men are very enthusiastic and apprecia- tive of the hearty hospitality of the people among whom they are temporarily living, and glowing accounts have been sent home of many kindnesses received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BELGIAN REFUGEES RESCUED AT SEA.</head>
        <p>BELGIAN REFUGEES RESCUED AT SEA.</p>
        <p>iQUERABI )u'vo lost evel NS: "Not in:</p>
        <p>The Royal National Lifeboat Institution an- nounce that two Belgian fishing boats with 35 Belgian refugees and the crews of ten men aboard got on the Newcombe Sands, off Lowes- toft. The Lowestoft lifeboat was launched and rescued the 35 refugees. The two fishing vessels were towed off by a tug, and the members of the crews of the vessels remained aboard when the lifeboat returned to Lowestoft to assist the crew of the tug. The sea was rough at the time.</p>
        <p>'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WAR AND ECONOMICS.</head>
        <p>WAR AND ECONOMICS.</p>
        <p>Philomaths and Capturing the</p>
        <p>, - Enemy's Trade.</p>
        <p>GERMANY'S CREDIT GOING.</p>
        <p>Some very interesting remarks with regard to the cry "Capture German trade" were made at a meeting of the Warrington Philomathic Society, held in the Church House, Bridge- street, on Wednesday evening, when Mr. J. Walsh preAsided, and a discussion on " War and Economics" was initiated by Mr. A. H. Bath. bone.</p>
        <p>The envious, at, the outset, referred to the regret the members felt at the indisposition of the President, Mr. Norman Nield. They all hoped that his recovery would be rapid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>GREAT WARS AND TRADE.</head>
        <p>GREAT WARS AND TRADE.</p>
        <p>Mr. RATHBONE, in opening the discussion, said that not the least complex of the questions aris- ing out .of the present war was that of economics. There was the very prevalent idea that the war would be followed by a boom in trade. Indeed, he was not sure that it was expressed so much as an idea or a consummation to be desired, but something which would surely happen in the due course of events. Time would, of course, give them the true answer, but, so far as the economic position was concerned, they could endeavour to anticipate time, and be prepared to some extent for future eventualities.</p>
        <p>_ Fortunately, or unfortunately, they had little to guide them in the way of past experi- ence, for the only things which could possibly be used as comparisons—and very poor ones at that—were the Franco-German war and the Napoleonic wars of the early part of the 19th century. His remarks as to what he conceived would be the result of the present war would be made with a full consciousness of the fact that, under all the circumstances, trade was now not what might be called bad. Let them ex- amine the idea of a boom. There was always a more or less normal ebb and flow in the matter of demand, largely affected amongst other things, by the abundance, or otherwise, of the world's harvests operating on the price of food- stuffs, and the demand was constantly being overtaken by the supply. War Likened to a Bad Harvest.</p>
        <p>The point the speaker:- NC:i-s-he-clic;--e-n;phasise was that the power to demand, using the word in its commercial sense, was limited by certain eventualities, and that it could only increase when the ability to purchase increased. To his mind, the war would have precisely the same effect as a very bad harvest, except that it would operate in a rather different way. He might liken it to a devastating pest, sweeping over the cornfields of the earth and destroying the crops. In one case the actual means of human sustenance were destroyed, whilst in the other the means of purchasing that sustenance were wrecked.</p>
        <p>_ It was not enough to say that this, that and the other would be wanted, because they had been destroyed. They all wanted things they could not have, simply because they could not afford to buy them, so they had consequently to do without them. Otherwise, they might go oil creating booms ad lib. The fact that Germany would be called upon to pay a heavy indemnity did not substantially alter the position he (the speaker) toot up. They might say that loans would be floated, but then the question of credit arose, and credit depended on security. The credit of all the countries concerned must suffer more or less, and the consequence would be that higher rates of interest would be charged. Again, from whom would the loans be obtained?</p>
        <p>Even at this early stage America had refused to have anything to do with the loan which the Germans tried to raise a short time ago. The question of loans had a further aspect. It had been the policy of this country to finance the Colonies and other partially developed countries, a policy which had been very much to their advantage, both industrially and politically. Supposing that Great Britain was able, to some extent, to negotiate loans when the struggle was over, they could not ignore their Allies in the matter: with the result that the Colonies and other partly developed countries would be de- prived of a large portion of the means which were necessary from time to time to assist in the continued development of their very great re- sources. Destruction in Place of Productlnn—____ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Men had been taken from the mills of produc- tion, and were being employed by the machinery of destruction, and in that they had to be sus- tained by those who remained to produce. Re- move the later and everything must collapse. Restore the former and the mills continued to grind. But after the destruction there must be a time for recuperation. The huge amount of money that was being spent on the war was being blown from the cannon's mouth on their behalf. That could not result in increased ' trade. It must be paid for, and to that extent would reduce their resources for trade. It could not improve trade, except in a certain channel, for the time being, any more than the smashing of windows could improve trade. He mentioned windows, because when one happened to get broken, the amateur philosopher would say that it was good for trade. The logic of that was to go and smash every window in the town, as that would be still better for trade. So far as Warrington was concerned, he thought he was right in stating that'trade was not bad, but that could not be taken as apply- ing to all towns, or even all industries, formally were feeling the pinch of the war, and it was only in those industries which were supplying war material that business continued as usual. The conflict was so immense that enormous quantities of all kinds were necessary to keep it going, and the production of those supplies was creating a somewhat artificial activity in some branches of industry; an activity to which there would be a violent reaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>hnthth,................................"............______ % WARRINGTON MAN . . Kept Well, 8 Years. .</head>
        <p>hnthth,................................"............______ % WARRINGTON MAN . . Kept Well, 8 Years. .</p>
        <p>On September 11th, 1905, Mr. T. Rigby, of 121, Church-street, near the Training Sollege for Girls, Warrington, said: "Through hav- ing to work out in all weathers I became subject to kidney trouble. For several year I was a sufferer from acute pains in my back, which were sometimes so bad that I could scarcely get about I was.almost afraid to stoop, for it was a diffi- cult matter to straighten myself up again.</p>
        <p>"For three years I took differ- ent medicines, but with no good re-</p>
        <p>-s filter</p>
        <p>The Dis-</p>
        <p>appearing Backache.</p>
        <p>Backache Kidney toning and strengthening the kidneys assist them to filter out the blood poisons which cause:</p>
        <p>Backache Dropsy Rheumatism</p>
        <p>Bladder T. Impure Uric Acid Diseases</p>
        <p>Nervo Lu</p>
        <p>Sciatica Gravel</p>
        <p>suits. It was different, however, when I started with Doan's Back- ache Kidney Pills; they did me good from the first, and brought me back to health. lam thankful for the benefit I derived from Doan's Pills,and shall do all lean to make them wider known.' (Signed) "T. RIGBY."</p>
        <p>On November 21st, 1913—ovEa MORT YEARS LATER—Mr. Rigby said: "I "Thaü still keep very well indeed. I have You Dona' " often recommended Doan's Pills. They have done me lasting 'good." WHY 9 Because the kidne the blood and</p>
        <p>4AOS4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>DOAN'S Pat</head>
        <p>DOAN'S Pat</p>
        <p>Prior 2'Q, six boxes 139, of all dealers or Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Welts wed, Oxford Street, Lortd on, W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>3 B.'</p>
        <p>They had all heard of the effort to capture German trade, and, like himself, had given it their hearty blessing. The thought of it acted as a balm to the soul, but it would have to be undertaken in a different spirit to that which was preached in some of the papers. At the moment, it was not a very difficult matter, for a certain amount of trade had been automatically diverted to this country, and many fresh in- quiries were being received through the export houses which had previously been sent to Ger- many, but so far he did not think the results had been very encouraging. It was, perhaps, unfortunate that the movement had to wait for the war to act as the driving force, as he was afraid that it tended to make that driving force somewhat vindictive, and when people were vin- dictive, reason took a back place. Shall We Buy Gorman Goods? One often heard the remark that certain in- dividuals would never again buy German goods of any kind, but he ventured to say that those very people would be buying German goods not many months after the war was over, whenever it paid them to do so. Nobody bought a German article because it was made in Grrmany, but because it suited the pocket, or was of better value than the home-made article. Sentiment would have no place when industry resumed its normal course. It could not be supposed that German manufacturers would remain quiescent, while the British captured the trade. Let Ger- many send this country all she could when the war was over, and, so far as raw material was concerned, as cheaply as the British could make her.</p>
        <p>In the Board of Trade reports, which had been issued for the purpose of assisting the manufac- turers in the proposed capture of German trade, the outstanding feature of almost all the con- sular reports issued from all over the world was the continued references to the thoroughness and persistency of the German methods. This must be met with an equal, or better, system, coupled with untiring research and ceaseless energy at home, in order that the best and most economical methods of production might be dis- covered.</p>
        <p>In that way it might be possible to capture German trade, and if the agitation was only in- strumental in awakening the manufacturers to the fact, it would hare done much to establish a period of prosperity at the earliest possible date. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>OUR SOUND ECONOMIC BASIS.</head>
        <p>OUR SOUND ECONOMIC BASIS.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. G. BURGESS said that Mr. Rathbone's paper gave a very fair view of a very complex question. Had it not been for the foresight of the Government the country might have re- ceived a shock at the very basis of the economic system, from which it would have taken them many years to recover. Despite the fact that they were spending the huge sum of .Z 5,000,000 a wekk, their credit was still very sound. If a country's financial position was not sound before a war, that country was damned from the start. Therein lay overwhelming advantages to the British, and therein was the reason why the German nation had commenced a losing battle. There was the question of the Stock Exchange. Many people were saying that the Stock Ex- changes should be re-opened, but he did not agree, for, if they were, there would be a whole- sale decline in value. Many people would be wanting to sell, whilst very few would be want- ing to buy. WILL THE WAR BE PROFITABLE? _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. F. CHADWICK thought that the';;;.with Germany would not be profitable, because the expense would be so great that the return would never equal it. The Germans had obtained their trade because they deserved it. The whole organisation, from the Government downwards, had been bound up in trade.</p>
        <p>Mr. BARLOW said there were enormous quanti- ties of products from Germany sold in this coun- try which could be made and sold in England, provided the manufacturers did not run up against the trade union rate of wages. Mr. 11. SHERBURN said that one of the good features of the war was that it was inducing the nation to foster the production of those things which were absolutely necessary to human life, whilst the trades which were concerned with what were really luxuries were suffering. Per- haps it might be as well that that tightening in the direction of luxuries had taken place. The Germans had a thorough knowledge of their trades and had spent enormous sums of money in research work. GERMANY'S CHANCES BETTER IN PEACE THAN WAR. , Mr. i. WALSH said that no man could possibly _</p>
        <p>doubt that Germany had been far more -success- ful when her policy was on peace lines than she could hope to be after this war. Commerce was now international, and, therefore, any serious disturbance or sacrifice of men and material was bound to hit all civilised nations. It was a horrible thing to think that at the present time the best customers we fighting one another. Some of the manufacturers who were laying themselves open to capture German trade would find to their sorrow at the termination of the war that the Germans' enforced cheapness would be disastrous to them. He did not think they were going to take Germany's cheap lines away from them. The United States would benefit from the war—so long as it kept out of it. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>FUTURE.</head>
        <p>THE FUTURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. RATHBONE, in winding up the discussion, said he did not agree that the German scientific man was any better than the English scientist. (Hear, hear.) There were more scientists in Germany, and it was only in the bulk that the German scientists held the advantage over the British. It might be that for a time after the war was over Germany would be crippled in her competition with this and other countries, but, as time went on, he felt quite sure that simply from her own natural wealth she would once again come forward and take a place equal to that which she held before the war. Others who joined in the discussion were the Rev. E. Downham and Messrs. C. S. Woods, L. Robinson and A.-I. Newton.</p>
      </div>
