      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ALL GOES NVELL.</head>
        <p>ALL GOES NVELL.</p>
        <p>The Germans have met with a rebuff before Warsaw similar in all respects to the rebuff they received before Paris. They rushed upon the capital of Poland till they got within half-a- day's march of the place—and then their left wing was shattered. As their right wing is largely composed of Austrians, it almost goes without say- ing that the centre was left very much in the air. The situation, therefore, is that our antagonists have failed on both sides, and they must continue to endeavour to hold two formidable enemies in two widely separated areas. If, at a time they were fully prepared for striking, and the Allies were not prepared to meet them, they could not carry out the first stages of their plan, what can they hope for now with their armies battered, their resources dwindling, and the Allies improving in strength with every passing day? They have not even the hope of being able to make a diversion by maltreat- ing a Belgian village. No likely village remains. Belgium is laid waste ; but wasted as she is she will still be the most potent of all the enemies of Germany and German ambition. , The whole world stands aghast in the presence of such barbarity as is revealed by the treatment of Belgium, and against civilised opinion Germany, in any case, could not stand.</p>
        <p>TO CAPTURE GERMAN TRADE. A discussion which took place at this week's meeting of the Philomathic Society regarding war and economics resolved itself, to some extent, into a speculation as to whether we shall be able to capture and to hold trade that Germany must lose, at any rate for the period of the war. It all depends. It is argued by some that we shall not be able to retain the trade we take because the same superiority in method and organisation, and, if you will, intrigue, that has served Ger- many so well in the past Will again assert itself, and we shall lose what we gain. Of course, if we sit still we shall lose the trade, and we shall deserve to lose it, but if we benefit by our present advantages and experience there is no need to fear the result. We shall be in possession ; Germany will be without credit, material and moral. "Scraps of paper," as such, are but poor instruments of commerce, as Germany will learn if we do but play our cards correctly. The masters must see to that. The men have their part to play, as is explained in a manifesto —which Mr. Joseph Elwell has sent us—issued by the Associated Iron and Steel Workers of Great Britain, by the General Secretary. We must work and win as our soldiers will fight and win, and there will be no retracing of steps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>THANKS TO THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THANKS TO THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>At the last meeting of the Council for the present municipal year, tO be held on Tuesday night, the members will be asked, on the motion of Alder- man Bolton, to offer its, best thanks to Alderman P. Peacock for the manner in which he has discharged the duties of his office and for the unvarying courtesy and impartiality displayed by him on all occasions. We venture to say that it will be in no formal , manner that this customary thanks will be tendered. Alderman Peacock has proved the ideal Mayor we all thought he would prove, and it is a source of satisfaction, in view of the hard work that lies ahead so long as this war lasts, to know that Alderman Peacock has consented to take upon his shoulders the burden appertaining to the office of Chief Magistrate for another year. During the past twelve months hiS Worship has attended no fewer than 192 public engagements. Of these, 23 were of a religious character, 55 were social events, 19 were sporting items, and 23 were meetings and events Which were connected with the Friendly Society world. The remain- ing 72 engagements were connected with miscellaneous happenings. At 30 of the meetings he attended he took the chair.</p>
        <p>IN RELIGION. We are fond of Co Onigtptulating our- selves upon the grit never revealed unityne religious British. That grit aterlanlistic in a nobler way than by tsm as we know it has element of the COMm the presence of the greatest upheaval the world, ever seen, the Churches stand Undis- mayed. They may be sorrowful a the fact that a nation with tremendous possibilities for doing good work in the regeneration of mankind should have deliberately set up the belief that while Germans individually may suc- cour one another collectively they are to renounce their interest in, and even to oppress others who live across a given boundary. They may regard with horror the abominations with which a, Godless' " culture " has afflicted humanity. But they have an unbounded faith in *a final triumph Nh ic h the present experiences may haw rather rather than retard. We fancy</p>
        <p>that in a religious, us well as in a military sense, the modern German school will receive a staggering blow that will leave it much less powerful for evil. _</p>
        <p>The British and Foreign Bible Society, bont in the dark days of our struggle with Napoleon, just over a hundred years ago, is stretching out a helping hand in these days of trouble. Sources of income may suffer, but the work must not, and they propose to give copies of the Scriptures not only to our own siek and wounded, prisoners of war, civilian refugees, contingents from our Dominions, the men of France, Russia and Belgium, but, if practicable, to the men of Germany and Austria. The Bible knows no limitations, nor do its carriers know of boundaries:</p>
        <p>_ Locally, we are to have the annual meeting of the .branch of the society next Thursday. So far from yielding to the inclination to _miss the engage- ment for this year the officials are mak- ing a special effort, .and have succeeded in getting the assistance of the Rev. J. H. Ritson, one of the secretaries of the society, who has the double advan- tage of being a much-travelled man and a splendid speaker. Dr. Joseph will occupy the chair.</p>
        <p>PAYING TIIE RATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodcock had a highly satisfac- tory report to make to the Finance Committee as to the collection of the rates for the half-year ended Septem- ber 30th, and doubtless the Council will have some nice things to say of ' his department on Tuesday night. Two-thirds of the period, war, in its initial and most unsettling phases, was raging ; yet there was little, if any, difference shown in the usual prompt and full meeting of all liabilities by the ratepayers. On our four rates £68,108 7s. Id. was collected, composi- tion allowed being £4,412 17s. sd. Only £36 Os. lld, was excused by the magistrates on the score of poverty, and only £l5 17s. lid. figures as loss in collection. Arrears carried forward amounting to £325 14s. 6d. are some- what larger than last half-year, but over £lOO has since been paid, and another £lOO is the subject of an appeal. These are figures which not only do credit to the Collector's Office and the ratepayers, but speak elo- quently as to the soundness of our position financially and industrially. It is something to know that at a time when we have a great war on hand the increment in the product of the rates this half-year over last half-year is £1,364 18s. sd.</p>
        <p>GRIT IN -0f c(</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>MIDSHIPMAN DICKSON</head>
        <p>MIDSHIPMAN DICKSON</p>
        <p>M Goes Down With His Ship, thd Hawke.</p>
        <p>PARENTS' BRAVE APPEAL TO PARENTS.</p>
        <p>The lists of the few who were saved from the Hawke last week did not contain the name of Naval Cadet D. A. Dickson, the youngest son of Dr. J. Dunbar Dickson, of Marlow, brother of Mrs. John Crosfield and the racy speaker who distinguished himself so much at the opening of the John Crosfield Memorial Hall in July.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>PROMISING BOY.</head>
        <p>A PROMISING BOY.</p>
        <p>The South Bucks "Free Press" says ,that the town looked forward with confidence to a career of distinction for David Dickson, one of the most promising boys in Marlow. To say that the lad was popular scarcely conveys his merits. He was liked by everyone. As a bright, upright, and fearless young Briton. good at work and play alike, he will be long' remembered by many friends and by his col- leagues at school and college, and in the Navy. The sympathy of the whole of the residents is exteaded to Dr. and Mrs. Dickson and their other sons in this great losS. The lad is one of the many who could ill be spared who have had to sacrifice life for the honour and defence of the country. Midshipman Dickson was 17 years of age, and, like all the four brothers, he commenced his education at Borlase School. From Borlase he went to the Royal Naval College at Osborne, where he was exceedingly popular with his fel- lows. From Osborne he 'went to the Royal Naval College at Dartmouth, and finished up as one of the two chief cadet-captains of the college. Moreover, in April last he was awarded His Majesty the King's gold medal for "the cadet who during the period of train- ing exhibited the most gentlemanlike bearing and the best influence amongst the cadets." Last Tune he joined H.M.S. Cumberland, and on the outbreak of the war was transferred to the ill-fated Hawke. The last letter received by his father was full of cheery hope. He referred to the fact that he was fit and well, and only wanted a full night's sleep, a bath with a good depth of water, and a game of golf. We have referred to the fact that he was good at play as well as work. This may be judged by hi§ record at Dartmouth, where he won the Rushworth Challenge Seal for boxing, taking both light and middleweight champion- ships. He also won the Senior Sculls and the half-mile flat race. When at home at Marlow he ,was always ready for participation in any manly sport. He played hockey for the Marlow H.C., and was a member of the Marlow R.C., always being to the fore at the club aquatic sports. He was also a fine swimmer, and played a useful game at golf and lawn tennis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ROYAL SYMPATHY.</head>
        <p>ROYAL SYMPATHY.</p>
        <p>Dr. Dickson received the following telegram from Sandringham on Saturday morning The King and Queen deeply regret the loss you and the Navy have suffered by the death of your son in the service of his country. - Their Majesties truly sympathise with you in your sorrow. Dr. and Mrs. Dickson replied : Please express to our King and Queen our heartfelt thanks for their sympathy, and our pride that our brave boy died for his King and country. JOHN DUNBAR DICKSON. EMILY MARGARET DICKSON.</p>
        <p>OR. DICKSON PREPARED TO GIVE MORE. Dr. Dickson wrote the following letter to the Editor of the South Bucks "Free Press"</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—l would crave a small space in your columns to tender heartfelt thanks, on behalf of my wife and myself, to all those who by word, letter, or a simple handshake have testified their sympathy with us in our present sorrow.</p>
        <p>May I add that the loss of our brave boy has roused in us no feelings of a desire for revenge for him personally, .but we and our other sons are Prepared to give still more, and by thought, "rd, and deed to do all in our power to help to wipe this cursed German militarism, with its ruthless atrocities, off the face of the earth once for all. If perchance any parents who read this are holding back their sons, we appeal to them over our son's watery grave for their King and country's honour, and for the children yet unborn, not to hesitate to send their boys for- ward into the battle line.—l am, dear sir, faithfully yours,</p>
        <p>-JOHN DUNBAR DICKSON. The Gables,. Marlow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
