      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>LABOUR COUNCIL AND TIIE</head>
        <p>LABOUR COUNCIL AND TIIE</p>
        <p>TRUCE.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—The Labour Council has decided to fight, and I would not take interest, hut my brothers must pay the price, &amp;c. These are not times when the owners of these organisations have sur- plus moneys. Their servants need to be frugal. There is no need for me to talk about spending other people's money, as past experience teaches that the difficulty is to get it to spend. Yet I must ask the Labour Council to take a common- sense view of the position to see if the game is worth playing. I submit the following as food for reflection :- 1. This split in their own house. 2. That the greater portion of their most likely supporters are away from home on the business of His Majesty the King, God bless him. 3. That they will lose caste from a patriotic standpoint, and the set that teaches the wrong book are sure to see that the mugs swallow it. I hold no brief. Yet I do think the Trades Council had an obligation to the Chief Magis- trate, their only friend in 1914, who, I feel sure, would be pleased to see no fight, and delighted if the whole of the parties involved were to send what moneys they have to spare for the Belgian Belief Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>ROAD-MENDER.</head>
        <p>THE ROAD-MENDER.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am sorry to have to reply to your correspondent, " Vincit Amor Patrice," because there are a few people really desirous of drop- ping political controversy. There are others professing this desire, who, like " Vincit Amor Patrice," only wish the other 'people to drop it. In his letter he tries to shoulder the blame on to the Socialists for the municipal contests, by informing your readers that the Liberal and Tory parties have called a truce, which we refused to agree to. First of all, it is the Labour Council who are responsible for the contests, and I merely point this out so that they may receive the credit for their project. No one to-day is frightened of the term Social- ist; the pervertion is unnecessary. What does not seem clear to your correspon- dent is that we are a "party," and ought to have been considered before any agreement was decided upon. On the contrary, we were told of the agreement after arrangements had been made, and expected to accept the terms,, the ternis being that we should be left out. We are asked why have we chosen this year for the contests? Let me again repeat our ' contests were decided upon before the war was thought about, and any attempt to associate our selection of time with the war is unworthy and discreditable of any man. They :who take up their pen should be sure of their facts. When, however, "Vincit Amor Patrice" makes reference to the victories of Liberals and Tories at the front he only dis- plays the despicable depth to which political perverters will sink whilst hiding their face behind a mask of so-called patriotism. What of the thousands of trade unionists? Have they taken no part in the victories won? Our contests were decided with the approval of many who to-day are facing the brunt of battle. Suppose we commence to tell the public of the hitter complaints of the wives of these men, threatened with distraints by landlords and rent collectors who are not of our party? I am sorry to have to make reference to these matters. After all, they have nothing to do with the question at issue. Our civic life has to be carried on during the war as in times of peace. No party has done more than their duty; no one should seek political prestige for what they have done. Let it be definitely understood that any fitr- ther attempts to discredit the Labour move- ment or to introduce the war topic into the municipal controversy, and we will give to the public what at the moment is better left unsaid.</p>
        <p>There is not the slightest reason for saying that the municipal elections will be costly to the town. The rates so far have not been touched for war expenses, nor are they likely to be. "Vincit Amor PatriEe" has no grounds for saying that the cost of the elections will interfere with any financial aspect of the war, nor that the Red Cross work is carried on by the Liberal and Tory parties as such. If there was the slightest truth in this it would be the duty of your Town Council to take it immediately out of their hands so as to ensure impartial treatment. I am going to ask "Vincit Amor Patrise" to drop this controversy, or to send me a copy of his letter so that my reply may appear in the next issue of your paper along with his next letter. I think this is fair and equitable and will prevent either party gaining an advantage just prior to the poll. I have no desire for further controversy, but if our right to contests is challenged, I am prepared to meet a representative person of either party and thrash the matter out upon any public platform.—Yours, ie., CIIAS. DUKES.</p>
        <p>CYCLISTS FOR THE FRONT. New War Office ,Requirement. The War Office requires the services of a cer- tain number of cyclists—apart from motor- cyclists—for possible service abroad. A com- munication has been received by the National Cyclists' Union to the effect that twenty, cyclists are required to act as despatch bearers to the headquarters of the Second Mounted Division. These wheelmen will be required to enlist in the Royal Engineers for the period of the war, and might be required for foreign service. The pay for privates is is. 2d., with an additional mini- mum of 6d. a day Engineer pay. Bicycles will be provided. Personal application should be made to the general secretary of the National] Cyclists' Union, whose offices are at 27, Chan- cery-lane, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>FOR SICK AND WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>FOR SICK AND WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Institution.</p>
        <p>The St. John Ambulance Association, which forms part of the Red Cross Organisation of Great Britain, derives its name and traditions from the Order of St. John of Jerusalem (Knights Hospitallers), founded at the time of the Crusades. It has at this moment many thousands of workers engaged in tending the wounded at the seat of war and in the hospitals of the Order.</p>
        <p>In peacetime it does not appeal to the public for subscriptions, but under the stress of war it finds itself in urgent need of help, and is abso- lutely compelled to ask for funds. Gifts should be sent to the Chief Secretary, Colonel Sir Her- bert C. Perrott, Bt. C. 8., at St. John's Gate, Clerkenwell, E.C., and cheques should be crossed "London County and Westminster Bank, Loth bury," and made payable to the St. John Ambu- lance Association. In aid of its work, a concert (at which Madame Patti will sing) is to be given at the Albert Hall this afternoon. The fund for the joint benefit of the Red Cross Society and the St. John Ambulance Association has now reached .t 500,000. Much suffering is caused through delay in get- ting injured men from the battlefield to hos- pital. The difference between prompt treatment and delay often means the loss of a limb, if not of life, and a'special effort is being made to place motor ambulances at the disposal of the Red Cross Society in the field. Already 500 of these life-savers have been given, including those from the King and Queen. Miss Sylvia Broadbent, The Hollies, Latch- ford, will be glad to receive subscriptions on behalf of the local or general funds of the Red Cross Society. I Mr. A. E. Spann, Friars' Green, local sec. of the St. John Ambulance Brigade, will also be glad to receive subscriptions towards the local or national funds. Last week the Working Men's Infirmary Committee voted £4 7s. sd. to the brigade from the proceeds of the open-air con- cert.</p>
        <p>Help for a Great and _Historic Humane</p>
        <p>in the day room, or looking at the English pic- torial papers over a "peaceful pipe." Whilst they are more than grateful for the generous gifts of local ladies and gentlemen, there is one thing these men want, and that is tobacco, and we feel quite sure that many kind- hearted sympathisers will send gifts or dona- tions in this direction. The men are unanimous in their praise of the excellent staff of nurses, a large number of whom can converse quite flu- ently with their patients. This is a great ad- vantage, which is thoroughly appreciated, and the men are pleased to be ale t.. supply any information for which they are asked. On Tuesday several of the wounded were con- veyed to St. Austin's House, Bold-street—in a motor car sent by Mr. Leonard Davies—where they saw and conversed with the refugees who are staying there.</p>
