      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>AVOUNDED WARRIORS. First Batch of. Belgian Patients at</head>
        <p>AVOUNDED WARRIORS. First Batch of. Belgian Patients at</p>
        <p>Haddon Court. " EXAMINER " INTERVIEWS. No sooner was Raddon Court Red CroSs Tem- porary Hospital fully equipped than it was requisitioned for wounded soldiers. Twenty- five Belgians, who had taken part in the world's greatest war, were received there on Monday, and for a long time prior to the hour fixed for their arrival from the Second Western General Hospital, Manchester, a large crowd were assem- bling outside the grounds in Knutsford-road. Despite the fact that they had only a few hours' warning, the Board of Management and mem- bers of Voluntary Aid Detachments 8.R.C.5., Cheshire, 22 and 48, had prepared everything for the reception of the patients, and the whole staff, with Mrs. F. A. Frost (quartermaster), the Matron (Mrs. Reynolds), Drs. Bower and Hutton (medical officers), were in readiness long before the lusty cheers outside announced the arrival of the motor cars containing the wounded. The Belgians looked around with smiles of thankfulness for the kindliness of their recep- tion, and, whilst it was plainly evident from their blanched and bearded faces and general unkempt and forlorn appearance that they had suffered in the cruel war, one was glad to note that there were apparently no desperately serious cases amongst them. One good-looking soldier had his arm in a sling, another had to have recourse to crutches in order to get along, but the others were able either to hobble or walk through the porch without assistance, which, if necessary, would have been rendered by several members of the men's detachment of the Red Cross Society, who were in attendance.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED GUESTS IN GOOD SPIRITS. After bidding adieu to the Army nurses and the orderly who had travelled with them, the patients were escorted inside the hospital and were soon made very comfortable by a number of the Red Cross vadets. Tea was served in the well-appointed day room, and over this the men grew very cheerful, and in many ,a way showed that they were thoroughly grateful for all that was being done for them. The hospital is well staffed, and patients who will be treated thero will have every care and attention. There ?ire three certificated nurses, besides the Matron. When he visited the hospital a representative of "The Examiner" found the men in good spirits. They are all able to get about, and some were seen having an " airing " onthe spacious lawn, whilst the others were quite happy Playing cards, dominoes, and other games</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MAYOR AS SANTA CLAUS.</head>
        <p>MAYOR AS SANTA CLAUS.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS GIFTS "AS USUAL" FOR POOR CHILDREN</p>
        <p>A meeting was held at the Town Hall on Friday afternoon to discuss the advisability of continuing the Christmas distributkin of dolls and toys, which have for the past two years been such a source of pleasure to the poor children of Warrington. The Cinderella Club of Manchester, under whose auspices this distribution was started, have decided not to make any distribution this year. His Worship the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) was very strongly of opinion that it would be a great pity if the distribution was stopped, and he very generously: offered to pro- vide such dolls as might be required for dres- sing by the school children, who contributed so well last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>"AT H 0 M E."</head>
        <p>"AT H 0 M E."</p>
        <p>His Worship also explained that it had been his intention, on account of the war, not to hold the usual "At Home." He, however, now proposed to make a new departure, and that was to hold an At Home" in the Parr Hall in the afternoon and evening, and he ex- pressed, the hope that everyone who attended might bring a doll or a toy, and these would afterwards be distributed amongst the poor children of Warrington. Such a course would give him an opportunity of entertaining his many friends, and also enable them, by their gifts, to bring a little light and sunshine into many homes, which might otherwise be dark and dreary during the coming Christmas. The suggestion met with the hearty approval of .the meeting. and'a small committee was ap- pointed to make the necessary, arrangements.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Guardians' Sympathy. The fortnightly meeting of thelluncorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., presiding.— Mr. Linaker, sen., mentioned the death of Dr. Selby's only child, and on his proposition a vote of sympathy was passed to the Doctor and his wife.—Dr. Selby is the Medical Officer to the Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>HAD HIS HORSE SHOT UNDER HIM.</head>
        <p>HAD HIS HORSE SHOT UNDER HIM.</p>
        <p>One of the patients, Camille Buyl, a freckled and tanned gendarme, told our reporter that when the war broke out lie was Mled upon to join a special corps of gendarmerie. On one occasion when fighting near Antwerp the horse on which he was riding was hit five times before it was killed under 'him by the same bullet that inflicted a wound in his foot. Shrapnel was bursting all around, but he was picked up by the ambulance and ultimately conveyed to Man- chester, via Ostend and Dover. He had not heard news of his wife and child for seven months, and did not expect to see them again. Yes ! He had visited the Belgian refugees in the town, and was very pleased to be able to speak with them. He thought the English were exceptionally good to his fellow countrymen and women. He thought that when the war was over there would be a very strong alliance be- tween the English, French and Belgians. He thought the ultimate victory would be with the Allies. .</p>
        <p>Jean Batiste Baguet, who was in the First Cavalry Regiment, received his wound in the trenches at Lierre, where he came across several of the British Marines, who, he said, rendered useful service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ESCAPED FROM SHELL-WRECKED BUILDING.</head>
        <p>ESCAPED FROM SHELL-WRECKED BUILDING.</p>
        <p>Edmund De Ryck said he was at St. Catherine before the advance of the Germans. When the enemy shelled the fortress, he and several com- panions sought shelter in a building, which was struck by a shell and collapsed. He was ren- dered incapable of further military service by a falling stone which hit him on the back. They had to sleep on planks on board ship when crossing to England. Adamer Thulier was also wounded at Lierre, and he was loud in his praise of the conduct of "Les braves Anglaise" in the trenches. Francois Rouje informed "The Examiner" man that after having been wounded at Haecht he was taken to a convent by the Germans, who occupied an upper room, whilst the prisoners were put in the cellars. The Germans were forced to leave Haecht, and friendly soldiers re- leased the prisoners. Guillaume Ceuleers, who suffers from rheuma- tism, was at Terlimont„ and when his regiment was hard pressed by the Germans they had to swim down a river for safety. A number of his compatriots were drowned in the attempt. He had to keep his wet clothes on for two days, during which time he was actively engaged in fighting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>LOST TRACE OF HIS FAMILY.</head>
        <p>LOST TRACE OF HIS FAMILY.</p>
        <p>M. Dassy, a non-commissioned officer, said that he was engaged in the fighting at Antwerp, being near Fort Warre, St. Catherine. The Germans came along in such great numbers that there was nothing left for the Belgians to do but retire. During the retreat he sprained his foot. Some of the British Marines, he said, visited the wounded Belgians when they were in hospital. His wife and children did live in Antwerp, but where they were now he could not say. He had also lost trace of his mother. Asked if he had seen any evidence of the Ger- man barbarities, he replied that as the troops were passing through a wrecked village he saw a well which contained the dead bodies of a farmer's wife and children. They had been drowned in the well, which was facing their own</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph, the medical officer of health, has visited the hospital, and has expressed himself as highly delighted with all the arrangements, whilst a trained nurse speaks very highly of the manner in which the under nurses perform their duties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>REFUGEES IN PENKETII.</head>
        <p>REFUGEES IN PENKETII.</p>
        <p>Twenty Homeless Belgians at "Hal</p>
        <p>Nook."c</p>
        <p>For some days past willing hands have been preparing Hall N'ook, Penketh, for the reception of a number of the unfortunate Belgian refugees, who, rendered homeless and penniless by the present cruel war, are being brought over to this country in hundreds in order to give the British people an opportunity of repaying, in some , measure the debt they owe to the gallant little ' nation that so nobly stood between the Germans and France whilst the Allies were organising their resistance to the Teuton invader. The refugees, who are to be guests in the house for an indefinite period, arrived in Penketh yester- day afternoon. Their coming was kept very quiet. When they detrained they were met by the Rev. Fathers Wilson and Rockliffe and Mr. C. E. Parker, who were accompanied by one or two others. The women and young children were taken in conveyances to their future home, but the bulk of the party walked there, probably relieved at the fact that there was no formal civic reception, or any fuss of that sort. As soon as they arrived the refugees were provided with a light repast, consisting of coffee, bread and butter and jam, after which Mrs. Parker, on behalf of the Garnett family and the local committee, extended to the guests a hearty welcome to Hall Nook, her kindly words of greet- ing and sympathy being suitably acknowledged by one of the party. Mrs. Parker accompanied the refugees on their train journey from Liverpool, being accom- panied by a capable matron, who has been aP- pointed to superintend the household manage- ment at Hall Nook. The party consists of twenty-one persons, in- cluding five families of superior working class character. The men have been engaged in various callings, and among their number is a school -teacher, a tailor, a mechanic, a butcher, and a shoemaker. The various household duties have been appor- tioned to the women, who will have ample leisure for needlework, &amp;c., and the men will give what, assistance they can, as well as taking some part in the garden work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>School Meals Discontinued.</head>
        <p>School Meals Discontinued.</p>
        <p>It was reported to the Education Committee on Monday evening that the provision of meals to school children was discontinued on Satur- day, September sth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>YOUNG PATRIOTS.</head>
        <p>YOUNG PATRIOTS.</p>
        <p>Trafalgar Day in the Schools.</p>
        <p>NELSON'S WORTHY SUCCESSOR.</p>
        <p>The 109th anniversary of "the glorious and ever-to-be-remembered" Battle of Trafalgar— the great sea fight in which Lord Nelson found the crowning success of his career and in which he also met his death—was celebrated, after the Manner of the past few years, in a number of the Warrington schools on Wednesday. It will be remembered that as each year the anniver- sary of the supreme battle which broke Napo- leon's sea power comes round, it is the custom of Alderman Bennett to offer prizes to be com- peted for by the scholars in the Secondary School and in the elementary schools of the town for the best essays on some phase or other of the career of the greatest British admiral, whose memory Trafalgar Day keeps green. This year the subject set was "Nelson's Motto and the Present War," and many essays, several of them of high quality, considering the youth of the authors and authoresses, were sent in, and it was with some difficulty, that the best were selected from amongst so many that were good. Eventually, however, it was decided to award the Alderman's prizes, each of which con- sisted of a substantial bundle of suitable books, to the following:—</p>
        <p>Secondary School: John B. Kay and Fanny West Upson. Bolton Boys' Council School: John Haines. Fairfield Girls' School: Annie Chantler.</p>
        <p>_ . The Head Master added two prizes for junior scholars at the Secondary School, and they went to Blanche Arden and Arthur Bayley. Of all the elementary school essays sent in, that penned by John Haines was adjudged the best, and accordingly the school to which he belongs will hold the Trafalgar Day Shield— also the gift of the worthy Alderman—for the ensuing twelve months.</p>
        <p>THE SECONDARY SCHOOL. On Wednesday Alderman Bennett visited the schools in order to formally present his prizes to the successful essayists. In the morning he went to the Secondary School, where he ad- dressed the scholars, who were gathered in the spacious gymnasium.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL SIGNIFICANCE OF NELSON DAY. The Alderman began by reminding the boys and girls that that was the ninth occasion on , which he had had the pleasure of addressing the ' school on Nelson's Day, and he need hardly say that he never spoke to them with a greater sense of responsibility. Although some of the people in this country had for a long time been rather afraid of the German menace, he did not think that any of them thought, a year ago that day, that on Nelson's Day, 1914, this country would be plunged into the midst of the most horrible war that had ever devastated Europe. Whilst they celebrated Trafalgar with peculiar pride on all occasions, this year they did so with a special significance. The message of Nelson's last signal, no less than his dying words, rever- berated in the hearts of the British people, and the same spirit of self-sacrifice as was displayed in both the final signal and the dying words of the great Admiral was being manifested to-day wherever the great battles were being waged. To him (the speaker) to be an Englishman seemed to imply a love of duty; one could hardly think of an Englishman without thinking of courage, self-sacrifice and of all the higher vir- tues that might be summed up in the word "Duty." Right down in the depths of the heart of every Englishman and Englishwoman the noble ideal of duty held supreme sway, and the flower of the manhood of the country had already proved the fact again and again on the battlefields of Europe. Duty was always a trumpet call to the English people. England's Hands Quite Clean.</p>
        <p>In this great war this nation's hands were absolutely clean; she was fighting for that which' made life worth living; for that which would preserve the world and civilisation from destruc- tion. They were fighting for the protection of small races; they thought that the smaller coun- tries like Belgium and Servia had a right to a place in the sun, as well as the great German Empire. They did not think that any nation by exercising, brute force had a right to crush the civilisation of the smaller races and prac- tically bring them to a state of slavery. Again, this country was fighting for the sanctity of treaties, and she was also fighting in aid of France—France, who, a hundred and nine years ago that very day, was Britain's enemy, but to-day was her best and firmest friend. She trusted to Britain and Britain had not betrayed her trust.</p>
        <p>The English soldiers and sailors of to-day, the speaker went on, were just as brave and self- sacrificing as the men who stood round Nelson over a hundred years ago, and the Admiral at present in charge of the Fleet—Admiral Sir John Jellicoe—had proved, and was still proving, himself to be a modern Nelson. There were</p>
        <p>people who were asking: "Wii7tioes not Jellicoe have a great big 'flare-up,' a world shaking battle like Waterloo?" Well, that was just the one thing that he and all his sailors wanted; for if only the enemy would come out of their harbours Nelson's triumph at the Battle of Trafalgar would be repeated. But, great as was the courage required when it came to a matter of getting into grips with the enemy, he (the speaker) often thought that it needed even greater courage to stand by waiting, waiting, waiting, as the British sailors were doing, never knowing what was going to happen, but realis- ing all the time that at any moment the vessel may be smashed to atoms, and all the men be sent to the bottom. Nation's Debt to Jellicoe.</p>
        <p>England owed much—perhaps, everything—to Jellicoe and his gallant, patient sailors, who were keeping their indomitable vigil in the may Nor cometh Se a, watching, waiting for the terror that without their being able to see it. The Nelson spirit was there just as it was on the historic day of Trafalgar. Every British sailor of to-day had the Nelson spirit in his veins and when the time came it would be seen that the Trafalgar lesson had not been learned in vain. Then, what of the British soldier? He had heard at first-hand from men who had fought at Mons and the Battle of the Aisne something of the awful suffering that the troops had had to undergo—fifteen hours without a single rest; two days, with not more than two hours' sleep; days and nights in wet trenches, men saturated to the skin, and the German shells booming and bursting overhead." I was always proud of being an Englishman," said the speaker, drawing to a conclusion, "but I was never prouder than I have been in the last few weeks. Nelson's words ought to ring in our ears always, but particularly now! To-day England expects every man to do his duty; aye, and every woman and child as well 1" (Cheers.) WHAT THE SCHOLARS ARE DOING TO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>HELP.</head>
        <p>HELP.</p>
        <p>The scholars signified their hearty thanks to Alderman Bennett by a rousing round of cheers, • Mr. Robson, (head master) having first re- minded them how year after year the worthy Alderman had tried to bring home to them the lesson of local and national patriotism. This year the Alderman was seeing the fruit of his prizes being gathered. He would like the Alder- man to know what the students at that school were doing in these days of great crises; he ' would like him to know that already they had I contributed towards the funds—the local war Lund, the Belgian Refugees' Fund, and the sol- diers' and sailors' fund, and the fund to provide tobacco for the soldiers organised by the Mayor. Last Saturday Form VI. raised £2 13s. for the tobacco fund by means of a concert, and Form Va. promised another social entertainment in a week or so, as also had Form 11., whilst the other Forms would come forward in due course, and the woodwork and metal-work departments intended to turn out a number of toys for sale in aid of the various charitable funds. Alderman Bennett briefly acknowledged the compliment paid him. Before and after the prize-giving the scholars joined in singing a number of appropriate selec- tions, among them the National Anthems of the Allies and the International Hymn, , Miss Lillie Weston recited Bret Harte's poem, "The Reveille," in a most polished and effective manner. whilst</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>BOLTON BOYS' SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>BOLTON BOYS' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>• In the afternoon Alderman Bennett visited two of the elementary schools in order personally to hand over to the winning scholars other -of his prizes. First he went to the Bolton Boys' Coun- cil School,/ Latchford, where he had the pleas- ure of presenting the premier prize in the essay competition (and with it the shield) to John Haines, whose essay was of special excellence, the youth of the writer being taken into con- sideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>The interesting presentation ceremoq school was presided over by Alderman Pier ENGLISHMEN AND THE CALL OF In making the presentation Alderman 111 said that as long as there was a British the name of Nelson would be remembere( pride and gratitude. The answer famous signal, hoisted on the Victory oni dred and nine years ago, evoked was evoked every day in these troublous Englishmen were always willing to flflw. call of duty, and if ever they answered Just and righteous war in the histoll 01 land, or of the world, it was in the. war—this war for the freedom of Be. for the observance of international treat! After referring to the manner in vf'hu gallant Belgians had stood in the araY German advance into France, the aPeakel on to say that the children of Latchford feel special pride in the fact that on!Y hundred yards away from the school 1-a„.. they were assembled, some thirty odd •E soldiers, who had been wounded 111 th( for the freedom of their nation, were qua England owed a great debt to Belgium, al an honourable nation England was PO England. had decided that "scraps 01 1, •i.i 1,„ I,nnouree</p>
        <p>bearing her signature sh'ould be honour' land had decided to be loyal to her friend to the extent of fighting for them.</p>
        <p>England a Steadfast Friend. The German Emperor wanted this cola buy peace for herself at the expense of ing her friends, but we refused to do o would have ceased to be English had it. They had chosen the honourable eta!! had stood firm by their obligations to P and their treaty with France. "And how do you think we are 61 lads?" asked the speaker, receiving ilk TE enthusiastic chorus of answers, amid No, words, "Fine, sir !" and "Splendid, sir' distinguishable. "Jellicoe is the modern Nelson," CO the Alderman. "His work is just as V brave and as noble as that of Nelson OX dred and nine years ago! Thank God I( food and—if I may say so without being i eat—thank Jellicoe also! People sa,Y_ should like to see the Navy do some flg," they did in Nelson's time.' Well, t", done some of that, and all they wanted, to do some more, was that the Germans come out and fight. But the Germar afraid to face Jellicoe and his British Brave as were the British sailors, the soldiers were just as brave; no British ever fought better than did that at , fighting under General French. ll° this would be the last great war Oa would ever see; they would never agall any nation to tyrannise a whole Conti; the Germans had done; they would nere all the horror, suffering and bloodshed t again. They had had in this present .1 hibitions of courage on the battleshil courage in the trenches and on the bat! they also had exhibitions of courage in and that was a peculiar feature of the war. In conclusion the speaker urged tl to try to do their duty, as were the solcli sailors, in that sphere of life to whirl' pleased God to call them. Alderman Bennett then presented 5 parcel of suitable books to Haines, w.l described by the Head Master (Mr. ton) as "a good lad, a real good lad; .5 whom other boys should look and say `-` boy who I am trying to copy.' " COUNCILLOR HENSHALL'S TRIE°I ALDERMAN BENNETT. Councillor lienshall, in proposing 5,1 thanks to Alderman Bennett, said t: worthy Alderman had done much to a boys and girls; aye, and the men and wc, Warrington, too; to remember what a thing it was to be an Englishman—a, He had written the very best historY rington ever penned, and those who I would find that in a-11 the epochs of tory the old town had played an imP°r,,"i honoured part. Continuing, the the boys boys always to bear in mind, and of, the fact that of all the nations, Grea" had played the greatest and most bahl part in the history of the world, the great dates in the story of our gre,:i try none, perhaps, was more memoraa that of the Battle of Trafalgar. t( The Rev. G. C. Briggs, vicar of Chris.o Latchford, seconded, and the proposlo carried by the youngsters after the ns. ner of the grown-ups. At this school the children sang, items, "The Death of Nelson," ".-1-,e,„,'01 Brave," the Russian National Ant' our oars with feathered spray," - laise and the English National Antieio FAIRFIELD GIRLS' S‘}(ll- - g CANON WILLIS AND THE ENE- -I,AI MUST ENGAGE. The Disgrace of Drunkentle4i Next, Alderman Bennett proceed Fairfield Girls' School, there, in theb; of the assembled scholars to present a of prize books to Annie Chantler, the as of Councillor Chantler, whose essay lv.aoc to be the best sent in from this scicle Presentation ceremony was presided °Th The Rector (Canon Willis), who, the proceedings, said that according all in "The Times," the welr-known sig," land expects," &amp;c., was not Nelson c that read, "Engage the enemy plar-t1; That command might be applied tclorg lives of the people of this countrY• tbe they must engage more closely was Is of excessive pleasure, and another tii enemy of drunkenness. Sometin.liesio seen even soldiers the worse for, '1.1101: wearing the King's uniform, and lie d f all who had gazed on the spectacle I'sti be a great disgrace. In that e°ll,lleicia( should note that the Czar of Russ's' eo an order that from the present 600_41 eating liquor was to be drunk throlaso vast dominion, and that despite Oleo by issuing su