      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Enemy-Aliens Arrested. An compliance with orders from the Home .7ace eleven aliens have been arrested in War- -011 during the week. Nine of those in 'WY are of German nationality. The The War Relief Funds. cit , Warrington Local Relief Fund now £ll7 is. 2dwhilst from this district , 38. has been sent to the National Relief 't1144(1. Nearly 1:60 has been subscribed to the ohYor's Fund for providing our soldiers with 4eco and "smokes." The National Relief 4114 totalled last night 1:3,386,000. A Oliver-street School. t . haeeting of the Education Committee on .7.lay evening approval was given to a re- u'uuendation by the Sites and Buildings Sub- :tittlittee that the Local Government Board aPplied to for an order for the compulsory e,httse of certain land and buildings adjacent 'cc school site in Oliver-street.</p>
        <p>Not Opportune. headmaster of the Secondary School e to the School Management Committee egesting the formation of ambulance classes 40onection with the school, but the corn- decided that at the present time it was ;6 desirable that such classes should be 43111 ed, a decision with which the Education a'aoittee concurred at their meeting on Mon- evening.</p>
        <p>Our Belgian Guests. 'ollections at the Royal Court Theatre on iday and Saturday last on behalf of the Bel- „III Refugees' Fund, realised ..tl5. ;" Lady Chairman of the Sub-committee in it'ige of St. Austin's House acknowledges thanks the receipt of many parcels of thing.</p>
        <p>To Help the Orphaned. 06 niusical entertainment and gymnastic dis- bY the Farnboro' Branch Choir of the tn"uonal Children's Home and Orphanage, °ll, will be given in the Co-operative Hall, Y-street, on Wednesday evening. The kil'utlan. was founded by Dr. Stephenson in 1869, st• is now one of the largest child-saving in- ions in the kingdom. More than 10,000 44 been rescued and helped. "Closing Time" As Usual. k a aining, approximately, 2,500 signatures, etition organised by the various temper- Societies of the town, in favour of the tin‘l,er closing of the public-houses in War- t:lt:lnn during the war, was considered at a "rlff of the Watch Committee held yester- ,afternoon. After discussion the commit- that, whilst fully appreciating the ations of the petitioners, there was at pre- no need to comply with their request. " Bonfire Nigh." _ It iS strongly felt that in the public inter- during the present state of affairs, the 4rlnff of bonfires on the night of November should be discouraged by everyone. Num- iii),,tlB reports have been circulating recently th,"llt aircraft passing over the town, and, al- there is, of course, no cause for alarm, 44,"nglit he well to refrain from causing largo hilOf light after dusk. We understand the are doing all they can to discoarae k, Practice—a dangerous one at any time. Y Crippled Children in the Town. e School Attendance Sub-committee re- to the Education Committee on Monday to i."l,llff that for the present they had decided tioh`a,''e no action in the matter of an applica- ler financial help towards the training of ! ,o3rsically defective child in a school for ioTillas, in view of the number of similar cases 10. le town. The sub-committee, however, pro- iptd full consideration of the whole platter "le near future. New Headmistress. Monday evening the Education Committee tt:rnved of a recommendation by the School killlnffement Sub-committee appointing Miss ti,'„ty7l Crompton as headmistress of the Infants' ok,''4llment of the St. Ann's School, at a cora- ll,etikting salary at the rate of .R,llB per annum; 4144;agement to commence on November, Ist. tertitCrompton is at the moment acting as a • St assistant assistant in the boys' department ,tBabc,i)4'eter's School. She will succeed Miss Harrison at St. Ann's, that lady hav- -elffned her appointment. Ti The Late Mr. James Ford. • mortal remains of the late Mr. James tt00),',., Of Pinewood-avenue—whose demise was, '‘ld in our last issue—were laid to rest in 4:11.; "darrington Cemetery on Saturday, the 401:4.41 service being conducted by the Rev. Ne'„cl,n. The principal mourners were Mr. Nithzin Ford and Mr. James Ford (sons), Mrs. (sister), Mr. and Mrs. Newton, Dr. °i:l.„"eit, Mr. R. Adamson, -Mr. S. Allen, Mr. tlrs4 Bradshaw, Mr. John Hitchmore, Mr. and holt; Nter Rigby. A large number of neigh- nod local Methodists—of which body the 'ih,,ed was a member—attended at the grave- '. u`n Pay their last token of respect.. Messrs. k"ilfield and Sons carried out the funeral gents ,(14 ,IScholastic Appointments. leb, "le recommendation of the School Man- ant Sub-committee the Education Com- ▪ have appointed Mr. Reginald Furness to teacher of chemistry at the Evening Tech- t,lt4;,` Institute, and Mr. John M. Couch to have ef a class for Post Office workmen at educational centre. The following have been appointed assistant teachers, -11,n staffs of the branch Technical Schools 1`" ensuing session ;—The Misses Grace ,tlld Lucy Marron (Fairfield). Ada Egerton, 44-,1". Parkinson and Ada J. West (Bolton), ,lf,"allies Amelia Gorton and Bridget Richard- tlitAta_nd Mr. Cormac Kelly, have been ap- supply teachers at the Beamont, St. ! and St. Mary's Schools, respectively, Nbisabella Chappell has been appointed. a of dressmaking at the Bolton Evening °Ol for Women. 41W0 Plucky Lads Rewarded. •Pols their bravery in rescuing three little boys `11(ll tlie canal at Black Bear Bridge on July Ilerbert Brady (13), 21, Oxford-street, ,4111,Lnford, and Edward Paul Powell (15), 18, ,4't-fitreet, Latchford, have been awarded the 40col Humane and Shipwreck Society's cer- lkali'n and 12s. 6d. each. The certificates and ' :010'ere formally presented to them at the v,,t00 Court on Monday.—The Chief Constable , tilled that he had received information of 5/„8' brave conduct from a member of the ‘-;00nci1, and he had brought the matter the Humane Society.—ln making the i`tl• ett-'4.tinii, the Mayor pointed out that the q%-'34 the three boys who were saved was only f, Ora of sae. Addressing the lads, he said, irtie" quite ;tire YOll will be.able to show these kt'? io'illtes in your homes as a standing reminder ; families and relations that you at one c Your history rendered noble service tci hkb othil±ldren in danger. I hope it will ericour- tilto. Ni,cr boys to learn to swim, so that, if the h t}bq arises, they can do as you have done." 4YOr.°YB shook hands with and thanked the</p>
        <p>A T. A tr, -Ime for Unswerving Faith. the'Vlsual Pleasant Afternoon seriyilocn Sun- e, held wYcliffe Congregational Churc I.* G. Collins gave a thoughtful address, e4„!°llrse of which he ably contrasted the itth" means of conversion in the cases of o 58, n the Nicodemus, and the thief o Pointed out that they were different 4 men, but they were all brought totl, of view and each was "born again. ttbrar, who presided, had a fewwordsto livib°ll,t the German doctrine of militarism ,4y-4.,11ew the gradual inculcation of the false might is right had brought about kliti,..'serlt bloody war, belief in such a doctrine '4 1116 inevitably to the conclusion that war it; e:B,_B4rY and good for the nations. That had led the people of a great nation °llt, thank God, the British people still Fa'islt to their faith in the Prince of Peace, 44,ri100d of God, and the Brotherhood of 44-erha4)s, many more months of strife „lest lead, and many more gallant lives would '4,llllnd and sea, but let them go on trust- Living God, wife, in His own good j`le'));eeld bring the minds and hearts of Rimself.—The Rev. T. T. James g(,"4l)eeial prayer for the P.S.A. men who ti,e to serve their King and country, and tl‘'4:l)ith44a4.t, he had letters, from some of the men 41eY spoke of the happy memories they Past P.S.A. gatherings at which they lieged to attend.—Mr. Yearsley and *IRI t 14.1 also spoke, and Mr. Payton pre- " Organ.</p>
        <p>What the Schools Cost.</p>
        <p>At their monthly meeting on Monday night the Warrington Education Committee endorsed the recommendation of the Finance Committee that accounts amounting to £5,366 3s. Bd. should be passed for payment.</p>
        <p>Civic Church-going. The Mayor and members of the Corporation will attend divine service at Bewsey-road Wes- leyan Church to-morrow morning, when a col- lection will be made in aid of the Belgian Refugees' Fund.</p>
        <p>A Well-known Clairvoyant. Mr. Tom Tyrrell, of Blackburn, a well-known clairvoyant, is advertised to be at the Spirit- ualist Society's Rooms, Druids' Hall, to-mor- row at 3.15 and 6.30.. The meetings should prove of interest to all students of psychology.</p>
        <p>Band and Torches To-night. Crosfield's Band and the bugles of the Cros- field's Boy Scouts have organised a torchlight parade for this (Saturday) evening, leaving the works at 8 o'clock, the object being to collect money for the provision of tobacco for our troops at the front.</p>
        <p>To Help Red Cross Work. In aid of local Red Cross work a rummage sale will be held in the Kendrick-street School on Saturday, November 21st. Those who have suitable articles to donate should communi- cate with Mrs. W. H. Tickell, Roseneath, Marl- borough-crescent, Latchford; or Miss Bogstrom, 99, Bewsey-road, Warrington.</p>
        <p>llunicipal Scholarship. The first 'Warrington Municipal Scholarship to a University has been awarded to William Edward Mason, son of Mr. W. Mason, head- master of Beamont School.</p>
        <p>The winner had his early training at Bea- mont School, and, later, up to this year, he attended the Secondary School, where, in suc- cessive forms, lie was at the top, or there- abouts. A successful career at Manchester is, all being well, in store for him.</p>
        <p>Scholars Master Natatory Art. A report submitted to the Education Com- mittee on Monday night showed that during the past season 802 boys from the public ele- mentary schools of Warrington attended at the public baths for instruction in swimming. At the end of the season the number able to swim was 276. Owing to the alterations to the baths no girls had attended for instruction during the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Cheshire Cheese.</head>
        <p>Cheshire Cheese.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall's cup for Cheshire cheese shown at the British Dairy Farmers' Show in London this week was won by Mr. C. E. Parton, Haughton Hall Farm, Tarporley. Sir Gilbert was the president this year, and at the annual meeting on Wednesday was thanked for his services. Lady Greenall was down to distribute the prizes in the butter- making section yesterday afternoon.</p>
        <p>Housewives and Craftsmen in the Making. A return submitted to the Education Com- mittee at their meeting on Monday evening showed that duriiy, the school year 1913-14, 1,554 Warrington scholars receis)ad instruction in cookery, 1,421 making themselves so proficient that the Board of Education grant was claimed in respect of them. During the same period 649 boys from four schools were learning light woodwork.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Market Prices.</head>
        <p>Market Prices.</p>
        <p>The following were the -/-ii-d-ce; ruling in the Warrington Market on October 21st :—Wheat, ss. hi. to ss. 4d. per 701 b.; oats, 3s. 2d. to 35. 2d. per 45th.; straw, 20s. per ton. Wheat is firmer and better prices are being asked and given. Oats are slightly easier, there being good sup- plies and the demand not being so • strong. XX flour was selling at 335. per 2801 b.; and best bran at is. id. per 201 b.</p>
        <p>Christmas Boxes for " Tommy " and " Jack."</p>
        <p>The Mayoress (Mrs. Peacock) appeals to War- ringtonians to help Princess Mary to realise her wish to send a Christmas present to every sailor afloat and every soldier at the front by sending subscriptions to Her Royal Highness at Buckingham Palace.</p>
        <p>Independent Order ol Reehabites. The " Pioneer " Tent (Juvenile Section of Rechabites Tent), which has for its place of meeting the Independent Methodist School- room—the birthplace of the first English Total Abstinence Society—held the first of a series of lantern entertainments on Monday evening. The subject was "The Valley of the Wye," and an interesting address was given by Bro. W. Watkins. Other local views and several photographs of District Officers were also shown. The meeting was largely attended by members, parents and friends, who afterwards listened to a bright little address by Bro. P. Howard (district superintendent).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Christmas Cheer for Our Soldiers.</head>
        <p>Christmas Cheer for Our Soldiers.</p>
        <p>- It will be seen from an announcement in our advertising columns that Messrs. Henry Milling and Co., Ltd., the well-known grocers in Horse- market-street and Corporation-street, announce the arrival of the New Season's Fruit for Christmas Puddings, Mincemeat, szc. All interested in sending seasonable presents to friends at the front, or those stationed at the various military centres in the kingdom, can therefore be supplied with all requisites for the compounding of such Christmas creature com- forts as plum puddings, mincemeat, &amp;c. Ex- cellence of quality being one of the things upon which Messrs. Milling and Co. have al- ways prided themselves, our townspeople can with confidence purchase at their establish- thent.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Prison for Unregistered Alien. At the Stockton Heath Police Court yester- day, before Mr. W. Fletcher (in the chair) and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, Arnold Hind, alias Arnoldnus Adrianus Van Der Heiden, a Dutch- man, was sent to prison for six months for being an alien residing within a prohibited area, and failing to report himself.—lt was stated that prisoner had come from London, and had been staying with Mrs. Arnold, at 334, Thelwall-lane, since the 19th inst.--Mrs. Arnold said that a neighbour of hers had given infor- mation to the police.—Prisoner said he did not know he had to register himself. He thought the order only applied to Germans.—The Chairman mentioned that Mrs. Arnold was liable to be dealt with severely, but the Bench considered that she had acted wisely in having information sent to the police and let her off with a caution.</p>
        <p>A War Wedding. The marriage of Miss Rhoda Lister-Kaye, (laughter of Mr. and Mrs. A. Lister-Kaye, of Grappenhall, and Lieutenant Guy S. Mitchell, of the King's Liverpool Regiment, was solemn- ised at Colwyn Bay Parish Church on Thurs- day, the officiating clergyman ,being the Rector of that parish. The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in a becoming travelling dress, and after the ceremony a re- ception was held at Rhos House, where a num- ber of guests were assembled. Afterwards the newly-married couple left for London, from which place they will journey to Aldershot, where Lieutenant Mitchell's regiment is sta- tioned. Mrs. Lister-Kaye, the bride's mother, who is in a delicate state of health, is staying at Colwyn Bay, and it was for that reason that the wedding took place there instead of at Grappenhall. The many handsome pre- sents which were received included one from the officers and staff of Lieutenant Mitchell's regiment.</p>
        <p>Secondary School Scholars Raise War , Funds. Secondary School scholars are doing their share to help and encourage our brave soldiers in many ways, and on Saturday Form VI. gave an excellent concert to the rest of the school in their class-room, as a result of which ..t2 13s. was realised. Part of the proceeds will go to the Tobacco Fund and the other portion will be utilised in the purchase of wool with which to make socks, &amp;c., for the soldiers. The scholars had arranged all the details of the concert, and the sketches, " The Demon Phonograph" and " Women's Eights," which were included in the programme, were given in admirable fashion, as also was the duologue, in which the girls played their parts exceptionally well. Mr. Hobson (the headmaster) spoke of the necessity of functions of that kind in aid of the soldiers, and announced to the assembled ! scholars that the performance would be re-1 peated this (Saturday) afternoon, in order to give the parents of scholars the opportunity of witnessing it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 24, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 24, 1914.</p>
        <p>PATRIOTS.</p>
        <p>WHY TIIEY ARE GOING TO THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>The Right Thing.</p>
        <p>" Hello !" I exclaimed to a young man, who measured 6ft. 2iin., when he was stripped for medical examination, "why are you enlisting?" "Well," he replied, "I read deeply into Mr. Lloyd George's speech on the war, and that settled me. I had been thinking much about the call for young men, and that speech finished me off."</p>
        <p>I spoke to this young man, for I had known him and his people for many years. He was an exceptionally bright young fellow of only nineteen summers, and was educating himself for the ministry. I felt he was bound to have some qualms whether he was doing a right or a wrong thing in taking up arms. But after much heart-searching he had decided it was his duty to fight for Britain to crush the military power of Germany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>"BRITAIN WANTS ME."</head>
        <p>"BRITAIN WANTS ME."</p>
        <p>Another case that came under my notice was that of a man of exceptional attainments, whom I had known both in Liverpool and in War- rington. He is of Scotch parentage, and has travelled much—to Canada, South America, China, Japan, Borneo, among other places. He said, as I shook hands with him, "Britain wants such men as me. lam single, and though it does not pay me to enlist, as I have made as much as £7OO a year in Argentina, I am going to the front to do my share. The German power must be crushed, for I feel if she wins, her mili- tarism will be a curse to the world." His view was that it is for those who love peace to destroy those men "whose delight is in war." Another case I came across was that of an ex- soldier, who had fought in the South African war. He is now in a very good situation, and lie informed me that he was very happy in it. "But," he said, "as soon as I read what the Germans did at Louvain, I at once made up my mind that I would fight against a race that per- petrated such barbarities."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>SOLDIER'S DAUGHTgR.</head>
        <p>A SOLDIER'S DAUGHTgR.</p>
        <p>Another case was that of another ex-soldier, who stated he had married the widow of his "chum." He said "The country needs such men as me at this time of her trial, and I am going to do what I can."</p>
        <p>len: he was asked by one, "What will your wife say when she hears you have enlisted?" he replied quickly, "She will throw her arms around me," and then he added with pride, "For she's a soldier's daughter !" One might also mention many other cases, as, for example, the eight old Grammar School boys of Warrington—" chums "—with life and excel- lent prospects before them, who have gone to the front, believing that "King and country needs them." These are simply typical of the spirit with which Britons are coming into the recruit- ing depots, and it is a spirit which spells the doom of Kaiserdom and German militarism. E. ENGLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>MENTIONED IN DISPATCHES. honour for Brothers Well Known in Warrington.</head>
        <p>MENTIONED IN DISPATCHES. honour for Brothers Well Known in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Two brothers, well known to many people in Warrington, have achieved the distinction of being mentioned in dispatches, viz., Lieut. and Quartermaster Boast and Sergt. J. Boast, both of the South Lancashires. They were formerly quartered with their regiment in Orford Bar- racks, and during their stay there they made a host of friends locally, one and all of whom will learn with pleasure and pride of the rare honour which has been conferred upon the gal- lant pair. Lieut. and Quartermaster Boast, the elder of the twain, will be better known to his Warring- ton friends as Sergt.-Major Boast, for that was his official description in the days when he re- sided with his family at Orford. Both in and out of the Barracks there he enjoyed much popularity. His brother, Sergt. Boast, left Orford about nine months ago. He then had his foot just on the bottom rung of the ladder of promotion, being a corporal. He is at present lying in the K ward of the Fazakerley Hospital, Aldershot, suffering from a wound in the left shoulder. He was mentioned by General French on account! of gallant service rendered at Mons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>MEN DROPPING IN DOZENS.</head>
        <p>MEN DROPPING IN DOZENS.</p>
        <p>Talking to a friend from Warrington he says: "It was at Mons that we had our first experi- ence of the enemy's fire. As we came along they suddenly opened a terrible artillery fire upon us, and we, surprised, fell back a little. Around me the men were dropping in dozens, not being prepared for the unexpected encoun- ter. Not far away was a hill, and rushing some distance up this I threw myself down and called to the men to rally round. About one hundred and fifty of them managed to do so, and we kept a large part of the Germans in check with our rifle fire. Presently Sir John French came round and asked: "'Who is in charge of this party?' " 'I am, sir,' I replied. "'And who are you?'</p>
        <p>" Sergt. Boast, sir!' "The great General then passed on, and I heard nothing more about it until the publica- tion of the dispatches." This is not Sergt. Boast's first taste of warfare, for he served in the South African War, and took part in the historic relief of Ladysmith. He wears with pride the Victorian and Edward- ian South African medals. As a reward for their bravery, both brothers will receive Distinguished Service medals. The following is a list of the local officers, non- commissioned officers and men brought forward for special mention for services in the field from the beginning of the campaign to October 8 by Field-Marshal Sir John French :</p>
        <p>2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment. Captain W. B. Ritchie. Lieutenant L. A. Clemens. Lieutenant B. V. Fulcher. Lieutenant S. T. Boast. Quartermaster-Sergt.-Major T. Roberts, 7656 Sergt. W. Harrison, 9263 Sergt. J. Boast, 4495 Company-Sergt.-Major J. Murphy, 9170 Sergt. A. Leavers, 7331 Corpl. J. Jones, 6749 Corpl. P. McQuiggan, 8265 Pte. S. Riddell, 8639 Sergt. F. Winser. Ist Battalion the King's (Liverpool) Regiment. Lieut.-Colonel W. S. Bannatyne. Capt. J. H. S. Batten. Capt. and Adjutant E. Hudson. Lieut. D. C 4. H. H. Scott-Tucker. No. 4533 Sergt.-Major T. H. Cuddy; No. 6915 Comp.-Sergt.-Major M. Swannick; No. 9485 Sergt. M. Fitznolan; No. 8615 Lce.-Corpl. J. Blackburn. •</p>
        <p>Ist Battalion Cheshire Regiment. • Lieut.-Colonel D. C. Boger. Capts. J. L. Shore and W. S. Rich. Lieut. W. G. R. Elliot. No. 7724 Comp—Sergt.-Major J. W. Francis; No. 7415 Sergt. Edge; No. 8475 Sergt. W. A. Nor- ris; No. Pte. Burns; No. 7173 Lee—Cori-A. A. Smith; No. 7085 Pte. F. MacCarthy.</p>
        <p>CLERICS WITH THE COLOURS. Two local chaplains have been ordered to join their regiments. They are the Rev. W. Brace- camp, vicar of St. Paul's, and the Rev. H. S. F. Collier, vicar of Christ Church, Padgate, and they will respectively rank as major and cap- tain. Mr. Collier is expected to leave the town to-day and Mr. Bracecamp will go early next week. At the meeting of the Board of Guardians on Wednesday the members wished both rev. gentlemen the best of health, and hoped that they would return safe, well and strong. The Bev. W. Bracecamp was the chaplain at the Workhouse, whilst the Rev. H. S. F. Collier occupied a similar post at the Padgate Cottage Homes.</p>
        <p>HAVE "JOINED UP." At the meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Tuesday, Miss Broadbent referred to Mr. Garnett haying joined the Sportsman's Battalion of the Royal Fusiliers, and to the fact that the Rev. A. M. Mitchell's son had also joined the colours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RESERVE TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>RESERVE TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>A party of about 170 of the members of the 4th (Territorial) Battalion of the South Lan- cashire Regiment arrived in Warrington late on Thursday. in order to strengthen the new re- serve battalion. They were under the command of Captain Egerton Fairclough, who was assisted , by Lieut. Seymour Jones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>War Stories : Bu Letter &amp; Interview</head>
        <p>War Stories : Bu Letter &amp; Interview</p>
        <p>A HAAVKE SURVIVOR.</p>
        <p>Warrington Man's Thrilling Raft Story.</p>
        <p>WAITING GERMAN SUBMARINE.</p>
        <p>The--tory of a terrible night spent on a raft, with comrades dying all around, while the living endeavoured to keep up their spirits by singing snatches of rag-time, was related to a representative of "The Examiner" by Stoker Robert Bradshaw, R.N., who, at present, is spending a furlough with his wife and family at 27, Lord Nelson-street. Stoker Bradshaw, whose mother lives in Wash-lane, Latchford, is one of the survivors of the crew of H.M.S. Hawke, the cruiser that was sunk last week by a German submarine. It was," he said, "about five or ten minutes to 11 o'clock on Thursday morning of last week when we were struck amidships, and ten 'minutes later the Hawke had gone down. At the time we were hit I was having a game and was really enjoying myself. The men knew what was amiss and all coolly made their way to deck before the "Still" was sounded. Everybody obeyed the order and did not move from his position until he received his instructions. There was no panic, everybody was cool, and the thing was properly done. "I was deputed to see to the boats, but the steam davits would not work. Only one boat wal' successfully launched; another capsized in the attempt to launch it. Some of our men said that they saw the submarine, but it was then too late to retaliate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>MANY DEATHS FROM EXPOSURE.</head>
        <p>MANY DEATHS FROM EXPOSURE.</p>
        <p>"After swimming about in the water for half an hour, I came across a raft. There were three other similar ones. They were made of planks bolted together and then fas- tened to six barrels. Other men came on to the raft, until we totalled about 30. We were not 'in the dumps' on account of our misfor- tune, and things seemed a little better owing to the fact that the sun began to shine during the afternoon. As night came on we began to feel the cold, and it became terrible as mid- night and the early hours of the following morning approached. Men were dying from exposure and dropped over the side of the raft. "We could see the lights of a number of merchant ships as they ploughed their way through the water, but we were always too far away to signal to them. We realised that we would have to make the best of matters, so we began to sing the old ditties and rag-time songs. We also helped to keep up our spirits by ask- ing ourselves the old tag, 'Are we down- hearted?' And answering it with rousing yells of 'No.' _</p>
        <p>"With the advent of dawn those who re- mained alive on the raft began to believe that there was hope of rescue. That hope was realised when, at about nine o'clock, we were sighted by H.M.S. Swift. We were in high glee, although we mourned the deaths of our comrades and shipmates. Amongst the other boats who came to our rescue was H.M.S. Christopher, and it was on this vessel that. we were taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>STARTLING SURPRISE.</head>
        <p>A STARTLING SURPRISE.</p>
        <p>As these boats were coming to our aid we received a startling surprise, for, not more than three yards from our raft, the periscope of a German submarine appeared, standing about six inches out of the water. No doubt it had been prowling about all night to see if any British boats came to our assistance. However, none came during the night, but when they began to show themselves on Friday morning this submarine disappeared in the opposite direction. "When the Christopher came near us we gave her a cheer, and the captain of the boat asked his men to respond, but they could not— there were lumps in their throats! "After 221 hours' exposure, only six of the thirty men who got on to our raft were alive. The others had all died from exposure. "The officers and men of the Christopher could not do too much for the few of us left. They were very kind indeed and showed a spirit of brotherhood and comradeship which we can never repay." Stoker Bradshaw looked little the worse for his terrible experience and stated that he was more than willing to go back to duty again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>"TOMMY'S" CANADIAN COMRADES How the Lads from the Far West Came.</head>
        <p>"TOMMY'S" CANADIAN COMRADES How the Lads from the Far West Came.</p>
        <p>Mr. Martin McMullen, son of Mrs. McMullen, of the Winwick-road Post Office, who went to Canada about five years ago, and is one of the first contingent to come over from the Land of the Maple Leaf to take part in the war, has written two letters to his mother, both of them reaching her in the same envelope. One of the letters was written at Valcartier, Quebec, but the writer was unable to post it until he reached England. It tells how he and his comrade-volunteers got a great send-off from Victoria. "The streets," he says, "were so packed with people that we had hard work to make our way through the people, and when we got near the wharves we had to march in single file, but we managed to get on the boat after a hard struggle. When the beat set out the noise was deafening. There were about 20,000 people on the wharves and they made noise enough for a crowd of a hundred thous- and at an English Cup final. All the factory whistles and boat syrens were blowing their loudest, but with all the noise and hand- shaking, the cheers and the heartiness of our send-off there was one thing that was missing for most of us, and that was that there was no mother there to wish most of us good-bye.' It was much easier to leave Victoria than it would be to leave homey</p>
        <p>A Six-day Train Journey. "Well, we got away and started for Van- couver, and walked off the boat on to the train, and then started on a 3,200-mile journey, which took us nearly six days. We were in the same railway coach all that time can tell you that six days aboard a train'is no joke, especially when it is crammed full. . . . The camp here (Quebec) is a great place; there are about 33,000 men in it lt is a great sight to see the miles of tents all in a straight line, and our camp-town has the ap- pearance of a very busy city." The writer goes on to describe how on the long railway journey to Quebec the people waited at the stations, especially those in British Columbia, to present the gallant boys with boxes of fruit—oranges, apples and grapes. There were a number of stops on the long journey to Quebec; for instance, at Shuswap Lake the train was halted whilst the volunteers had a swim; at Medicine Hat they got out and marched round the town, and at Winnipeg they went to the public baths "I am not sure if I will be one of the lucky ones to go to the front, but I think I will be." Writing subsequently from Salisbury Plain, Martin informs his mother that the Canadian volunteers were on board six days before they left the American Coast. "There were," he writes, "thirty-two liners and we had six cruisers to escort us across As we came into Plymouth Sound on the boat it was a good sight for sore eyes, especially to those of us who had been used to seeing nothing but mountains and rocks _ _ We made</p>
        <p>--------- - a very long passage, as some of tha boats were very slow, and we had all to keep together, so that it took us ten days altogether."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>CIIURCHES BLOWN 'lO PIECES.</head>
        <p>CIIURCHES BLOWN 'lO PIECES.</p>
        <p>Writing to his mother, who lives at 8, Friars' Green, Warrington, Private J. Brodrick, of the Ist Battalion of the King's Liverpool Regiment, which is serving with the Expeditionary Force in Prance, says: "My health is very good con- sidering the roughing we have to ,do. Our last spell in the trenches was twelve nights and days, spent in a very cramped position. It is very wet and cold at nights and a lot of our fellows are down with rheumatics. John Frainey, of Cockhedge, got wounded a few days ago. He is the only chap from Warrington that I know of as having got hit. We are hav- ing a lot of comfort at present, being billeted in a barn for a few days. This is a beautiful country and it seems a shame to smash it up; even the churches are not escaping. Nearly all the people have left their homes, leaving beau- tiful furniture behind them. At nearly all the cross-roads crucifixes are erected, and all the buildings are of white stone, and look picture- esque in the sunlight Send me a I muffler and handkerchief if possible."</p>
        <p>'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>200 SHELLS A MINUTE.</head>
        <p>200 SHELLS A MINUTE.</p>
        <p>Graphic Stories by Brothers Who Fought at Antwerp.</p>
        <p>The adventures of two Warrington brothers who are serving in the Royal Marine Brigade at Antwerp were related to a representative of "The Examiner" this week, by Privates William and George Sollery, whose home is at 163, Longford-street. The brothers are in the same section of the Brigade, but whilst at the front they did not see one another for a few days. During this time the younger brother, George, was informed that William had been killed.</p>
        <p>It was, said the brothers, on October 3rd they marched from Dunkirk to Oudegod, a small town near Antwerp. At the time they arrived there the. German guns were bombarding the city. On Sunday, October 4th, the Brigade relieved the Belgians behind a barricade, but did not see any of the enemy during the day. However, during the night, German shells began to fall at the rate of about 200 every minute. This fighting took place near Lierre, and the barricade, which was made of sand- bags, was erected across the street and in front of it there was a canal, the bridge over which had been blown up.</p>
        <p>"On the Monday," said the elder brother, "there was again little to do, but during the night the enemy brought a gun—a four-pounder —to within 200 yards of us. At that distance the gun looked like a black patch, and at first we could not tell what it really was. However, the gunners who worked it fired 30 shells, and although they were so near, only one of them hit us, and that one did nothing serious. It just caught the barricade, knocking several bags over and throwing four of our men on to their backs. They were not hurt. Exciting Gun Duel. "The work of the gun," said the elder brother, "was brought to an end when our machine gunman got on the scene. He cleared the enemy away from their gun and they dare not go back for it. When daylight came the Germans tried many times to regain the gun, but every attempt was met by 'a few cracks' from our machine gun. The Germans even tried to lassoo it—but without avail. "On the Tuesday morning the Germans had found our range and began to send shells at us from the big guns. The first shot brought down the side of a house which stood •just to the rear of us. We were told to get away as best we could, but some of our men were killed. One man had both arms shot off. He ran away with us, but fell down exhausted, or because he had received a further wound as we were scuttling along the railway. "As we were scattering a 'Jack Johnson '— a shell which gives off a dense volume of black smoke—exploded near me, the shock throwing several of us to the ground. I got up and found that I had not received a scratch, but some of the other fellows—poor chaps !—had been killed. Later my brother was surprised to find that I was alive, for somebody had seen me bowled over with the shell and had told him that I was killed. I ran through a house, over a hencote, and dropped into a cabbage field. There were others there, and, although the Germans were firing at us all the time, not a single man was hit.</p>
        <p>Shells Falling Like Rain. "We reached a village two or three miles away, and from there we intended to launch out upon another attack, but the shells were falling like rain, so we fell back again. "We took up a position in the trenches, but could do nothing save listen to the whistling of the shells as they flew through the air. On Wednesday we got into other trenches near a wood, but our officer had an idea that the Ger- mans had found our range. We cleared out into an open field at three o'clock on Thursday morning, and there we remained until we received the general order to retire. "In the meantime I lost my brother. He was just caught on the shoulder by a bullet as he was helping one of his pals, who had been injured, into a house. The poor lad died in my brother's arms before they reached the house. My brother is wearing the dead man's hat now. "During the day the Germans had been shel- ling the main road to the city, thinking that that would deter us from taking that road, but we took it in spite of them and got along with- out, a scratch! We went through one of the city gates, where the Belgians were on guard, and then over the River Scheldt by means of a pontoon bridge constructed on German barges. As soon as the last man got over a Belgian soldier blew the bridge up. We saw him with the fuse.</p>
        <p>"We were going to sleep as we marched along and every time we halted we dozed off. There were thousands of poor homeless Belgians tramping the same road. Among them were old men and cripples, women and children—it was a sight that was enough to break the heart! As we left Antwerp the city was in flames—a fate which had befallen a number of villages in the locality." Going Back Again. . _</p>
        <p>After various other experiences the Brigade arrived at Ostend, where they were put on a boat for "rest." _ _</p>
        <p>"On Saturday we were told that we were going back to England to be re-organised, and we took our quarters up on a boat called 'The Honorius.' All our baggage was left at Oude- god, and we could not recover it, because the Germans had shelled the station and set it on fire.</p>
        <p>" Whilst we were away we were all greatly pleased to receive a number of cigarettes which had been sent out by a fund raised in England. They were very welcome; they were a Godsend to some of the chaps! ianct that they were g amongst our fellows,Theruenlemsv anxious to make a bayonet charge, but were assit was agtrutimlablf not given the opportunity. It was impossible owing to their artillery. "We are going back again to-night to do our little share," said the brothers, as they shook hands with our representative at the close of the interview.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>GERMANS IN LUMPS.</head>
        <p>GERMANS IN LUMPS.</p>
        <p>South Laneashires Were Certain "To</p>
        <p>Hit Somebody.</p>
        <p>A vivid description of the first two days of the great war was furnished to "The Exam- iner" representative by Private Finch, a mem- ber of the South Lancashire Regiment, who is at present at Orford Barracks.</p>
        <p>"After we landed in Prance,?" he said, "we were on the march for two or three days, until we came in the vicinity of Mons. Here it was that we commenced to dig our trenches on that memorable Sunday, but when they were hardly half-finished, a German shell was dire:tted at us. That was the first shot of the battle. We took what cover we could, and waited for the enemy, and when they did come, they did so in 'lumps.' We were fighting all day on Sunday, and from early on Monday. It would be about 9.30 the same morning, as we were retiring, that I was bit in the shoulder, and the bullet came out again underneath my arm. On the first day we hardly lost a man, but terrible havoc must have been wrought upon the Germans, for, if we fired low, we were certain to hit somebody. War is a terrible thing! When the Artillery and Infantry on both sides are engaged the noise is deafening, and you seem to lose your head a little. German aeroplanes were circling over our positions, and it was no doubt owing to their services that the enemy's fire proved so deadly the following day. "It was a very hot day, and many of our men were fighting in their shirt sleeves, for they found it much cooler. During the Sunday night I neighbouring villages were burning furiously, and we set fire to a cornfield which might have . been of assistance to the Germans. In the night the Germans worked their position, and got on a level with the end of our trench, and enfiladed us the next morning. This is what made our list of casualties so heavy, and what made us retire. We charged them with our bayonets, and they did not seem to relish the idea of cold steel. "After I had been hit. I was put on to a cart, and shells were dropping near as I was taken away. Every care and attention was paid to all the wounded by the staff at the French hospital where I was taken, and after a few days' stay, I, with many others, was sent to Rouen, where we boarded a ship for home." This young soldier's tunic, which he still wears, was torn on the shoulder, where the bullet struck him. He stated that he had a great admiration for the British officers, who J had every sympathy with the rank and file.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>MITTENS AND GLOVES FOR THE COMING WINTER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield has succeeded in</p>
        <p>interesting several bodies in Warrington—the Women's Liberal Association Sewing Party, Bank Quay Mission, Crosfield's employes, and others—to work for the Grand Duke Michael's scheme to provide the British troops in France with woollen gloves and naittens to keep their hands warm in the trenches during the coining 'cold weather. To any party willing to knit, Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield will gladly send the wool free of charge, that being their contribu- tion towards making a success of the effort. They would also pay the carriage to London. Mittens would be worn continuously, and, excepting while engaged in actual fighting, for a great part of the time spent in the trenches and elsewhere, the gloves would be quite in- valuable. One need only consider that many and many a time in the course of this terrible struggle benumbed hands might cost a man his life, just as hands that are warm with a good circulation might save it, in order to realise the great importance of having a supply of these necessaries sent to the front. _</p>
        <p>The Grand Duke, in making his appeal, which has the active approval of the Queen, and the warm sympathy of Lord Kitchener, says he proposes to send 250,000 each of mittens and gloves. It lies within the power of every woman and child in the country to help the cause. "Children in the schools of the coun- try," he says, "may help by knitting pairs of gloves and mittens, and sending them on to me. This would indeed be a kindly and use- ful task for every child who wishes to do some- thing to help the country's defenders in this hour of need—and all children wish to do so."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>HOW TO WORK MITTENS AND GLOVES.</head>
        <p>HOW TO WORK MITTENS AND GLOVES.</p>
        <p>MITTENS.</p>
        <p>MITTENS 14IN. LONG TO RE WORN OVER COAT SLEEVE.—Cast on 84 stitches, knit Bin., then narrow ten times. You have then 74 loops; knit 12 rows, then narrow eight times, leaving 66 loops; knit 12 plain rows.- You will have 22 on each needle. To form thumb knit 10 loops, then make a loop, miss one and make a loop; knit off the remaining loops. Knit all round and raise every other needle until there are 22 loops, which form thumb.</p>
        <p>Take thumb loops off and put on a piece of wool and knit three plain rows round; then knit one plain and one purl until you have eight rows cast off. Now take up the 22 stitches (knitting double to fasten the wool). There will be a loop at each side of thumb. This makes 24 stitches. Knit two rows plain, then purl, and plain six rows and cast off.</p>
        <p>Size needles 12. 4oz. to soz. of four-ply wool required.</p>
        <p>GLOVES.</p>
        <p>MAN'S WARM GLOVE.--Diaerial required, 20z. (I cut) of double knitting in any shade of khaki, grey, or navy blue and 6 needles, No. 13. Abbreviations: p purl, k knit, st stitch. Cast on 48 stitches, 16 on each of 3 needles, knit 30 rounds of 2 knit and 2 purl or 21 in. for the wrist.</p>
        <p>Foit THE HAND AND TO INCREASE THE THUMB. First round.—P 1, kl,p 1, increase by knitting next st from front and back, p 1, k 1, *p 1, k 3*. Repeat all round increasing 2st in round to make pattern end right. Second round.—P 1, k4,p 1, rest plain. Repeat this round after every pattern round, taking notice that the same 2 st are purled each</p>
        <p>time.. The piece between the p a forms the</p>
        <p>thumb. Third round.—P 1, k 4, *p 1, k. 3. Repeat from * and end with p 1, k 1. Fifth round.—P 1, k 1, p 1, k 1, increase, p 1, k 1, *p 1, k 3. Repeat from *. Seventh round.—P 1, k 3, p 1, k 1, p 1, *k 3, pl. Repeat from *. The two plain et will be plainly noticed now. The pattern after the second p st is alter- nately started, k 3, p 1, as in row 7, and k 1, Pl,k3 as in row 5. This is done till 8 in- creases for the thumb are made. These in- creases are made in every 4th round. After these are made k 8 rounds, keeping to the pattern.</p>
        <p>FOR THE THUMB. 18 ST. Take those st between the 2 p st and the 2 p st also, cast on 4 more, put 6 of those on each of three needles and join into a round tightly and work 9 patterns or 2 inches, then narrow or decrease at the beginning and end of each needle, k 1 round, decrease at beginning of each needle and break off wool, thread a darning needle and run the end of wool through the st on the needle and draw up tightly and fasten off end. The fingers are finished off exactly in the same way.</p>
        <p>FOR THE HAND"</p>
        <p>Divide the 45 on the two needles on to three and pick up 3 st at the bottom of the thumb when the 4 were made (it is well to twist them to prevent a hole). Join into a round and wok 9 Pattern rounds to finish hand. First Finger.-16 st. Take the three made st and 1 to the left with 10 to the right, cast on 2 and join into a round, k 15 pattern rounds And finish as for thumb. Second Finger.-17 st. Take 6 st from each of the 2 needles and pick up 3 st at bottom of the Ist finger and cast on 2 at end of front needle, join and work 17 pattern rounds and</p>
        <p>finish off. Third Finger.-16 st. Take 6 st from each of the 2 needles and pick up 2 at bottom of second finger and cast on 2 at end of front needle. Join and work 15 patterns and finish</p>
        <p>off. Fourth Finger.-14 st. Take the 10 remaining st, pick up 4 between this and third finger, join and work 12 patterns and narrow off. This is the right hand glove. To work that for the left hand, increase for thumb after the first p st instead of before the second.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The sewing party held in the John Crosfield Memorial Hall every Monday and Thursday continues to be well attended, and shirts, socks, helmets, body belts, cuffs, mittens, &amp;c., are being turned out in large numbers. Many articles are being supplied to Warrington men belonging to various regiments stationed all over the country. Large consignments are also being made to the Territorials in bulk through the official Battalion Equipment Com- mittee at the Drill Hall. On November sth a whist drive will be held in the Memorial Hall to augment the funds for buying material for the workers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>PRESSING NEEDS.</head>
        <p>PRESSING NEEDS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Edelsten gave the following particulars to the ladies at the Town Hall on Thursday :</p>
        <p>Total number of patients received at faza- kerley Hospital since the outbreak of war, 1,215. Of these, 803 had been discharged, 404 were at present in the wards, and there had been 8 deaths. The greatest need at present is second-hand suits of clothes for the men leaving the hos- pital. Their uniform in nearly every case is in rags, and a fresh military outfit is net given till they re-join their depot. Warm shirts, socks, and mufflers are also much needed, and easy boots and shoes for hospital wear. Large bundles of clothing were dispatched to the Base Hospital, to Lady Barr for the hospi- tals at the front, to the London headquarters of the Belgian refugees, and woollen comforts were also sent, per Miss Woods, for the use of the crew of a newly-launched submarine. Letters of thanks had been received from Lady Greenell and Mrs. Vere Davies on behalf of the Navy League and the Belgians at St. Austin's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>DOMINIONS HELP BELGIANS.</head>
        <p>DOMINIONS HELP BELGIANS.</p>
        <p>In Australia special efforts are being made to send relief to the people of Belgium. The Aus- tralian House. of Representatives has voted £lOO,OOO as a gift to the Belgians. The Canadian Government has presented £lO,OOO to the Belgian Relief Fund, and numerous gifts in money and kind are being made by the Provincial Governments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WORK FOR ALL. No Need fort' nemployment in the Tewa. 'WARE THE SHIRK-WORK.</head>
        <p>WORK FOR ALL. No Need fort' nemployment in the Tewa. 'WARE THE SHIRK-WORK.</p>
        <p>If there is one person other than the Kaiser who ought to be given a "hot time" just now, it is the professional loafer, never-work and cadger. Many firms in the town who are ex- ceptionally busy on account of large Govern- ment orders, are crying out for me l to take the places of those who have joined ths. colours, but the Weary Willies and Tired Titll3 in our midst are past masters in the art of being unem- ployed, and .go on with "business as usual." They can spin a yarn of poverty and privation well enough—it is their business. Unfortun- ately, there are far, too many people who, affected by the tale of "the starving wife and kiddies," give alms indiscriminately. This should be stopped at once. Ask the roadster or the street corner habitue why he doesn't work, and if he survives that shock and assertc that there is no work for him to do, tell him that if ' only he will visit the large works in Warring- ton, he can have work and plenty of it. Don't give him money on any account. Think of how hard it has been for you to earn it, and bow easy 'it is for the cadger to get :what he wants —enough to keep him going in "grub and beer "—if you are foolish enough to respond to his appeal.</p>
        <p>Work Shunned. Work, to these men, is a thing to be shunned as the plague; it is a means of earning an honest living, which to them is one of the greatest crimes imaginable. The Editor hit the nail on the head last week when he said: "Able-bodied men who won't work in these times certainly should not eat. We have better and more urgent need for our foodstuffs." The works managers in the town are experi- encing to a great extent the difficulty in obtain- ing workmen, and, moreover, keeping a large number of them who make a start, and, finding that earning a living means a little hard work, "retire" after a couple of days or so. I had an interview with a manager of one of our large wire works, who said: "There is a feeling amongst men that they can pick their jobs. A man came to me the other day and asked for a job. I sent for the foreman, who took the man up the yard. The work-seeker said, 'I am on the rocks,' intimating that he desired a 'sub.' When told that he would have to work before he got anything, he said, 'I don't think I will start. I have changed my mind.' A lot of these men have come in the town from the outside districts. They either get frightened or dis- gusted with what they see and clear off." "You have plenty of work for them to do, haven't you?" I asked. "Oh, yes," came the reply; "there is plenty of work for those who will settle down. Some of them stay for a day or two, but in most cases they settle down for a fortnight, get some money, and then clear out of the town." Effect on Wages. "Has this shortage of men had any effect on wages?"</p>
        <p>To this question I received an answer in the affirmative. "Yes," said the manager; "it has had a temporary effect in increasing wages." "You are managing to keep things going all right?" I ventured. "Oh, yes; but I can assure you it is a science to do so. Still we have to make the best of things, you know." _ _ _</p>
        <p>, I was glad to hear that final optimistic sen- tence, as I left my informant to continue his thankless task of interviewing men who osten- sibly seek work, but who, in reality, wish they were far enough from it. I saw another of these "workmen-seekers" at another equally large works, and his was a similar story to the one I had previously heard. "This kind of thing undoubtedly makes the men very independent when they know there is a big demand for them." Such was his first statement.</p>
        <p>I asked him to give a comparison between now and ordinary times, and he replied, "Well, in the ordinary way I could get 40 men in a morning. Now I have to try about a week to get them." "how are you situated as regards skilled labour?" was a question to which the answer I received was: "The only skilled labour we can get now is by the men who are medically unfit for service. Owing to that, firms are tak- ing far greater risks of compensation cases than they would do otherwise." False Independence. This gentleman had had similar experience to the other in regard to men who would work two or three days and then "chuck the job up." "They give up their' jobs for very slight reasons," lie said. "Navvies are very inde- pendent as a rule, but now they are ten times worse. One of them came for a ' sub ' when all the office people had gone home, and because he could not get one he demanded his money the next morning and went off." At other works in the town I heard the same stories, one manager complaining that on account of there being a shortage of experienced men they had to take on two men who were in- experienced to get the work through. They were also giving men a full wage to do work which, at ordinary times, could be done by a lad. "There is absolutely no need for anyone to be unemployed." Such was the statement made by all with whom I spoke. "Silver bullets" will win this war, so we must ' remember not to encourage these "lazy good- for-nothings" by giving them coppers, but put them face to face with work or starvation. Every man likes to live, and before men will starve a large majority of these knights of the road will work, and so help to maintain our industries, which means so many more "silver : bullets" for our country. 11fen are badly I needed, so that when accosted by the man who I" hasn't twopence for a night's lodging," send him to one of the works in town or the Guild of Help, and you can rest assured that if he really wants to live he can do so by the sweat of his own brow. E.</p>
        <p>NEW COUNCIL SCHOOL. Parr Street Building To Cost Nearly £B,OOO. At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee, held in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall on Monday evening, Councillor H. Roberts presided, and there were also present Mr. A. J. Jolley (vice-chairman), the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Alder- man Smethurst, Councillors Broadhurst, Osborne, Dr. Joseph,Wilkinson, Jenkins, Locker, Henshall, Dickenson, Archer and Chrimes, Mrs. Jackson Holmes, the Rev. Canon Steven- son, Dr. Fox, and Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, Longland, Unsworth, Fairclough, Holman Kingdon, J. Upson, C. E. Parker, Jarman, and Plinston, together with the Director of Educa- tion (Mr. J. Moore Murray). The Sites an Buildings Sub-committee re- commended that the tender of Mr. Harry Fair- dough, amounting to £7,896, for the erection of the Parr-street Council School, be accepted, and that application be made to the Local Govern- ment Board for their sanction to the raising of J. a loan to cover the cost of the building. The committee were informed that the esti- mated cost of the main school building was put at £5,932, whilst the cost of the special subjects centre in connection therewith was estimated at £1,497, the remainder of the estimated total being allocated to the caretaker's cottage, .1%.c. The cost of the building would work out at about £l2 14s. 10d. per head on the number of scholars it was proposed to accommodate. The committee's recomMendation wa9 adopted.</p>
        <p>The 2111thVagrurairntegrtloynmFeeotrinegsteinrs. connection with the Warrington District a the Ancient Order of Foresters was held at the Blue Lion Hotel on Saturday, when Bro. A. B. Healey. D.C.R., presided, and a good attendance of delegates included the Mayor (Alderman Bro. Peter Peacock). The principal business was the election of officers. Bro. John Chrimes, secretary of Court 00, was chosen Chief Ranger, with Bro. E. R. Hollins, of Court 475, Sub-district Chief Ranger. Bro. Healey, the rre.tDir.cin.ll,s retiring Chief Raanndgea f Ranger, rl'amsed epresalteedm. with a Vital Statistics. The report of the Medical Officer of Health (Dr. G. W. N. Joseph) for the week ending October 17th shows that during the period under review there were 42 births and 24 deaths, as against 47 and 28 last year. Six of the deaths were those of babies under one yas eara onldd . t heTbhier udleta.atthe-ratel, for as ctshmepwareeedk wiw 19.6 and 33.0 last year. There were on the 17th inst. 130 patients in the hospitals, 91 Of whom were suffering from scarlet fever.</p>
      </div>
