      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURUALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURUALIST.</p>
        <p>• About Dogs. I think it was Mark Twain who said the more he saw of men the better he liked dogs; but, of course, just as there are men and men, so there are dogs and dogs. Mr. H. Greensll, sitting as clerk at Daresbury Petty Sessions last week, asked a police witness why one of these animals —which, the subject of inquiry at the moment, was ult.mately ordered to be destroyed—always attacked postmen, and the constable was unable to explain its strange antipathy to letter-car- riers. In. a previous reference to this Subject, I gave it as tfie common experience of fanciers that there are dogs to which the sight of Ef'Uniform is as the proverbial red rag to a bull, and the troubhsorae beast just now in question was evi- dently one of that kind, the postmen being prob- ably the only uniformed person regularly visit- ing the house. I knew of another of these strange animals, sent to the lethal chamber only the other day, that was lashed into ungovernable Airy .whenever a stranger passing by made a little extra noise. A whistling boy or the clatter of a pair of clogs would so excite him that even the owner of me dog was afraid to interfere. Some years ago I was bitten on the leg by a dog while on my way to a certain fire station. The superintendent of the brigade promptly re- ported the circumstance to the police, and tne offending animal was destroyed through their representations. I was surprised at the action of the chief of the brigade, as the dog belonged to one of his own men; but 1 sunsequently learned that it had previously bitten the super- intendent himself, and that he had ever since been on the 100 K-out for a decent excuse to get yid of it. After all, there are too many dogs. So I and good reason to think on Sunday, when I came upon a number of mischievous lads amusing tnemselves by setting a dog to chase and frighten tne farm live stock in an isolated field. The Tramp Problem.</p>
        <p>At the same time there are householders whose pretence as to a savage dog on the premises is eimply bluff—and small blame to them. Over the gate leading to the backyard one may read the solemn warning", "Beware of the dog," re- minding knowing ones of the characteristic alteration which '2om Hood once made, causing the legend to read "Where be the dog?" How- ever, tne tramps whom it is deigned to keep at a distance generally give such places a wide berth. 1 noticed from last week's Borough Police Court news that one sturdy beggar who had terrorised the Orford-lane lovality and as- saulted a constable, was sent to prison for a paltry 11 days. All- own view is that, in a crisis such as the present, the drunkard, the thief, and the mouc her deserve no consideration whatever. I would treat as alien enemies the besotted wretch who now wastes time and money in self- indulgence, the heartless criminals who rob their fellows and commit other anti-social acts, and the professional cadgers and " griddlers " ,who habitually prey upon generous working men and their open-handed wives. I have lived in two industrial towns where "padding kens " were rather numerous, and I have studied at first-hand the ways of these pariahs and para- sites. I know something of the freemasonry whereby so many of them are enabled to in- ' dulge in daily brigandage with impunity. I have discovered among them broken-down uni- versity men, brought by drink to the lowest depths of degradation, and in my hearing they have gloried in. their shame. As a brother scribe once wrote of the padding ken, here the blind see, the lame walk, and the deaf and dumb hold long and acrimonious debate. He proceeds to tell— and any experienced member of the local police . force could give corroborative testimony in abundance—how he heard two tramps relate their experience in such a kitchen. one evening. They had worked the streets from door to door as genuine unemployed during the morning, and made 10s. 9d. each. In the afternoon they changed coats, tied their trousers round below the knee as navvies, and went through the same district singing: "We've got no work to do." _</p>
        <p>This brought thea-a in a further ss. 6d. each, making 16s. 3d. for the day, which was not bad pay for dodging work. Have my readers never zioticed how certain a wet and miserable day is to bring out these imposters, whining some dole- ful hymn and making as sorry an exhibition as possible? This street-singing business always pays best when there is a drizzling rain, and at the end of such a day there are some wonder- fully " tasty " and substantial meals at the padding ken, at the expense of the kindly, but mistaken, dispensers of alms indiscriminately. These may safely take my assurance that they are well known in the beggars' haunts, and are there ridiculed as fools and blind. " How_We Die." Some of the causes of death in Warrington are set forth in the weekly report of the Medical Officer of Health. The jokers have a habit of adding others. For example : "It was beautiful —marvellous! The whole operation was per- formed without a flaw, and at every turn con firmed the absolute accuracy of the diagnosis. It was wonderful !" "Indeed," remarked the man whom the doctor was addressing. "And the patient—?" "Oh, well, course, the patient died; but it was a beautiful operation!" Doctors are capable of telling with huge en- joyment some entertaining stories against them- selves. For instance: "I was rung up pretty late one night by a peasant from an outlying village, fifteen miles away. It was in the days before I had a car. The wind was blowing hor- ribly, the rain was sweeping against the house, and it ivas deadly cold. The peasant asked me, rather shame-facedly, if I would come and see his mother; I invited him to come in; I gave him a glass of whisky. 'Patrick,' I said, your mother is a very old woman.' 'I know that doctor,' he admitted. 'She's over eighty, Patrick.' 'She is all that, doctor.' 'And noth- ing that I could do to-night would be of the smallest use to her.' 'Shure, doctor,' said he, 'I know very well it's the truth you're telling me; but me poor mother, do you see, would have me come and fetch you because she does not iv ant to die a natural death.' " The Municipal Elections. An old Yorkshire friend of mine who has done useful research and historical work declares that to-day there is less personal liberty in towns "suffering" under bylaws than there was in the autocratic days of Henry -VII. So there! Pro- fessor Marsh is another pessimist. In a letter a z. while ago he wrote "Although the measures by which speedy and wholesale improvements in the health of the• -community could be secured are perfectly well- known our advance resemble the scarcely per- ' coptible crawl of a glacier." I commend these expressions to the considera- tion of local candidates for municipal honours --or rather, I submit them with all respect and deference, without troubling to add any poor opinion of my own. On the Word of an Angler. The salmon stopped The trout to say, " Yonder's an angler Coming this way; And he'll try to hook you On his line, A.nci swear to his friends " You were eight pound nine!" Then the trout replied— Let's swim up higher; We'd be safer away .From such so. gross exaggerator of the truth." These lines convey a sugge'stio'n 'which may be serviceable to members of certain local public bodies—for, f o "'arse, there is no insinuation against the organised anglers in these parts. To once mere cite Mr. Carter Platts, that old the angler's truth is not in him is angler's leading char- a absurdeteris ic fictionb e in gabtohuat h t tth all exploded now, and the golfer bears the un- disputed ; perennial struc- ture palm for a ready and city for the elongation and elaboration of a " primrose by the simpleof vividfact A river's brim, a yellow primrose is to 1.,; —an; and it is nothing more "—that is the angler, but ex- pose the same modest primrose by the bunker's brae to the fervid golfer, and by the time he has ' done the round and got back to the chtbhouse, it will have blossomed into a complete.horticul- tural show, with athletic sports and a brass band contest chucked in. As for our anglers, take "The Examiner" report of last week's achievements in connection with the Warrington Working, Men's _ t aas- Aoci tiOn:R.A. Centre—a plain, unvarnished ale, usual i a perfectly frank and manly avowal?f comparative failure, leading the frivolous i°Wingcr a it - • without excuse. But what will my 001 f readers have to say to all this? Ah, well, per- haps there is a side of the question which- - to be continued in our next or at some more convenient seasoy</p>
        <p>Al.</p>
      </div>
