      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>IN THE COURTS. BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS. BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. Before the Mayor (in tile chair),- Aldermen Smethurst and Bolton, Messrs. S. Wright, W. Roberts, W. Furness and G. Pilling. The Monopolist.—James McCann staying at a lodging-house in Barbauld-street, was charged with having been driink and disorderly in Stan- ley-street.—lt was stated that prisoner, who.was drunk, went down the street swinging a bucket round, thus monopolising a large part of the thoroughfare. When arrested he became yery violent.—A fine of ss. and costs was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor IL Roberts (in the chair), and Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, J. Rymer Young and T. Locker.</p>
        <p>Alleged False Pretences.—Albert Merchant, labourer, of 149, Great Moor-street, Bolton, was charged with, between October 17 and 19, obtain- ing food and lodging to the value of 55., by means of .false pretences, from Mary Alice Ingham, of 5, White-street.—The Chief Con- stable, in asking for a remand for,seven days, said that the prisoner went tp the house of the prosecutrix on Saturday afternoon and told her that he was starting work as a motor mechanic at Messrs. Bellian and Davies's establishment. She believed the statement and provided him with food and lodging until Monday. On that day he was to have started work, but he did not go. He had previously been promised a job at the works on his stating that he could drive a motor. He had triekto obtain money from the firm, but they were too wide-awake.—The remand was granted. Tired of Her Life.—Ellen Radbone, married woman, 9, Stanley-street, was charged with at- tempting to commit suicide by jumping into the River Mersey near Bridge Foot on the previous ,afternoon.—She was committed to the Work- house hospital on a 14 days' order.—P.C. Man- son, who brought the woman to the river bank, said that she was lying in the, mud a couple of feet from the water's edge. He had to go in the mud up to his knees in order to rescue her. and when he got hold of her she said "Oh, let me drown myself; I am tired of my life !" An Unnatural Mother.—Mary Harris, a char- woman, 19, Beswick-street, was charged with using threats ,towards Elizabeth Mary Cotton, on October 17th, and also with using obscene language in Eldon-street on the same date. She was further charged with assaulting Ethel Harris, her daughter, on October 10th.—Mrs. Cotton gave evidence with regard to the threats and the obscene language.—Ethel Harris said that her mother had been drinking all the day on which the assault was committed. At 10.30 at night she was drunk, and asked witfiess, to go for more beer. Witness refused, whereupon the prisoner knocked her down and kicked her several times. She also struck her with the back of a chair, and the neighbours had to come in to her assistance. On Monday of this week her mother threw a bottle at her, but it broke against the wall. She always treated witness badly when she was in drink.—Questioned by the Chief Constable, witness admitted that her mother was hardly ever out of drink.—The Chief Constable said that Alderman Smethurst had done his best Tor the woman, but it was all to no purpose. She was a terror to the neighbour- hood, and her cruelty had caused the daughter to suffer much hardship.—For the threats pris- oner was bound over for six months to keep the peace and ordered to find two sureties in £5 each. For the assault she was committed to prison for one month and was told that if she failed to find the necessary sureties she would be committed for a further two months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. S. W. Hayward (in the chair). Alder- man Smethurst, and Messrs. C. Jackson</p>
        <p>Holmes and W. T. Williams.</p>
        <p>A Pension in .Jeopardy.—Wm. Aitken, a cab driver, staying at a lodging-house in Barbauld- street, was charged with stealing a shirt, of the value of 3s. 11d.—He admitted the offence.— The Chief Constable said the man was a life pensioner from the Army. The facts were that the shirt was hung out to dry at the rear of Arpley-street. The owner saw it safe on the line at 2.30 in the afternoon, but at 3 o'clock it was missing. She reported the loss to the police, and P.C. Green was detailed to make inquiries. He visited the pawnshops and found the shirt had been pledged with Messrs. Briggs and Co. for a shilling by the prisoner. The Chief Con- stable said the man was last before the Court on August 20th, when he was charged with felony. He was then bound over to come up for judgment if called upon, the Bench feeling that if they sent him to prison it would jeopard- ise his pension of Is. id. a day.—The Bench im- posed a fine of 205., with the alternative of 14 days' hard labour.</p>
        <p>Must Have a Short Memory.—When James Connor was put in the dock charged with drun- kenness, the Chief Constablesaid that the man was so fuddled when brought into the Police Station that he did not even know the town he was in. That argued a very bad memory on hiS part, because only at the beginning of the week he was before the Bench and was fined ss. and costs.—Prisoner was fined Bs. 6d. " Beats the German Lie Factory."—Sitting as a Children's Court, the magistrates had before them a fifteen-year-old lad, who was charged with stealing three brushes, of the value of Is. 6d., the property of Messrs. Peers and Mor- ton, of Market-street.—The Chief Constable, in asking that the case be adjourned for a week, said that the boy had been employed by the firm in question for about three weeks, and during that period several brushes had been missed. The boy was a fearful liar; in fact, he turned out lies in a manner that beat the German lie factory. (Laughter.)—The adjournment asked for was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. J. Rymer Young .(in the chair). A. J. Jolley, W. Roberts, W. T. Williams,' and Dr. Naden. _</p>
        <p>One Way of Storing the Blankets.—Elizabeth Parker, a married woman, of 3, Stoney-yard. and Harriett Grant, of 23, Bank-street, were charged with being concerned together in the theft of four bed quilts and three blankets. valued at 18s., the property of Mary Rogerson, a lodging-house keeper, of 23, Bank-street.—The Chief Constable said that Grant had been em- ployed by Mrs. Rogerson as deputy at the lodg- ing-house, and Parker was a friend of hers. In August last Grant packed up a number of bed quilts and other bed clothing and told Mrs. Rogerson that she was putting them away dur- ing the hot weather. Since that time she had apparently been sending Parker to pawn the articles; a small portion of the money thus ob- tained being given to Parker.—Prisoners, who pleaded guilty, were each fined 10s. 6d. inclusive, or 14 days. Stole from a Soldier's Wife.—Sarah Maloney, a married woman, was charged with stealing between 11.10 p.m. on Tuesday, October 20th, and 7.45 a.m. the following morning, from a bedroom in a lodging-house, 9, Hill-street, Is. 4d. in cash, the property of Mary Langley.—Asked to plead, he said, "I am guilty of finding it on the floor, but not guilty of stealing it."—The Chief Con- stable said that the husband of the woman from whom the money was stolen was away with Kitchener's Army. On Tuesday afternoon Mrs. Langley went to the local headquarters of the Patriotic Fund and obtained a grant of ss. She then went back to the lodging-house and paid 2s. 6d. for her lodgings and is. 6d. for food. She gave the prisoner 2d., and afterwards lent her 6d. Prisoner went to bed drunk and later on prosecutrix retired to rest, having in her pos- session is. 4th, which she had wrapped up in a handkerchief and placed at the foot of her bed for security. During the night the prosecutrix woke up and found the prisoner lying across the foot of her bed. She asked her what she Was doing and cleared her off the bed. Subsequently she searched for the handkerchief and the money, and found them missing.—The prisoner, who between 1903 and 1909 was before the Court on forty-one occasions, was sentenced to two months' imprisonment with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>UNITED RUSSIA.</head>
        <p>UNITED RUSSIA.</p>
        <p>Professor Milyoukov, the distinguished Rus- sian Liberal, explains the Russian view of the war. The people have, he says, entered the struggle united. They recognise the enemy's strengtl. but are determined to win and con- fident .of their powers. Russian °Pillion demands that Germany shall be deprived of the means to endanger European peace again. The incorporation in a re-united Poland of Prussia's Polish -territories and the setting free of the minor nationalities in Austria- Hungary might be followed by the entry into the German Empire of Austria's German pro- vinces.</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>EXAMINER</p>
        <p>SCHOLARS' TEETH.</p>
        <p>iduontion Committee To Appoint</p>
        <p>Whole Tims Dentist.</p>
        <p>PARENTAL PREJUDICE GONE.</p>
        <p>The care of the teeth of the school children of the town is amongst the many duties that come within the scope of the Education Com- mittee, and, recently, the Medical Inspection Sub-committee of that body has been in con- sultation with the dentists of the town on the subject, with the result that they submitted the following resolution to the parent body for consideration at their monthly meeting on Monday evening : "That the Health Committee be requested to advertise for a whole-time dentist at a com- mencing salary at the rate of £250 per annum, and that, in the meantime, authority be given to the Director of Education (Mr. J. Moore Murray) to make arrangements for dental in- spection and treatment to be continued." Mr. KINGDON, in proposing the adoption of the minute, said that at a previous meeting it was decided to approach the dentists of the 'town to see whether the committee should go in for a whole-time officer or should make arrange- ments to carry on as heretofore with part-time dentists, only, having more of them. The Director of Education and the Medical Officer took the necessary steps to get in touch with the dentists, and the matter was settled for the committee, because those two gentlemen ascer- tained that the local dentists did not feel able to undertake the work. It meant a good deal of time—at least two hours a day—and the committee would quite understand that busy professional men would pause before they took on work of the kind required by the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>LOCAL DENTISTS SUPPORT THE PROPOSAL.</head>
        <p>LOCAL DENTISTS SUPPORT THE PROPOSAL.</p>
        <p>That body had done its duty in trying to work with the local dentists as far as they could, and those gentlemen recognised the fact and sup- ported the proposal now brought forward. Personally, he was glad the decision had been arrived at in that way; it marked a milestone in the life of the clinic and one of the out- standing facts they would look back to in the history of the committee was that in October, 1914, they took the plunge and went in for a whole-time dentist. The immediate increase in expense that would be entailed was, practi- cally, inconsiderable. Hitherto the cost of a part-time dentist had been about £B4 per annum, and, assuming that they had then such gentlemen working for them, it would be seen that the expense would be about the same as that of a whole-time officer. Dr. Fox said that the change was not pro- posed because the sub-committee did not think the work was well done. On the contrary, it had been excellently done. The reason for the suggested change was that the work had got beyond the scope of any man having other work to do, as would be seen when he stated that 3,376 children were examined in the first six months of this year, and that 1,528 of them required treatment, no fewer than 622 children attending the clinic for six months on end. He was very much in favour of the three dentists in the town being given the opportunity of doing the work, but they,,tfiemselves, said that it was not convenient, or possible, for them to do it, so the committee were left with no alter- native save to take the course recommended. With regard to the question of salary, he was not quite sure whether they would be able to find a suitable man for £250, but he thought • they might try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>WHAT ABOUT THE EYESIGHT?</head>
        <p>WHAT ABOUT THE EYESIGHT?</p>
        <p>Supporting the resolution, Dr. JOSEPH said he regretted that the work could not be carried on by the dentists alreiady in the town. lie would like to ask whether any arrangement had been made with regard to the holder of the office under the committee practising in the town, because it was possible that, after eighteen months or two years, the gentleman might throw up tlie post and start practising on his own account. He thought whoever might be appointed should be restricted in that respect for a considerable period, say, five years at least. Mr. KINGDOX assured the Doctor that that would be specified in the advertisement. Councillor BROADHURST raised the question of a permanent official to examine the eyesight of the children.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Uesos referred to the fact that when the dental work was first undertaken by the com- mittee it raised a good deal of resentment among the parents. They did not quite know what it meant. They, as children, had had bad teeth and toothache, and they thought that the dental work in the schools was an unnecessary inter- ference with the liberty of the parent. (Laugh- ter.) But all that resentment had utterly dis- appeared, thanks, largely, to the tactfulness of the man who had done the dental work. He felt that the committee ought to know that. The wonderful skill and kindliness of disposition of Mr. Taylor had entirely removed all objections; so much so, that the clinic had become abso- lutely popular—(laughter)—and parents, who had no right to them, came for cards in order that they might take their children there to be attended to for toothache..'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>SICKNESS VERY PREVALENT.</head>
        <p>SICKNESS VERY PREVALENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH suggested that the com- mittee might consider with the Health Com- mittee as to whether any means could be found of abating the sickness so prevalent at the pres- ent time. He further suggested a report on the matter before the next meeting.</p>
        <p>Replying to the discussion, Mr. E.INGDON said that it was gratifying to find the committee talo- mg such an interest in clinic work. He assured them that the sub-committee concerned would not be backward in bringing forward proposals if they received adequate support from the mem- bers, but at present they felt that they must move slowly, but surely. He did not think he could hold out any immediate prospect of their taking up eye treatment, but he did agree that, sooner or later, they would have to do so. The matter would not be lost. sight of. As to the question of epidemic diseases, raised by Mr. Fairclough, he agreed that there did seem to be an enormous number of such cases at present, but the matter was one which came entirely under the Health Committee. The committee's recommendation was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>THOSE AWFUL PAINS IN THE BACK.</head>
        <p>THOSE AWFUL PAINS IN THE BACK.</p>
        <p>Generally mean deadly kidney disease</p>
        <p>Pains in your back are Nature's warning that your kidneys are out of order. Take heed at once, and prevent the symptoms from develop- ing, and do not leave it until it is too late, or you will find, as many other poor sufferers have found, that you are stricken with deadly kidney disease, which carries off thousands of English men and women every year. Get a box of Be Witt's Kidney and Bladder Pills. Those wonderful little pills which are especially prepared for kidney and bladder trouble go straight to the kidneys and affected parts. That is the secret Of their amazing success. Be Witt's Kidney and Bladder Pills are so finely compounded that they pass from the stomach straight to the kidneys, and from thence into the bladder. Now you know the reason why Be Witt's give such amazingly quick relief EVERY time and why a permanent cure follows in almost every case. Do not be put ,off with ordinary kidney or backache pills, because you don't know and you have no proof that they have touched the kidneys: When you take De Witt's Kidney and Bladder Pills you know beyond all doubt that they have gone straight to the affected parts. This is positively proved to you when you see the blue-coloured urine. Go to your chemist and get a box of DE wiTT9 S</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>Kidney &amp; Bladder Pills Nature's _Safest Remedy. •</head>
        <p>Kidney &amp; Bladder Pills Nature's _Safest Remedy. •</p>
        <p>• - _ which are especially prepared for kidney trouble. The price of De Witt's—sold only in large blue and gold boxes—is 2/9 and 4/6 per box. You can depend on getting what yln ask for at, every one of Boot's 566 shops, who sell and strongly recommend De Witt's Kidney and Bladder Pills. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>DisTR cT Ews</head>
        <p>DisTR cT Ews</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>Band of Hope.—The opening entertainment of the session in connection with the Hill Cliff° and Stockton Heath United Band. of Hope was held in the Hill Chile schoolroom on Monday even- ing, when the Rev. George Curtis presided, and the Rev. J. S. Chedburn gave an address on " Weeds." An enjoyable programme was sub- mitted by members and friends.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Church Anniversary.—Anniversary services were held at this.church on Sunday, the preacher for the day being the Rev. T. Galland Hartley (ex-chairman of the Liverpool district), who delivered two eloquent and instructive ser- mons to good congregations. The choir rendered the anthems "Daughter of Zion," "0 praise God in His holiness," and "The heavens are telling," in a very creditable manner, under the conductorship of Mr. P. E. Penketh. Mr. T. Brereton gave a very effective rendering of the solo part. Mr. G. A. Whitfield presided at the organ. The collections, which were for the trust funds, were very satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>TIED-LANE LIGHTING.</head>
        <p>TIED-LANE LIGHTING.</p>
        <p>Squabble at District Council Meeting.</p>
        <p>A COUNCILLOR'S APOLOGY.</p>
        <p>There wit-s-i;li;rely discussion with reference to the recent petition from the Trustees of Hill Chile Baptist Chapel for the proper lighting of Red-lane, Appleton, at the Runcorn Rural District Council meeting on Tuesday. At the last meeting of the Council the petition had been handed to the Deputy Clerk by Councillor Mclntyre, and the Council decided to accede to the request of the petitioners. On Tuesday, when the minutes of the previous meeting came up for confirmation, Councillor Britch said he objected to the paragraph referring to street lighting at Red-lane. The matter would be a charge on the parish of Appleton, which he represented, and it had been brought forward in an irregular sort of manner. The Chairman: The paragraph is a record of what took place at that meeting. You do not dispute that what is written did take place?</p>
        <p>Councillor Britch : Oh, no! It is quite cor- rect.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: You cannot object to it then on the ground that it is not a correct record. You can have your remedy by giving notice to rescind resolution. .</p>
        <p>Councillor Britch I give notice of; motion to that effect. . Councillor Hallows: I think the Parish Coun- cil ought to consider it. The Chairman: Well, he has given notice. Councillor Listev-liaye: I would suggest that no action be taken on the resolut..en passed until this has been settled. The Chairman: Of course it e on't. Councillor Mclatyre : May I make a perst.nal statement?</p>
        <p>The Deputy Clerk : That will come on later. The Chairman: You will be able to speak on it 'then.</p>
        <p>Councillor Mclntyre: I only wish to make a personal The Chairman then rapped his mallet on the table for order. The Deputy Clerk read a letter from Mr. W. Hough, of Stockton Heath, in which he stated that he thought an apology was clue to Coun- cillor Britch, their representative, for the slight cast upon him. The 'Trustees of the chapel and residents in the parish desired that the petition should be handed to Coun- cillor Britch for'qlresentation to the Council. lie (Mr. Hough) should have handed it to Councillor Britch, but failecl to see him. Ile gave it to Councillor Mclntyre, who promised to hand it over to Councillor Britch. The petition was never handed to Councillor Britch; thus were the wishes of all the signatories dis- regarded, as though the Trustees and others ignored Councillor Britch and thought him unworthy of their confidence. "I therefore humbly apologise;" the writer concluded, "for the slight cast upon him, although no fault of mine." _ . _</p>
        <p>The following resolution was also submitted by the Appleton Parish Council :—" That in the opinion of this Council any petition from the ratepayers of the parish to the Rural Dis- trict Council asking for improvements entailing a special charge on the ratepayers of the parish should be presented to the District Council through their representative, so that if neces- sary he may consult the members of the Parish Council as to what action it would be advisable for him to take." .</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman: Have you any observations to make on these communications? The whole thing can be thrashed out. Councillor Mclntyre: I would like to make a personal observation. This petition was handed to me by Mr., Hough. He had failed to meet Councillor Britch, and he asked me to take the petition to the District Council. It was handed in through the Clerk long before I saw Coun- cillor Brach. I had no idea I was committing any breach of 'faith with the parishioners of Appleton, o 7 casting any reflection on Councillor Britch. I had not the slightest intention to usurp his position, and I say that if I have done anything which appears to he wrong, it has been done in perfect innocence. I am sorry Councillor Britch has taken it in a personal light, or that the parishioners of Appleton have taken it in a personal light. It was not my intention to do anything of the sort, and I offer my apologies to Councillor Britch and the signa- tories of the petition. The Chairman: That is a plain expression by Councillor Mclntyre, and I hope if there has been any ill-feeling in the matter that it will be entirely evaporated. Councillor Mclntyre assures us there was no slight on Councillor Britch, or anyone, and he has made a handsome apology. Councillor Britch: I accept Councillor Mc- Intyre's apology. A matter of this sort, refer- ring to any special expense on a township, should come before the representative, or at least' the Parish Council, for their consideration, The Chairman: Do not you want the lamps? We shall not meet again for a month, you know. Councillor Britch: Under the circumstances, I withdraw the notice of motion. The Chairman: I am very glad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. R. Higham.—With painful sud- denness the death took place at 2 a.m. on Mon- day of Mrs. Robert Higham, of Farnworth-road, Penketh. The deceased lady, who had been ail- ing for some considerable time, attempted to attend Sankey Church the previous evening, but when half-way there was obliged to return home, as she felt so ill, finally succumbing to a severe heart attack. Well known and well liked, very quiet and unassuming, the deceased will be missed by a large circle of friends. She had resided in Penketh about thirty years. The funeral took place 'on Thursday afternoon. Wesley* Guild.—The opening social of the winter session in connection with the Penketh Wesley Guild was held on Saturday, when over a hundred members and friends attended. A short concert occupied the company during the first half of the evening, light refreshments fol- lowing, and the remainder of the time being given up to games and various mirth-provoking competitions for both seniors and juniors. Those who took part in the concert were: The Misses E. Baker (soprano), and E. Cook (recita- tions),.and Messrs. L. Platt (bass) and A. Coupe (violin).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild Social.—The Rev. 11. T. Dixon presided over a successful social held under the auspices of the Wesley Guild on Wednesday evening. Musical items were given by the Misses E. Done, Clare, R. Tanner, A. Lawton, A. Millington and M. Eustance, and an amusing sketch "Women's Rights and Men's Wrongs" was presented by the Misses L. Done, A. Milling- ton and M. Enhance. After the sketch came games and an amusing hat trimming competi- tion, in which the prizes were won by Mrs. P. Bodell, Miss M. Theodore, and Mr. R. Wallin- tou.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>On the Bench.—The magistrates at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday were Messrs. A. Watkin (in the chair), W. Peter Rylands, J. Coxon, A. Callender, and T. J. Ridgway.</p>
        <p>The "Trinitarian" Movement —A meeting was held on Wednesday evening in the Wesleyan Church in connection with the " Trinitarian" movement, when an address was given by the Rev. Cecil E. Lovely, Mr. Fletcher. of Warring- ton, occupying the chair.</p>
        <p>Death of the Rev. J. H. Pawlyn.--We regret to announce the death of the Rev. James H. Pawlyn, of the Hamlet, Lymm. Mr. Pawlyn had been bedridden for a considerable time. He passed away on Friday. The funeral took place on Tuesday, a memorial service being held in the Lymm Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>Will of the late Mrs. Macread.--Probate of the will of the late Mrs. Jane Macread, who was for many years the licensee of . the Church Inn, Lymm, has just been granted by the Chester Probate Registry to Mr. William Mullitrd, the clerk to the Lymm Urban District Council, and Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, of Warrington, the two executbrs. The estate was sworn at £3,873 13s. gross. Amongst the legacies are £25 to the vicar and churchwardens of Northop Parish Church "towards a fund for augmenting the stipend of the curate or curates," £2O for the purchase of a Masonic banner for the use of the "Earl of Chester" Lodge of Freemasons, Lymm; and £lOO for the cottage hospital at Mold. After payment of several other legacies to relatives and friends, the residue of the estate is equally divided between nieces.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>GUN-SHOT AT MIDNIGHT. Lymm Teamsman's Foolish Act.</head>
        <p>GUN-SHOT AT MIDNIGHT. Lymm Teamsman's Foolish Act.</p>
        <p>The sound of a gun shot near the Lymm Waterworks at nearly midnight on October 3rd caused Sergt. I)avies to make inquiries, and, as a result, at the Petty Sessions on Wednesday, William Harris, teamsman, Water Tower Cot- tages, Lymm, was summoned for not having a licence and also for firing a gun within 50 feet of the highway. The officer, in giving evidence, said that at 11.45 on the night in question he was in the vicinity of the Water' Works, aecompanied by P.C. Gauld, when he saw a flash and heard the report of a gun. He went in the direction from which the sound came, and saw the defendant on the other side of the hedge. He had a double- barrelled gun; and in reply to the witness's ' questions he said he fired the gun to frighten two men who were going along the road. De- fendant was eighteen to twenty feet behind the hedge. It was very quiet at the time, and the report was calculated to alarm a good many people. P.C. Gauld corroborated.</p>
        <p>Defendant asked the Bench if the Sergeant had a right to shoot a gun near the highway. ' The Sergeant said that there was a cartridge in each barrel. One had been fired, and he thought it was safer to discharge the other. Defendant said he did not know that he was doing any harm; he only did it to frighten his son, who was talking about the war, and to make him hurry up. Defendant was fined 55., including costs, for each offence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>COSTLY DRINKS.</head>
        <p>COSTLY DRINKS.</p>
        <p>Three Male-Fide " Travellers " Fined</p>
        <p>Three Irishmen Peter Morton, Bailiff Cottages, Heatley; Peter Burke, Williams' Farm, Warburton; and Patrick McCann, Dunn's Farm, Heatley=appeared at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday to answer sum 4 monses for being on the licensed premises of the Swan Hotel, Bucklow Hill, during pro- hibited hours on Sunday, October 4th, and also with falsely representing themselves to be travellers. Morton was further summoned for giving a wrong address, and the other defendants with giving false names and ad- dresses.</p>
        <p>Defendants pleaded guilty to the offences. Mr. A. Keogh, solicitor, Altrincham, who represented the licensee, Charles E. R,othwell, said that the three defendants went to the hotel on the morning in question and asked to be served. The licensee inquired of them as to where they came from, and they replied "Irlam." He also. asked each man individu- ally, and they all gave the same answer.</p>
        <p>When the Police Came In. The men were perfectly sober and Mr. Both- well had no reason to disbelieve them. Soon after the men had been served two police offi- cers visited the house and asked how many travellers there were inside. Mr. Bothwell replied that there were seven—four from Broadheath and three from Irlam. The offi- cers went into the house, and, after being sat- isfied that the Broadheath men were bona-fide travellers, they went to the three defendants, two of whom then said they came from Altrinc- ham. The licensee remarked: "Why, you told me you caine from Irlam !" and one of them replied: "I did, governor; I told you a lie." The licensee gave evidence bearing out his solicitor's statement. Sergeant Davies also gave corroborative evi- dence, adding that Morton lived two miles 229 yards Oorri the hotel, Burke two miles 229 yards and McCann one mile 1,450 yards. The Chairman (Mr. A. Watkin) said that it was a serious thing for the defendants to re- present themselves as being travellers, and for that offence they would each be fined' 55., in- cluding costs. For giving wrong names they would each be fined 2s. 6d., including costs, and for being on licensed premises during pro- hibited hours they would have to pay a further 2s. 6d. each., including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>DOGS' COSTLY SPORT. Pollee Court Sequel to Sheep- Worrying.</head>
        <p>DOGS' COSTLY SPORT. Pollee Court Sequel to Sheep- Worrying.</p>
        <p>At the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Elias Hirst and his . daughter, Martha Ellen Hirst, of Rock House, Lymm, were summoned for not keeping their two dogs under proper control.</p>
        <p>Harry Odber, butcher, The Grove, Lymm, stated that he rented part of the Rectory Field, and there kept sheep whilst they were waiting to be killed. On October 14 the sheep then in the field were all right at 3.30 in the afternoon, but half an hour later, from information he received, he again visited the field and found that one of the sheep was in a very exhausted condition. It was worse the next morning, and could hardly lift itself up. On the following Friday morning he again went to the field and saw a black retriever dog chasing the sheep. The dog belonged to Miss Hirst. He had pre- viously complained about the dogs chasing the</p>
        <p>sheep. He had chased the black dog away so often that whenever it saw him in the street it commenced to bark.</p>
        <p>Miss Elizabeth Marsh, The Grove, Lymm, said that about 3.45 p.m. on October 14 she saw a sheep jump into the road, two dogs following later. One of the dogs was a ,black retriever and the other a fox terrier.</p>
        <p>Dog Seen Chasing the Sheep.</p>
        <p>Joseph Wilson and William Brace, head gardener and gardener, respectively, at Lymm Hall, both deposed to seeing the black retriever chasing the sheep on October 14th. Alfred Pilkington, a boy employed by Mr. ,Odber, stated that he found the sheep in the road, and P.C. Atherton gave evidence of mak- ing complaints to Miss Hirst concerning her dog. The male defendant said that he occupied part of the Rectory Field. The fencing was in- adequate, and at times Mr. Odber's sheep got into his portion of the field. His dog and his , daughter's were not the only dogs that could I have gone into the field, for all the dogs in the village went there. The Chairman (Mr. A. Watkin) said that the summons against the male defendant would be dismissed, whilst the Bench ordered Miss Hirst's dog to be kept under proper control. Probably, if that order was not complied with, they would have to make an order for the animal to be destroyed. Miss Hirst would have to pay the costs, amounting to £1 173.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>~ SATURDAY, OCTOBER 24, 1914.</head>
        <p>~ SATURDAY, OCTOBER 24, 1914.</p>
        <p>LYMAI'S HOSPITALITY. Hurried Preparations for Receiving Belgian Refugees.</p>
        <p>SIR W. LEVER'S OFFER.</p>
        <p>Once more Lymm has proved that she is not asleep, for, within a very few days, arrange- ments have been completed to accommodate 35 hapless refugees from Belgium. At a meeting of the local National Relief Committee on Sat- urday a letter was read from Sir W. 11% Lever offering the large house, Beechwood, as a home for the refugees. A committee was appointed to make inquiries, and• Mr. T. r W. Dixon. the hon. treasurer, got into communication with residents, and his re- port at a meeting on Tuesday was so satisfac- tory that Sir William's generous offetwas unani- mously accepted. A, committee was appointed, with Mr. A. Watkin as the chairman, and they were so enthusiastic in their self-imposed task, that they immediately applied for 35 refugees. IA letter has been received from Lord Lytton accepting the offer. In the meantime the officers and the com- mittee have worked so untiringly that every- thing was ready last night for the stipulated number of Belgians, who, it is expected, will arrive early next week. A substantial sum has already been given or promised, and the number of weekly subscrip- tions which have been offered for the whole time the refugees will be in Lymm is most en- couraging. Although the gifts of furniture, crockery, &amp;c., havd been very great, many more are needed, and it will redound to the credit of the inhabitants of the district if these additional wants are sup- plied willingly and with promptness until suffi- cient has been contributed to make the 100 refugees the building will accommodate forget something of their horrible and heartrending experiences. All contributions in money or kind will be gladly accepted by Messrs. W. Mullard and Mercer, the hon. secretaries, or by Mr: T. F. W. Dixon, the hon. treasurer.</p>
        <p>Inhabitants will have an opportunity of view- ing the building on Sunday afternoon, when a charge of sixpence each will be made to aid the cause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The usual weekly meeting of the Guild was ,held on Thursday evening, the Rev. Geo. Jackson, of Didsbury, being the preacher. The subject of his address was "Being brave: A message for the home," and in the course of his remarks he said that he wished to speak to the men and women who were playing the coward's part. The fighting spirit, he pointed out, was not necessarily the only form of courage, as the strain on those who did not fight might involve more heroism than was demanded of those who took part in the actual conflict. Let them think of all the heroes who could not fight, but were enduring! This hero- ism found its opportunity always and every- where.</p>
        <p>Relief Funds Concert.—ln. aid of the local war funds a successful cbncert was held in the Wesley Hall on Saturday evening, an excellent programme being submitted by a number of well-known artistes from Warrington, viz.: The Misses Jennie Armstrong and Marie Houghton and Messrs. J. B. Poole, J. Babbitt, A. Trow, A. Dutton and Hopkins. The Orpheus Male Quar- tet also appeared. At the close the Vicar pro- posed a vote of thanks to the artistes, and to Mrs. Duckworth for the trouble she had taken in organising the concert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>PUT GOLD IN YOUR BANK. How Non-Combatants Can Help in National Crisis.</head>
        <p>PUT GOLD IN YOUR BANK. How Non-Combatants Can Help in National Crisis.</p>
        <p>The economic crisis created by the war so far as it involves banking was the subject of Pro- fessor S. J. Chapman's presidential address to the Manchester Bankers' Institute last week. With less than £40,000,000 in one pound and ten shilling notes in circulation, it was evident there was much gold still in the hands of the people. Some of this was hoarded, and it should be realised that those who held it would gain nothing unless a premium on gold appeared, Which was practically out of the question.</p>
        <p>_ Non-combatants , could furnish aid in the national crisis by paying their gold into a bank and taking paper in exchange. A sovereign in the Bank of England was worth a hundred in the pockets of the people. What one would like to see would be the public taking the initiative and chasing any gold that appeared hick into the banks. All that was in the country could be then concentrated in the Bank of England, or at least. recorded periodically in connection with its accounts, and the tangible foundations of our credit, firm already, would become as firm as the Rocky Mountains. Dealing with the problem of credit, ProfeSsor Chapman declared that exports from overseas could not be indefinitely delayed, and, in par- ticular, harvests had to be shifted and marketed, however great might be the slump in their values. This trade that must come must ride on bills, and its financing must automatically liquidate outstanding obligations and indirectly furnish the means for the financing of trade in the opposite direction. - Many unconventional things might have to be done to patch up economic dislocation before the war was over. The Government would re- ceive much weighty support in'aiming first at such restoration of normal procedure as was possible. One step in its policy was the with- drawal of the general moratorium. Another step should be to get the stock and produce ex- changes very effectively at work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>ECONOMICS OF THE WAR.</head>
        <p>ECONOMICS OF THE WAR.</p>
        <p>The economics of the war were lectured upon by Professor Chapman at the Ancoats New Islington Hall, Manchester, on Monday, and he expressed the opinion that if the war was a moderately long one—say, about two years— there was no reason why there should be any undue stoppage of British industries, thanks to the prompt action of the Government. • We should all be poorer for years after the war was over, for all war was a sheer waste. In the economic field we had nothing to gain but a great deal to lose. The war must be seen through despite its heavy cost, and the cost would not be so great to us as to Germany.</p>
        <p>DESTITUTE ALIENS, 0 Miss Broadbent Objects to Lieh'S at the Workhouse.</p>
      </div>
