        <p>Cunfiffe.</p>
        <p>BELGIAN REFUGEES.</p>
        <p>The Committee -oit-the—Wa;ri;gi-on Football Club have decided to allow Belgian Refugees in the town free admission to their matches.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY. WARRINGTON v. HUNSLET. At Hunslet</p>
        <p>Taberner, Tranter, 13riuishaw, Baker. Dainteth Young. s s _ ,</p>
        <p>Cox, Fearnley, Green, _Criteilley, Mather,</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. v. WARDLEY.</p>
        <p>These teams meet at Wardley to-day, the train leaving Warrington Central Station at 1.4.5. The following will represent the Recs.:— F. Horrocks; H. Povey, A. Bramhall, A. Hor- rocks, and J. Rowlinson; G. Warren and J. Gallop; J. Whittaker, J. Brandwood, A. Penn, F. Potter, E. Smith, and A. N. Other. Re- serves: W. Stretford (back) and J. Dumbell (forward).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. FINANCIAL POSITION OF CLUBS. A Scheme to Cut Down Expenses..</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION. FINANCIAL POSITION OF CLUBS. A Scheme to Cut Down Expenses..</p>
        <p>At a special general meeting Of Northern Union Clubs, held on Tuesday in Manchester to consider tlap present financial position of the clubs, it was resolved that club committees should effect economies in the management of clubs in every possible way; that wages of all League players should be reduced at least 25 per cent., the reduction to take effect from November Ist and to continue until the League decides otherwise; that, in order that this reso- lution shall be observed, every club shall forthwith send to the Management Committee a statenAent of wages payable to their respec- tive players under agreements now in force, and a further statement showing the amount of reduction; that the referees' fees for the 're- mainder of the season shall be as follows: League matches in the same county 128. 6d., League matches in adjoining county 155., Com- bination referees 7s. 6d.; and that the meeting recommends that contributions to the British Playing Fields' Society for this season shall be suspended. LANCASHIRE SENIOR CUP DRAW. Warrington to Play Salford. _ _ _</p>
        <p>At a meeting in Manchester the draw for the second round of the Lancashire Senior Cup, to be played next Saturday, was made as follows: Warrington v. Sa Swinton ,v. Runcorn. Rochdale v. Widnes. Leigh v. Wigan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. RYLANDS' RECS. V. BROUGHTON CENTRAL.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. RYLANDS' RECS. V. BROUGHTON CENTRAL.</p>
        <p>These teams met in the First Division of the above League on the Gorsey-lane enclosure on Saturday afternoon, before a very fair attendance. The visitors came with an un- beaten record. Ryland?, who were without Stretford and Griffiths, won the toss; and Mears kicked off for Broughton. Rylands' made tracks for the line, S. Evans saving grandly. Still, the " Wiremen " would not be denied, and soon afterwards Potter scored a try, the goal-kick failing. The visitors played well after this reverse, and kept the home team busy defending. From a pack formed in Rylands' half the visitors started a passing movement, but H. Povey, intercepting, raced away, and, outpacing all opposition, scored, Warren goaling. Half-time arrived with the score: Rylands', 1 goal 2 tries (8 points): Broughton Central, nil. Whittaker commenced the second half for the home team, but Mears returning, Brough- ton, who were playing well at this point, soared, the goal-kick failing. A. Horrocks next scored for Rylands', Povey goaling. After this the game got a bit rough, Warren and Aspinall, of the visitors, being given their marching orders. A. Penn scored for the home team, Povey again adding the extra points. Rylands' again attacked and Brough- ton were granted a free-kick for obstruction. A difference of opinion as to whose ball it was ensued and Gallop was ordered from the field. Smith next scored a good try for Rylands', Povey converting, Horrocks, Povey and War- ren played well for the home team, and S. Evans and Mears were the pick of the visitors. The final score was :—Rylands', 4 goals 5 tries (23 points); Broughton Central, 1 try (3 points).</p>
