      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Working Men's Association.</p>
        <p>NA. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>A fair number of members were out last week-end and results were a little better than the week previous. Again Beeston recorded the best of the catches taken, Messrs. Hill and Moores being responsible for fine bags of roach. The Gowy fished better than last week, some fine roach being taken, but not heavy enough to disturb those already on the board. Norton had not the usual muster, and we are yet waiting for an 8-oz. roach. EXECUTIVE COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Held at the Clock Face Hotel, Burnley. Mr. R 0. Atherton presided. Representatives were present from Crewe, Bolton, Wigan, Preston, Farnworth, Makerfield, Warrington, Horwich, Leyland, Little Lever, Oldham, St. Helens, Hollinwood, and Salford, along with the Vice- president, Treasurer and Secretary. The cost of 28s. incurred by Crewe Centre in Shying fish when the Shropshire Union Canal was drawn off was ordered to be paid. The report of the annual match, showing a surplus of 6s. 4d., was approved. The report •of the English championship match (in which the N.A.A. team was fifth out of 20 teams) was received with approval. Leeds team were the winners.</p>
        <p>The charges for professional services re the Ribble ProvisiOnal Order and the River Glowy lease, by Alderman F. A. Horridge, solicitor to the association, were approved ps very reason- able and ordered to be paid. '1 The Warrington delegate supported a notice of motion for the annual meeting with refer- ence to reducing the expenses of the Council meetings.</p>
        <p>Warrington has also sent in a notice of motion for the next annual meeting to be held in Warrington. Our delegate will read his report on the whole of the business interesting to the Warrington Centre this week. From what we can gather,. Warrington has some business to do before the season ends that will be beneficial to them. A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. PARTINGTON v. ALTRINCHAM ST. MARGARET'S.</p>
        <p>These teams met.............. of the former club, before a small number of spectators. The visitors won the toss and decided to play down hill, with the sun at their backs. The game opened in brisk fashion, and the visitors at once pressed, when the home backs defended well. After ten minutes' play Cowsill sent in a low shot, which the goalkeeper partially saved, and the ball rolled over the line. The home team made several spirited attempts on the visitors' citadel, but failed to capture it. The visitors were awarded a penalty kick, which was taken by Brown, but his shot skimmed over the bar, and directly after Worral sent in a very good shot, which hit the cross-bar and entered the net -off the goalkeeper's back. Cowsill scored a third goal for the visitors. Interval: against the home team. _</p>
        <p>In the second portion the game was more evenly contested, and after 15 minutes' play Walton scored for the home team. From now until the finish the game was keenly contested, and both custodians had to save shots, but no further score was registered. Result: St. Mar- garet's, 3 goals; Partington, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. PADGATE.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. PADGATE.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Padgate on Saturday. The home team won the toss and they at once made play on the left, but were soon checked by the visitors' backs. The Institute broke away in nice style, and after some good play by their forwards missed, an easy chance of scoring, the centre-forward shooting badly when he had only the goalkeeper to beat. After the game had been in progress twenty-five minutes Padgate opened the scoring, their centre-forward doing the needful. Shortly after this they again dashed in and scored a second time, after some good combined, work by the forwards. At half-time the score was: Padgate, 2 goals; Emmanuel, nil. After the interval Emmanuel put more life into their work, but could not score. The visitors had hard lines on several occasions, the /goalkeeper saving at the expense of fruit- less corners. The Padgate forwards raced away and scored a third goal. The visitors played a good game, but their forwards were nkinthei I r shooting, the last few inlga,thfrowig n chm ae after chance away. minutes Padgate scored two more goals. The home goalkeeper w played a good game. Padgate ran out winners by five goals to nil.</p>
        <p>PADDINGTON RESERVES V. GRAPPEN- HALL ST. WI-LFRID'S.</p>
        <p>- These teams met at Grappenhall on Satur- day, when T. Woodhead (Stockton Heath) and H. Wilkinson (Orford) made their first appear- ance for the visitors. In the I;rst )I.llf pad_ dington put on three goals, St. Wilfrid's failing to respond!' soeuinmdtedu In the second half, however, they managed t up one found themselves defeated by point, but when the final whistle four goals to one. T. Woodhead scored three goals for Paddington, and, altogether, gave such an excellent show, on this his first appearance with the team, that he has‘been selected to play in -to-day's League match against Latch- ford Baptists. The other new man, Wilkinson, also made a creditable debut.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>ATHLETIC CLUB_ SECRETARY ,</head>
        <p>ATHLETIC CLUB_ SECRETARY ,</p>
        <p>RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>At a special meeting of the Warrington Ath- letic Club, held on Thursday evening, Mr. Reuben Bennett asked to be relieved of the many duties incidental to the office of hon. secretary and treasurer, as he wished to apply his full energy in connection with the local Territorials. Mr. Bennett was presented with a suitably inscribed gold medal, the gift being handed to him, on behalf a the members, by the captain, Mr. William Bolton. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES STEAMSHIP CO. Disastrous Effect of the War.</head>
        <p>THE LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES STEAMSHIP CO. Disastrous Effect of the War.</p>
        <p>The report of the directors of the 04th year's working of the company's steamers states:— The season opened satisfactorily, and there was every prospect of good results, hut, unfor- tunately, owing to European complications and the subsequent declaration of war, business was for a time completely paralysed. Until public confidence had been partially restored the com- pany's steamers suffered very severely from the reduction in the number of passengers carried. When the first period of panic had passed it was then apparently too late for holiday-makers to make arrangements, and the passenger traffic not being sufficient to justify the retention of La Marguerite on the Liverpool station, she was withdrawn early in September. The service of the company has, been con- ducted with regularity, notwithstanding the unusual conditions, and the steamers have been well kept up. , A contract was placed in December last and agreement signed in February this year for a geared turbine steamer to take-her position on the station for this year's passenger season. She was suCcessfully launched and named the St. Beiriol, but delivery having been delayed by unforeseen circumstances beyond the date agreed, and the declaration of war having been made before the vessel was completed, it was decided not to place her on the service until next Easter. After deducting the sum of £4,181 ss. 4d. for depreciation on steamers and other property of the company the amount available for dividend and other purposes, including the balance brought forward from last year, is ,e 4,890 195., out of which the directors recommend a pay- ment of 5 per cent, per annum, free of income- tax, cartying forward a -balance of ,t 1,705 19s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <p>Fairfield United Rouse Themselves with a Vengeance</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>FINE GAME BY T. BARNABAS' AND BANK QUAY.</head>
        <p>FINE GAME BY T. BARNABAS' AND BANK QUAY.</p>
        <p>On Saturday our clubs were favoured with ideal football weather, and some excellent games were witnessed.</p>
        <p>Fairfield United were in opposition to the Fairfield Athletic, and the former, who were smarting under the effects of a defeat on the previous Saturday, determined to take their re- venge on their neighbours, the Athletic. The game was, in the first portion, very fast and keenly contested, the United proving themselves rather better marksmen and scoring twice before the interval. In the second portion a great change came over the game, as it was soon very evident the Athletic had over-exerted them- selves. After Millward had scored the third goal, the United forwards could not be held by the opposing defence, and inside twenty-five min- utes of the second half they had registered seven goals, ultimately running out winners by nine goals to nil. The Orford White Star continue in the vic- torious career, and in their game with the Y.M.C.A. from Earlestown they had much the best of the game, scoring once in each half. The visiting forwards had many chances, but their inside trio were unable to make any use of them owing to the splendid display of the home half- back line, which is the strongest portion of their team.</p>
        <p>Hollins Green paid a visit to the Mission Club at Earlestown, but they were not able to field their best team, and had to acknowledge defeat by three goals to nil. The meeting of St. Barnabas' and Bank Quay Recs. was as anticipated when these two rivals meet, a very keen and excited contest. The Bees. came out of the ordeal victorious by the odd goal in five. There was a fine crowd of spectators present, who were delighted at the exhibition shown by the teams. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The meeting of the Congregationalists took place on the Elmwood ground, where the home team had to acknowledge defeat again. The dis- play of the home team did not merit such a heavy score as that recorded against them, as their backs displayed erratid form, sometimes good, and then followed slackness, which allowed the visitors to break through and score. At the same time the visitors must be congratulated for their victory, for it is goals that win matches, and they accepted their chances and made them good. The Orford White Sear team accounted for the Y.ALC.A., scoring once in each half. The visit- ors were unable to reply owing to the strong opposition by the Star half-back line. Longford United met the Baptists of Latch- ford, and after a well-contested first half they proved easy winners in the second portion. Paddington played their return fixture with the Central and proved easy winners.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Linesman."</p>
        <p>the home forwards allowed Allen to centre from near the corner-flag. Meachin gained possession and gave J. Smith an easy opening and he had no difficulty in beating Martin. The game was not of a very interesting nature, is the visitors could make but little impression against the home half-back line, and they easily robbed them of the ball. The visitors' defence had a busy time, but they held out under heavy pressure, Chambers playing a very fine game at full-back. Half-time score:— White Star, 1 goal; Y.M.C.A., nil. On re-starting, the visitors now had the slope in their favour and the game was more evenly contested. The home goal was soon in danger, some good centres being sent in by Taylor and Lee, but the inside trio were unable to improve upon them. Denny, overshadowing Riley, pre- vented him from breaking through. The home forwards were being well fed with passes from the half-backs, and Dunbobbin and Allen tested Martin many times, but eventually Smith gained possession inside the penalty area and scored with a low drive. The visitors now made determined attacks on the home goal and gained several corner-kicks, but they proved of no avail. The outside wing men of the visitors played a grand game, and made good openings for the inside trio, but they • lost the chances by bad shooting. Result :—Orford White Star, 2 goals; Earlestown Y.M.C.A., nil.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD BAPTISTS V. LONGFORD UNITED.</p>
        <p>- At Weaste-lane. The visitors won the toss and faced the sun. The opening exchanges were very tame, neither set of players appearing to settle down. The visitors at length began to assert themselves, and by the aid of their left wing got to close quarters, but Sturgess was safe and removed the leather to mid-field. How- ever, the Visitors' display was now more con- vincing, and by good combination they again attacked the home goal, when Sherratt opened the score with a good drive. This success to the visitors was the means of a greatly improved display by both teams, but the visitors' half- back line proved the stronger. Stevens, passing judiciously to his extreme left, enabled Sherratt to again score. Following these reverses there was some keen play, and gradually the Bap- tists forced the visitors on to the defensive, and Pearson, by a splendid individual effort, scored for the home 'team. Give-and-take was the order for the following ten minutes, and then the visitors again became aggressive. Sher- ratt plied Hughes with a good pass, which enabled him to register the visitors' third goal. The home goalkeeper was well beaten. The half-time score was 3 goals against 1 goal, in favour of the visitors.</p>
        <p>Early--in—ibe--;eco—n.d half the Baptists were seen to much better advantage and soon gave trouble to the visitors' defence, Pearson again beating all opposition and scoring the second goal for the home team. Encouraged by this success, the players became very keen, but the visitors gradually gained the upper hand and the home 'defenders could not hold them. Moseley scored the fourth goal. The home team fell away and the United forwards came down again, when Hughes scored a fifth goal. The home team tried hard to gain ground, but their efforts were easily checked by the visitors' backs, and another raid resulted in Sherratt scoring a sixth goal. The Baptists were now a beaten team. Result :—Longford United, 6 goals; Latchford Baptists, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. EARLESTOWN ST.,JOHN'S MISSION v. HOLLINS GREEN.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. EARLESTOWN ST.,JOHN'S MISSION v. HOLLINS GREEN.</p>
        <p>On the Mission ground. Teams : Mission : Massey; Winders and Griffiths; Tay- lor, Eaton and Theobald ; Wallace, Jarrett, A. Eaton, Sherlock and Plumpton. Hollins Green: A. Perrin; Johnson and Clarke; Buckley, Etchells and Cleworth ; Brooks, Cowerbutts, Mellor, Eaves and Atkinson. Referee, S. C. Essenhigh. The visitors won.the toss and defended the top goal, with the sun at their backs. The opening exchanges were not very interesting, as both teams were penalised for fouls, no 'advantage _ _ . _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>beinggained from the ensuing free kicks. Elia- to-end play followed. The visitors' forwards had the home goal at their mercy, but shot very feebly. Then the home team improved in their display, and were soon in the vicinity of the visitors' goal, where A. Eaton made a mess of a splendid opening by shooting over the bar. This relieved the visitors' defence, and a visit was paid to the opposite end, where Massey saved in grand style, 'and with a huge punt enabled his forwards to set up another attack, from which Jarrett opened the score. This success acted as a tonic to the players, and the ball travelled very quickly, but the home team cpntrolled it better than their opponents, and A. Eaton scored the second goal for the Mission. The visitors now made tracks for the home goal, but their forwards finished badly. Mellor let a very , easy opening go by. The interval score was in favour of the Missioir by 2-0. On resuming the Mission made a rush towards the visitors' goal, and were driven back by the defenders, and' the visitors attacked strongly, but failed to pierce the home defence. Follow- ing a period of mid-field play, Taylor registered the Mission's third goal. The visitors tried hard to, reduce Hie arrears, but met a stubborn de- fence. Result: Earlestown Mission, 3 goals; Hollins Green, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>LOCAL " DEBBY 7_ AT PADGATE-</head>
        <p>LOCAL " DEBBY 7_ AT PADGATE-</p>
        <p>LANE.</p>
        <p>The Athletic Are Routed. FAIRFIELD UNITED v. FAIRFIELD ATHLETIC.</p>
        <p>- - These local teams, who play on alternate dates on the same ground at Padgate-lane, met on Saturday in the local League Competition. There was a large crowd of spectators, when the following players turned out:— United: Potter ;•Cox and Woodhouse; Gleave, Bebbington and Ditehtield; Roberts, Scott, Millward, Poulter and S. Woodhouse. Athletic: Warburton; Johnson and Jackson; Leigh, Roberts and Hayes; Woods, Lawless, Clark, Challinor and Bates. Referee, Mr. J. Hadkiss. The United won the toss and played with a slight breeze in their favour. From. the kick- off the United soon settled to their work, and at once made tracks to their opponents' goal, where Warburton saved a difficult shot from Millward, and good play by Roberts for the Athletic, who passed out judiciously to the left, when Bates sent in a stinging shot, which hit the upright and rebounded into play. The gapie was contested very evenly in mid-field during the next fifteen minutes, following which the United attacked very strongly; and S. Woodhouse beat Warburton with a beauty, after twenty-five minutes' play. Nettled by this reverse, the Athletic made a strenuous effort to equalise, but Bebbington and Isis backs proved to be too strong for the opposition, and the leather was sent well up the field:- Mill- ward, receiving from the right wing, went through on his own and heat Warburton with a low drive. At the interval the score was:— United, 2 goals; Athletic, nil. On resuming, the game was fast and keenly , contested for ten minutes, when the Athletic's defence broke down before the United's strong attack, and Poulter increased the lead. This appeared to demoralise the Athletic players, and during the next ten minutes further goals were .scored by Millward, Scott and Poulter, and the concluding stages of the game were alto- gether in favour of the United. The attempts of the Athletic were easily held in check by the United half-back line. At the call of time the result was :—Fairfield United, 9 goals; Fair- field Athletic, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>ORFORD WHITE STAR v. EARLESTOWN</head>
        <p>ORFORD WHITE STAR v. EARLESTOWN</p>
        <p>Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>. ..... These teams met on Saturday on the Bar- racks Field, the following playrs lining out : White Star: Mellor; Piling and Gosling; Gifford, Denny and Caldwell; J. Smith, T. Smith, Meachin, Dunbobbin and Allen: Y.M.C.A.: Martin; Chambers and Pope; Fair- hurst, Goulden and Buckley; Taylor, Gilman, Riley, Hughes and Lee. Referee, Mr. W. Bennett. Riley kicked off for the visitors against the slope, but could make little headway, the home team playing strongly. Good combination by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>GREAT GAME AT LOVELY-LANE GROUND.</head>
        <p>GREAT GAME AT LOVELY-LANE GROUND.</p>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS' v. BANK QUAY RECS.</p>
        <p>The meeting of these keen rivals in the local League Competition on the Lovely-lane ground brought together a great crowd of supporters, and a very keen and exciting game was wit- nessed.</p>
        <p>Referee, R. G. Brandretli. St. Barnabas' i Shaw; Glover and Long; Glover, Kimberley and O'Neil; Walker, Dar- lington, Irons, Vernon and Houghton. Bank Quay Recs.: Bushill; F. Millington and Smith; Booth, Welsby and Long; Eccleston, Kendrick, Banner, Millington and Lemmons. The Saints won the toss, and Banner set the ball rolling towards Lovely-lane goal. Follow- ing the opening exchanges the players quickly settled down to their game, and some excellent play was witnessed. The backs on both sides were very prominent, kicking well and with good judgment. The Recs. opened the score with a .capital goal by Banner. Then play be- came keen and exciting, and the Saints attacked strongly, their forwards giving a good exhibi- tion. Irons equalised in brilliant fashion. Both teams played to obtain the lead. The Saints' half-backs proved to be in excellent trim; they fed their forwards very neatly, and, after several attacks had been repulsed by the Recs.' defence, Vernon put the Saints ahead with a rasping shot. The game was now contested in ding-dong style, lunch to the satisfaction of the spectators, but the defence on both sides held out until the interval, when the score was 2 goals against I in favour of the Saints. I On resuming, the Recs., having the slope in their favour, quickly brought pressure to bear 1 upon the Saints' defence. Glover and Long proved to be very safe, and a splendid run by Vernon and Houghton ended by the latter mak- ing a splendid shot, which came within. an ace of scoring. The Recs.' defence prevailed, and the game was transferred to the Saints' goal area, I and Kendrick equalised with a shot that beat Shaw all the way. Being again on equal terms, both teams struggled harder than ever, and play was evenly contested in mid-field. Kimberley, for the Saints, defended very finely. Gradually the Rees. worked their way towards the Saints' goal, when Shaw was at fault with a partial clearance, and the ball was rushed through by the Recs.' forwards. From now till the finish the Saints were very .dangerous, and pressed very hard, gaining several corner kicks, but failing to improve upon them. Then Bushill was applauded for a very fine save; he just man- aged to put the ball over the bar. Result: Bank Quay Recs., 3 goals; St. Barnabas,' 2 goals. The committee of the St. Barnabas' Club wish it to be known that the collection, which amounted to 15s; 3d.. was handed to the Vicar, the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth, to be sent, along with the proceeds of a rummage sale which was held at the school, for the soldiers' and sailors' funds for winter clothing, and not the Belgian Refugees' Fund, as had been advertised by mis- take.</p>
        <p>ELM WOOD CONGREGATIONALISTS V. NEWTON CONGREGATIONALISTS.</p>
        <p>At Manchester-road. Teams :</p>
        <p>Elmwood Cons.: Jim Phoenix ; Jos. Phoenix and Hall; Pritchard, Mellor and Dutton; Smith, Gleave, Ashton, Wells and Lamb. Newton Cons.: Dagnall ; Tithers and Law- ranee; Clark, Porter and Atkin; Beresford, Dodd, Rhubottom, Jones and Gleaves. Referee, G. Bomber. . The visitors won the toss, and Elmwood de- fended the Cemetery goal. Ashton set the ball rolling, and play- was contested for a while in mid-field, the backs on both sides being equal to all calls made upon them. From a breakaway by the visitors, Jones caused the home goal- keeper to handle, and he cleared the danger very neatly. The visitors attacked the home goal very strongly, but Phoenix and hall Played a good defensive game, and repulsed the attack several times. Following mid-field exchanges the visitors returned to the attack, when it piece of slack play on the part of the home backs allowed the visitors' forwards to get through, and lanbottom scored. This was immediately followed by another goal by Clark. This second reverse roused the home team, and a good run by Wells and Lamb enabled Ashton to - score their first goal. Half-time Newton,, 2 goals; Elthwood, 1 goal. IThe second portion commenced in spirited fashion, the visitors showing up well .A mis-</p>
      </div>
