      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <p>1I</p>
        <p>PRIZE ESSAY. Roy of Thirteen Sums Up the Situation.</p>
        <p>We give the essay by John Haines (of the Bolton Boys' School), which was adjudged the best of all those sent in:— _ _</p>
        <p>"'England expects every man will do his duty V By 'duty' is meant what one ought to do, and Nelson's motto is applicable at all times, to every man, in every station of life. Englishmen recognise their duty in having good principles and ideals in maintaining honour and justice. We find ourselves at pre- sent involved in the greatest war of history. We have entered it, not because we love to fight, but because stern necessity has compelled us to do so in the cause of an ultimate lasting peace. Has it been our duty to go to war? The answer is Yes,' *cause we, on principle, must withstand at all costs the spirit of mili- tary domineering which is the great enemy of peace. Now, times of war bring duties which do not exist in times of peace. We realise how hateful war is in itself, yet our soldiers in the fighting line must bring their deadly weapons into use, not for the sake of slaughter itself, but in stern defence of honour and justice. We may sink our enemy's ships, but we shall not shrink from our duty in saving their crews from drowning. We may blow up the Krupp Factory at Essen, but we shall not wantonly destroy churches, cathedrals and libraries. We may charge our enemy with the bayonet, but we shall not molest women and children. We know we are face to face with dreadful slaughter and much agony, and we must be prepared to meet them bravely. We know what war is, and we encounter the horrors of war in order to make an end of them if possible. Now, it is evident that 'every man' cannot be in the forefront of the battle line. Nay, the majority must remain at home. What are their special duties at this trying time? In the first place they must stand united, and put aside all their little differences. They must be calm; and determined to avoid all excess of excitement and restlessness. They must not be unduly joyful at news of victory, nor unduly depressed at news of defeat; for there are sure to be both victories and defeats. Another very important duty is to do all in their power to keep the trade and business of the country going. Special duties at this time are—the care of the wives and children of those who are called to the front,' the pre- paration of hospitals where the wounded may be cared for and nursed back to health, and prayer that the war may come to a speedy end, and that the peace of the world, for which our whole nation is working, may soon be accom- plished."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>LABOUR AND THE WAR. An Answer to Attempted Misrepresen• tations.</head>
        <p>LABOUR AND THE WAR. An Answer to Attempted Misrepresen• tations.</p>
        <p>A manifesto has been issued by the General Federation of Trade Unions (representing 1,006,904 trade unionists) to its members and its affiliations in Europe and America, which states that in Germany and in Austria, and in neutral States, persistent attempts are being made to misrepresent the attitude of the British Labour movement towards the Government and the crisis. The Federation, therefore, declares at the outset that— In the opinion of the millions of trade unionists the responsibility for the war does not rest upon the policy or conduct of Great Britain.</p>
        <p>Referring to the relief measures, it states that the biggest effort yet made in that direction is totally inadequate; even the Prince of Wales' Fund can only relieve a tithe of the distress which already exists. "In this war the men at the top of the social scale have given their lives freely, and it would be ungenerous not to give credit for and express appreciation of their gal- lantry and self-sacrifice. The workman, too, at the call of his country, has left his job and his home, and is doing his share and doing it volun- tarily, but there is a large percentage -of the comfortable class whose main contributions have been criticism and advice." The manifesto calls for the co-ordination of charities and relief funds, and suggests that the prevention of waste and overlapping will be done better and more sympathetically if organised labour co-operates. In the furtherance of all these objects the Management Committee de- clares its readiness to act with all the force and influence at its command, and it invites the co- operation of all persons or organisations who desire to realise the objects set forth. . _ _</p>
        <p>oi ter ng .r.</p>
        <p>It concludes with the hope that " with the destruction of the Prussian militarist caste, which for forty years has worked for war, inter- national friendships, and efforts may be re- newed." ,</p>
        <p>-The manifesto is signed by Mr. J. O'Grady, M.P. (chairman), Furnishing Trade Association; Mr. Joseph Cross (vice-chairman). Northern Counties' Weavers;. Mr. John Ward, M.P. (treasurer), Navvies, Builders, and General Labourers; Mr. James Crinion (trustee), Amal- gamated Card and Blowing Room Operatives; Mr. Allen Gee (trustee), Yorkshire Textile Workers; Councillor Alexander Wilkie, M.P. (trustee), AsSociated Shipwrights; Mr. J. N. Bell, National Amalgamated Union of Labour; Mr. Ben Cooper, Cigar Makers' Mutual Associa- tion'; Councillor Ivor H. Gwynne, Tin and Sheet Millmen; Mr. Ben Tillett, Dock, Wharf, and Riverside Workers; Councillor T. Mallalien, Amalgamated Felt Hatters' Union; Mr. W. Marsland, Amalgamated Cotton Spinners; Coun- cillor A. Short, Boilermakers and Iron and Steel Shipbuilders; Mr. T. F. Richards, Boot and Shoe Federa °Peratives; Mr. John Taylor, Midland Counties' tion; and Mr. W. A. Appleton, secretary.</p>
        <p>LOYAL AFRICAN CHIEF. Griffith, the Paramount Chief of Basutoland, has offered the King financial assistance, and both he and Lewanika, the Chief of the Barotse in Northern Rhodesia, have assured the Gov- ernment of their loyalty and readiness to pre- pare for war.</p>
        <p>Summary of Authentic News From All</p>
        <p>Involved Areas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>SPECIALLY COMPILED FOR "THE EXAMINER."</head>
        <p>SPECIALLY COMPILED FOR "THE EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>TWELFTI</p>
        <p>FRANCO-BELGIAN FRONTIER.</p>
        <p>GERMANS FORCED FROM THE COAST.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday it was reported that the Ger- mans had reached the Belgian coast at Ostend; but the Belgian Army had joined up with the Allies and so become a fighting force with liberty of action.</p>
        <p>Ypres had fallen into the Allies' hands before the enemy reached Ostend. The Paris communique on Sunday announced progress in several places, while the London Press Bureau said a 30-mile advance has been made during the past few days. The German right wing was once again yielding under heavy pressure. _ _</p>
        <p>Further to the south a sensible advance was officially reported to have been made by the Allies between Arras and the Oise. The Germans advanced about ten miles from Ostend, and bombarded the Allies' front be- tween Nieuport and a point east of Dixmude. Farther east, and slightly southwards, the Allied forces—among whom the Belgians receive special mention—bent back the German line and held Roulers. _ _ _</p>
        <p>But the weight of the battle was farther south. In La Bassee there was heavy fighting, the Allies gaining ground " house by house." Round Arras the action raged for 10 days without re- spite. Here the Allied troops showed the utmost perseverance and dash.</p>
        <p>GERMAN FAILURE TO BREAK THROUGH. In the region of Chaulnes the enemy has again made a strong attempt to break the angle formed by the Allied line just north of the point where it turns to envelop his right flank. This attempt has again failed. The Allies again made some progress on Tues- day at different points on their front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>VIOLENT ATTACKS ON THE BELGIAN ARMY.</head>
        <p>VIOLENT ATTACKS ON THE BELGIAN ARMY.</p>
        <p>The Belgian Army gsustained without giving way violent attacks on the line of the Yser. There has been more fighting in the Ypres dis- trict.</p>
        <p>The enemy's defensive positions round Lille still held out; but definitely on the defensive, and the Allies were maintaining an unremit- ting effort to break his resistance.</p>
        <p>VIOLENT BUT UNAVAILING ATTACKS.</p>
        <p>Wednesday's Paris bulletin was unusually brief. It sufficed to show that the enemy's main effort was being made close to the coast and at La Bassee, a few miles south-west of Line.</p>
        <p>, At Nieuport and Dixmude, close to the coast about 10 miles south of Ostend, as well as at La Bassee, the enemy's attacks were "particu- larly violent." All, however, were repulsed, "with great energy" by the Allied Armies.</p>
        <p>THE NAVY ASSISTS. About Nieuport on the Belgian coast where the Allied front reaches the sea the British Navy lent the armies valuable aid. Three heavily armed monitors,. bought by the Admir- alty from Brazil, for whom they were complet- ing in England when war broke out, steamed in close to the shore, and by shelling the German flank powerfully assisted the Belgian troops. Machine guns were landed at Nieuport, and by that means also the navy reinforced the defence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>BRITISH SHIPS SHELL GERMAN TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>BRITISH SHIPS SHELL GERMAN TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau- issued -..naval casualty list on Thursday morning, together with a brief statement as to the naval operations on the Belgian coast. The communication is as follows :</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Admiralty announces that the monitors Severn (Commander Eric J. A. Fullerton), Humber (Commander Arthur L. Snagge), and Mersey (Lieutenant Commander Robert A. Wilson) have recently been engaged in operations on the Belgian coast, firing on the right flank of the German army. Owing to their light draft they have been able to contribute materially to the success of the operations in this district, and they have already abundantly justified their acquisition on the outbreak of the war. F In addition, detachments with machine guns have been landed from these vessels to assist in the defence of Nieuport, where they performed meritorious service.</p>
        <p>BELGIAN ARMY DISTINGUISHES ITSELF. The following was issued from the Press Bureau on Wednesday night : Throughout yesterday the enemy made vigorous counter-attacks against the Allies' front, but were beaten back, suffering consider- able losses.</p>
        <p>. _ The Belgian Army in particular distinguished itself by its spirited and brave defence of its position.</p>
        <p>In regard to the last part of the above an- nouncement by the War Office, the Press Bureau has received from an authentic source the fol- lowing description of the Belgian Army's work, which will be read with pride both by the many Belgians who have had to flee from their native land and by those Allies amongst whom they are now finding temporary rest and shelter For the last four days the Belgian Army have been in trenches holding the line of some thirty kilometres with the greatest determination against heavy odds. On several occasions they have made brave and successful counter-attacks against the German forces attacking the position they hold, and have shown the soldier-like qualities that have distinguished the Belgian Army during the long period they have been fighting against superior forces of the enemy in their country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>FRANTIC GERMAN EFFORTS UNAVAILING.</head>
        <p>FRANTIC GERMAN EFFORTS UNAVAILING.</p>
        <p>_ ..... Dispatches on Thursday and Friday showed that the Germans were making frantic but un- availing efforts on the coast and Belgian fron- tier: The warships were lending effective aid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>ALLIES' PROGRESS IN FRONT.</head>
        <p>ALLIES' PROGRESS IN FRONT.</p>
        <p>At the other end of the line of battle, on the right bank of the Meuse, a French force sud- denly revealed itself, operating in the neigh- bourhood of the Camp des Romains, a fort taken by the Germans about a month ago. It had been known for some time that a French column from Toul has been advancing in this neighbourhood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>AT SEA.</head>
        <p>AT SEA.</p>
        <p>CAPTURE OF A GERMAN GUNBOAT</p>
        <p>• Vice-Admiral Sir George Patey, commanding the Australian Fleet, reports from Rabaul, Ger- man New Guinea, the capture of the German gunboat Comet, a small sailing vessel which had a complete wireless telegraph station on board. The Nusa was commissioned on October 9 to search for the Comet on the north-east coast of New Guinea. The expedition was entirely suc- cessful. . .</p>
        <p>There were no casualties, and the captain, four officers, and 52 natives were taken pris- oners. The Comet has now been commissioned as a vessel of H.M. Australian Navy. '</p>
        <p>BRILLIANT FEAT IN NORTH SEA.</p>
        <p>Four German Destroyers Sunk.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon H.M.S. Undaunted, with the destroyers Lance, Legion, Lennox and Loyal, sank four German destroyers off the coast of Holland. In this action one British officer and four men were wounded, but only 31 Ger- mans survived. Captain Cecil Fox, who was in charge of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>WEEK.</head>
        <p>WEEK.</p>
        <p>Amphion when she was sunk through striking a mine in the first days of the war, commanded the Undaunted. His ship is a very fast cruiser, of the same class as the Arethusa, which fought in the Heligoland Bight, and completed since the war began. Accounts of the action given by men on the British destroyers which sunk the enemy show the victors' gunnery to have been much the better. Although out-classed and out-fought, the Germans defended themselves bravely. Their last ship went down about an hour and a half after the engagement began. Berlin states that the sunken torpedo boat destroyers were S 115, S 117, S 118, S 119.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>GERMAN OIL STEAMER CAUGHT BY THE CRUISER SUFFOLK.</head>
        <p>GERMAN OIL STEAMER CAUGHT BY THE CRUISER SUFFOLK.</p>
        <p>The armed liner Caronia took to Halifax on Sunday afternoon the oil tank steamer Bren- dilla, which was flying the American flag. The Brendilla is, however, a German steamer, her name having been changed to the American register after war was declared. _ _</p>
        <p>'She was captured by the Suffolk whilst leav- ing New York, it being believed that she car- ried contraband destined for German cruisers at sea. The captured vessel's firemen attempted to flood the engine-room by opening the sea- cocks, and the captain of the Caronia was obliged to put an additional farce on board, and remove the recalcitrant firemen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>FURTHER DESTRUCTION BY THE EM DEN.</head>
        <p>FURTHER DESTRUCTION BY THE EM DEN.</p>
        <p>The Emden has made another unwelcome ap- pearance in the Indian Ocean. This time she has emerged from the obscurity in which her commander has so skilfully involved his move- ments at a point about 150 miles south-west of Cochin. There she has rounded up and sunk four British merchant vessels and a dredger bound for Tasmania. A sixth British vessel, the Exford, outward bound with coal, was cap- tured.</p>
        <p>Since the Emden escaped from the China Seas early in the war and found her way into Indian waters she has inflicted considerable loss on British shipping. She has captured thirteen steamers at different times in the Bay of Ben- gal and varied her commerce-destroying activi- ties by shelling Madras. The damage the Emden has done is estimated at £2,000,000.</p>
        <p>FEARED LOSS OF SUBMARINE. Submarine E 3 is overdue at her base and the Admiralty fear that she has been sunk. The Berlin wireless circular two nights ago an- nounced that E 3 had been sunk in a German bay on the North Sea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>THIRTEEN VESSELS SUNK.</head>
        <p>THIRTEEN VESSELS SUNK.</p>
        <p>A message received yesterday stated that a German cruiser had sunk thirteen British mer- chant ships in the Atlantic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>RUSSIA MAKES HEADWAY.</head>
        <p>RUSSIA MAKES HEADWAY.</p>
        <p>Austrian attempts to force a passage of the San in Galicia have been repulsed. The fight- in.' in some places took ,the form of bayonet charges, in which the Russians excel. "The Times" special correspondent telegraph- ing from Lwow describes a journey through conquered Galicia, from which he has formed the opinion that the merchants and other in- habitants are genuinely friendly and that the Russian occupation was humanely carried out. The Russian General Staff reports partial suc- cesses in very severe engagements in the War- saw district and south of Przemysl. Hard fighting, with results favourable for Russid, has occurred on the Middle Vistula to the south of Warsaw. So far as the operations have gone the Czar's troops have had the best of matters, and have repulsed with heavy loss the German advance upon Warsaw. Near the southern flank the Austrians attacked with little success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>ANOTHER LONG BATTLE.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER LONG BATTLE.</p>
        <p>"The Times" military correspondent writes of the situation on the Eastern frontiers. His conclusion is that the main Russian armies, having been concentrated on the river Bug, have advanced to a carefully-chosen but very extensive front. The battle that is now in pro- gress may, as is the way of modern battles, last a long time. The official news from Petrograd supports this conclusion. No definite result either way is yet reported, though numbers of Austrian pris- oners are still being taken south of Przemysl.</p>
        <p>GERMAN LOSSES. The German losses on the Middle Vistula have been considerable. An attempt to cross the river and occupy Warsaw has come to noth- ing. Further south in Galicia the Austrians are so completely in check that they have in- terrupted their announcements of victories. Of course, the Kaiser's main armies still hold un- shaken Western Poland, but the failure of their first advance must have an effect on their offen- sive power. Their march eastward has stopped. their advanced guards have been beaten, and they may now be delayed to await reinforce- ments. Help can be procured only by with- drawing troops from the French battlefields.</p>
        <p>GERMANS ROUTED BEFORE WARSAW. The German attempt to possess themselves of Warsaw has failed, the Russian concentration in the neighbourhood of Warsaw having pro- vided an unpleasant surprise for the invaders of Russian Poland. "The Times" correspondent at Petrograd throws considerable light on the state of affairs in this quarter. Ile announces that the Ger- mans are in full flight from Warsaw, leaving their wounded behind them, and that the Rus- sians are in pursuit. There has naturally been a good deal of specu- lation as to the factors which made it possible for the Germans to advance so near to Warsaw. The correspondent shows that the Russian General Staff did not make a temporary sacri- fice of Poland west of the Vistula to the invaders for nothing. The Russian commanders have acted through- out on a consistent strategic conception of their main object. They have not allowed themselves to be diverted from this strategy by political considerations which might have dictated an effort to hold Poland beyond the Vistula in defiance of military expediency. Russia's claim of success find unwilling sup- port in the Berlin wireless circular, which has taken to announcing "no result" and "110 change" on the Polish front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>GERMAN REPULSE COMPLETE.</head>
        <p>GERMAN REPULSE COMPLETE.</p>
        <p>The news of the complete repulse of the Ger- man advance on 3Vaisaw received further con- firmation on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>SOUTH AFRICA.</head>
        <p>SOUTH AFRICA.</p>
        <p>THE REBELLION FIZZLING OUT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Merriman, the veteran statesman of South Africa, has made a speech denouncing in the strongest terms the treachery of Maritz. Commandants representing all the Orange Free States north of Bloemfontein have met and offered their services, within or without the Union, to the Government. They have also asked General Smuts to employ the burghers in dealing with Maritz.</p>
        <p>' The traitor was able to deliver 63 loyal officers and men of his former command as prisoners of war to the Germans. CAPTURES OF MARITZ'S MEN. Three officers and seventy men of Maritz's commando were captured by the Imperial Light Horse, and are now detained as prisoners of war. A further batch of four officers and forty men surrendered voluntarily. The majority of the latter have volunteered for active service. Mes- sages have also been received from others signi-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 24, 1914.</p>
        <p>fying their intention to escape and rejoin the Union Forces.</p>
        <p>It is rumoured that Maritz is quarrelling with the Germans, who do not like his inaction. Corporal Spencer, Imperial Light Horse, has been killed in a scrap with Maritz's men. Dr. de Villiers has been released by Maritz.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>JAPAN PLODS ALONG.</head>
        <p>JAPAN PLODS ALONG.</p>
        <p>EARLY SUCCESS IN VIEW.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the German fortress of Tsingtao was bombarded by the Anglo-Japanese forces from the sea and air.</p>
        <p>JAPANESE CRUISER SUNK.</p>
        <p>It is officially announced that the Japanese cruiser Takachilio was sunk by mine in lilac). chau Bay on Saturday night. One officer and nine men are known to have been saved. The Takachiho was on patrol duty when she fouled a mine. Destroyers hurried to her assistance, but the cruiser disappeared quickly, and rescue work was difficult owing to the darkness. Twenty-eight officers, 54 petty officers and 189 seamen lost their lives. The Takachiho has been off the effective list of the Japanese Fleet for over three years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>SIEGE GUNS AT TSINGTAO.</head>
        <p>SIEGE GUNS AT TSINGTAO.</p>
        <p>It is announced that siege guns have been placed in position, and that the final attack on Tsingtao is expected to start a,t. any moment.</p>
        <p>A TYPHOON AT_ KIAO-CHAU.</p>
        <p>A typhoon has struck Kiao-Chau, destroying the landing-stage. Twenty Japanese sailors have been drowned.</p>
        <p>GERMAN DESTROYER DESTROYED.</p>
        <p>S 90, a German destroyer which escaped from Tsing-tao, has been found by the Japanese aground and destroyed on the Shantung coast. Her crew had deserted her. No attempt to use her liberty was made by the vessel.</p>
        <p>JAPANESE CROWN PRINCE'S GIFT TO BRITISH TROOPS.</p>
        <p>The War Office makes the following announce- ment: His Imperial Highness the Crown 'Prince of Japan has, through an aide-de-camp, deliv- ered a most gracious message to the British troops operating with the Japanese forces before Tsingtao, and has presented them with a gift of refined sake (rice wine).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>GERMAN EXIT FROM PACIFIC.</head>
        <p>GERMAN EXIT FROM PACIFIC.</p>
        <p>The Navy Depart-men-t- has -81;i-iin;;Ced at Tokio the occupation for military purposes of the Marianne and Marshall Islands and the East and West Caroline Archipelagoes. The occupation of Jaluit, in the Marshall Islands, and Yap in the Caroline Islands had been already announced from Tokio. All the German islands in the Pacific are now in other hands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>ARMED GERMAN LINERS TAKEN.</head>
        <p>ARMED GERMAN LINERS TAKEN.</p>
        <p>Reuter's Rome correspondent telegraphs: The Japanese Embassy communicates the follow- ing :</p>
        <p>Two auxiliary cruisers of the enemy found. One sank herself; the other was captured. ‘.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>WAR ITEMS.</head>
        <p>WAR ITEMS.</p>
        <p>MILITARY ABSENTEES.</p>
        <p>At the Bore-iigh--POli;---&amp;-r-t—y—e-s-terday Pte. W. Farrington was charged with being an absentee from the 3rd Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment since the 12th inst. He was ordered to be handed over to an escort.</p>
        <p>FOUND ANOTHER JOB-TEMPORARILY!</p>
        <p>instead of spending his days chasing truants, Mr. J. H. Knowles, one of the school attendance officers for Warrington, is engaged in the more serious business of rounding up Germans, and whilst he is so occupied the Education Com- mittee have appointed Mr. Alfred Wood to carry on the more peaceful duties at home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>PRESENT FOR A "TERRIER."</head>
        <p>PRESENT FOR A "TERRIER."</p>
        <p>During a parade of the Fairfield and Howley Company of the Warrington and District Pre- paratory Training Corps, on the Cricket Ground on Saturday afternoon, Mr. Samuel J. Price, who has joined the Reserve Battalion of the local Territorials, was the recipient of a pre- sent from the members, to whom he has acted as instructor. The presentation was made by Mr. William Holbrook on behalf of the corps.</p>
        <p>RAILWAYMEN'S VIEWS. At the monthly meeting of the Warrington branch of the Railwaymen's Association a reso- lution was carried urging upon the Government the necessity of giving an assurance that ade- quate and reasonable provision shall be made for all the dependents of the men who lose their lives at the front and for those who are perma- nently incapacitated, a minimum ,21 a week basis being suggested.</p>
        <p>DESERTED FROM THE SOUTH LANCS. James Harris, a private in the 6th Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment, who, at a previous court, had been charged with desertion, was at the Police Court on Monday ordered to be handed over to a military escort.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER DESERTER ARRESTED. Herbert Sherratt, a private in the 3rd Batta- lion of the South Laucashire Regiment, which is stationed at Great Crosby, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday with being a deserter from his regiment since October 3rd.— P.C. Falkner said that he saw the prisoner in the town. He had no numerals on his coat. When he asked the prisoner for his pass he said he had not got one. He was taken into custody, and later admitted that he was a deserter.—The Chief Constable said that a telegram had been received from the Commanding Officer stating that an escort was being sent.—The Bench ordered prisoner to be handed over to the escort.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL TEACHERS WITH THE COLOURS. During the time they are serving with the . colours the difference between their salaries and their Army pay will be made up by the Educa- tion Committee to the nominees of Messrs. John Holt (Evelyn-street School) and Samuel J. Price (St. Mary's School), two certificated assistant masters, who are absent on military service. In the cases of Messrs. David M. James and Thomas M. Savage (of Beamont School), who are also with the colours, 12s. 6d. weekly will be paid to nominees, and in the cases of Messrs. Harold Beech, Reginald A. Dale and Wm. S. Furness, the nominees will receive 10s. weekly, a like sum being paid to the nominee of Mr. Thomas Stir- rup, of the St. Benedict's School staff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>ABSENTEE SOLDIER CHARGED.</head>
        <p>ABSENTEE SOLDIER CHARGED.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Saturday, Private Alfred Barrow, of the 6th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, stationed at Tid- worth, was charged with being absent from his regiment since October 13.—The Chief Constable said prisoner, who had been found at his home, had been gran.ted leave from October 10th to October 13th, but. had failed to return. When arrested he stated he was suffering from a rup- ture.—Prisoner was remanded, but on Monday the Chief Constable told the magistrates that the commanding officer had suggested that pris- oner should be handed over, so that he could go to hospital and be examined.—This was the course adopted.</p>
        <p>BROKE OUT OF BARRACKS. , At the Police Court on Thursday morning, Leslie McClelland, a lance-corporal in the sth Battalion of the Royal Inniskilling Fusiliers, was charged with being a deserter from his regiment since October ,17th.—lie pleaded not guilty.—Detective-Sergeant Holland said that when he accused prisoner of being a deserter, he replied, "No; lamon a pass." He produced a pass. Witness, however, told him that lie had received information' that he had been placed under arrest by the military authorities, and he replied, "That is so. I was put under arrest and then put in the guardroom, and I walked out of the guardroom when I saw the door open."—The Chief Constable told the Court that he had received a communication from pris- oner's comthanding officer to the effect that the man broke out of barracks whilst a prisoner, and had in his possession at the time a leave pass that had been granted to him, but which was cancelled immediately be was placed under arrest. —Prisoner, who said he had only been in the army eight weeks, was ordered to be handed over to an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>MIRTH AND MELODY AT THE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>"Our Kid" is the title of a merry pot-pourri of songs, dances and jokes which is being played twice nightly at the Royal Court Theatre this week by a little company of artistes, every one of whom is well up to his, or her, work. The piece entertains vastly from start to finish; it has not a dull moment about it, and, as a two-hour cure for the dumps, it can be safely recommended to all in search of a specific for that unpleasant malady. See "Our Kid," and no matter how misanthropical you may be feeling, "You will laugh some," and, with laughing, grow better. Quips and cranks that belong to the order commonly known as side-splitting, tunes that ring in tluzi head for long after you have heard them, songs that bring the patriotic' fervour in one to boiling-point, and others that enchant the ear, graceful dances that make an irresistible ap- peal to the eye—all these things, and more, are embodied in this lightsome, frolicsome trifle, which, from beginning to end, is played with a jolly swing that leaves nothing to be desired. There is a plot somewhere about the piece, but the person who pays at the door need not let that worry him, for it is of no real importance; the fun, the music, the pretty dresses, and the excellent scenery being the things that matter.</p>
        <p>A Sims' Drama Next Week.</p>
        <p>Next week lovers of drama are to be afforded the opportunity of seeing "The Staircase of Fortune," an enthralling play from the skilled pen of that master of melodrama, Mr. George R. Sims.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The programme submitted to Hippodrome patrons this week is ;very bright and entertain- ing. Mr. Paul Mill presents "The Courtiers" in an original musical comedy scena, and the quartet of ladies combine with the four gentle- men, who make up the octet, in giving a most excellent turn. Each of the eight is a most capable vocalist and many well-chosen songs are rendered in a fashion which cannot fail to win the approbation' of the audience. The octet also give a pleasing display of artistic dancing. The Bradshaw Bros. pay a welcome return visit and as usual they score a decided success with their acrobatic eccentricities. Josephine Langley is a charming ventriloquist, who "works" three figures with the utmost ease and presents a really clever show: De- scribed as "the human gramophone," Victor Wakeman gives a very entertaining perform- ance, in which he sings soprano, tenor, bari- tone and bass. Mr. and Mrs. Manning pre- sent a breezy comedy duologue, entitled "Our Wedding Anniversary," in which the fun is both new and really witty. Melt and Bray are smart eccentric comedians, who know how to please; and another capital comedy turn is provided by Damerell and Rutland. Topical war pictures are shown on the cinema. Musical Novelty Next Week. "Symphonia," the big Corner-Grey musical novelty, will top the bill at the Hippodrome next week, whilst another "star" turn t' ill Le supplied by Jock Mills, the Scotch ,omedian, who has been described as "Harry Laular the Second." "Captain Careless, V.C.," a one-act play, will form another leading attraction ,n an excellent programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>PICTUREDROME.</head>
        <p>PICTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>An interesting programme, in which comedy and drama were happily blended, was pre- sented at the Picturedrome during the first half of the week. "The Reformation of Cal- liope," a drama of the West, proved a big attraction, as during the full length of the two reels of this up-to-date film the interest neveii slackens. This remark also applied to the instalment of the series of films depicting the tale of "Lucille Love." "He Never Found Out" and "That's Torn It !" were comics of a first-rate order, the latter being especially funny. "Some Doings" was the title of a third comedy film, which caused the audience to roar with laughter. "Interlaken" was a beautifully coloured film, which depicted the beautiful scenery of the Swiss mountains in the neighbourhood of that famous health and holiday resort. "The Gaumont Journal" of topical events was, as always, watched with interest, whilst the singing-picture, "Good-bye, boys," caused merriment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>For Next Week.</head>
        <p>For Next Week.</p>
        <p>An exciting instalment of the picture serial dealing with the adventures of "Lucille Love" is promised for the first three nights of next week, whilst the "star" picture on Thursday, Friday and Saturday will be "Her Big Scoop," which deals with an interesting adventure in the career of an American lady reporter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>An excellent programme was presented at the Star Kinema at the beginning of this week. The films included two excruciatingly funny comedy films, "Mike Wins the Champion- ship" and " Snitz Joins the Force." "The Adventures of Kathlyn"—the Star's weekly serial—depicted her thrilling experienceil as "A Royal Slave." A fine "war" issue of "The Gaumont Graphic" (includ€ll " The Fall of Antwerp"—a remarkably complete "special." "Pathe's Animated Gazette" also depicted a variety of war incidents. "He Never Knew" was an engrossing drama; beautifully pre- sented. - .</p>
        <p>The second fine bill of fare includes two war subjects, "The Sack of Termonde" and "The Battle of Lebeke," neither of which shoind be missed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>Next Week:</head>
        <p>Next Week:</p>
        <p>The first programme at this popular house of entertainment will include "Child of My Heart," whilst on the last three days the -3hief film will be " The Jew."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>A film showing the new battalion of the War- rington Territorials and the Preparatory Train- ing Corps at drill gives this week's programmes at the Picture House an interesting topical and local touch. The Territorial recruits are seen executing various military movements and physical exercises on the recreation ground in Bank Park, whilst the members of the Train- ing Corps are depicted in line and on the march on the Warrington Cricket Field. This film is being shown all the week. The other films sub- mitted on the first three days were excellent. "The String of Pearls" was a fine two-reel drama, in which interest was sustained from beginning to end. "A Chance in a Life" and "Broncho Bill's Great Leap" were also absorb- ing dramas—the latter, as the title would sug- gest, dealing with life in the Far West. The comedy element was provided through the medium of "The Bathing Beauty," "Peter Loves Work," and "Fire! Fire!" "America's Heroic Nurses" was an interesting war film.</p>
        <p>Next Week's Attractions.</p>
        <p>For the first three nights of next week the main attraction will be the great fiatriotic film based on William Le Queux's thrilling story, "The Invasion of England." On Thursday, Friday and Saturday a fine picture problem- play, "Was she right in forgiving him?" will be shown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-10-24_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>Mr. Redmond on What Ireland is Prepared to Give.</head>
        <p>Mr. Redmond on What Ireland is Prepared to Give.</p>
        <p>Speaking at Kilkenny on Saturday, after re- ceiving the freedom of. the city, Mr. John Red- mond, M.P„ asked how in particular did the war affect Ireland. "J think," he said, "the occasion is one for very plain speaking and clear thinking on this matter. What is the theory of Home Rule? What is the basic theory of the whole constitutional movement of the last century in Ireland? The theory all through the century put forward with the unanimous con- sent of Nationalist Ireland was that we in this country claimed to be an autonomous nation within the circle of the Empire, and that if that were done Ireland was quite willing, while tak- ing any advantage that might flow from her con- nection with the Empire, was ready manfully and honestly to take her share of the burdens east upon all." There were in Ireland as well as South Africa a small body of men who, forgetting that Ire- land had now a free Constitution, would have , her break her leaders' pledges that if Rome Rule were conceded Ireland would be a source of strength and not weakness to the Empire. But the Irish people would not act on the advice of a handful of mischief-makers.</p>
