        <p>RUNCORN DISTRICT COUNCIL. Meetings to be Held Monthly. The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr. C. E. Linaker, sen., presiding. On the proposition of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Wright, it was decided that the Council meetings be held monthly instead of fortnightly. Councillor Linaker pointed out that an Emer- gency Committee of seven could meet whenever necessary to consider any matter of urgency. It would be advantageous to every member of the Council to have the meetings monthly. (Hear, hear.) He would also suggest that the Guar- dians should bold their meetings earlier. Councillor Woodward said there would be an economy in time. The next meeting of the Council will be held in a fortnight, and the ensuing one in six weeks' time. ' NO SPARE BEDS FOR WAR OFFICE. A letter was read from the Assistant Secretary to the War Office inquiring what number of hospital beds the Council could place at their disposal, for cases of enteric fever, without un- duly diminishing the accommodation for the iv ilDno. Councillor said their accommoda- tion was limited and they could not offer any beds. It was decided to send a letter to that effect. THE UP-TO-DATE HOUSEWIFE for , making her Puddings and Pastry, buys SHREDDED ATORA Beef Suet. No chopping, ready for use, goes furtherdsweet and whole- some. Your grocer sells it. Insist upon ATORA, refuse substituted brands.</p>
