        <p>take by Jos. Phoenix brought about the downfall of the home goal for a third time, Dodd doing the needful. The home team improved, and played vigorously to reduce the arrears, Ashton again being successful in scoring. They played with more confidence and kept up the pressure, the visitors being penalised for hands. Lamb took the kick, but shot yards wide. This proved the undoing of the home players, and the visit- ors again took up the attack and further goals were added by Rhubottom, Porter and Gleaves. The result at the call of time was: Newton Cons., 6 goals; Elmwood Cons., 2 goals. PADDINGTON v. WARRINGTON CENTRAL This match resulted in a win for Paddington by 6 goals to one. ITEMS. Longford United are the only unbeaten team in the League. Their latest victims were Latchford Baptists. Sheratt was in shooting form, scoring three times. Pearson's two goals were very well got. The White Stars are shining brightly Orford way. Denny, at centre-half, quite overshadowed I Riley, of Y.M.C.A. Gosling (late of the Heathens) has now become a White Star. ! J. Smith. (late secretary of the John's) has joined the colours . (Army Medical Service Corps). He. was home for the week-end and assisted the Stars, and scored their second goal. Caldwell, the White Star skipper, is very popular with the supporters. St. Barnabas' met their Waterloo when they met the Recs. Bushill, Millington and Smith proved very strong in defence. Banner is very good at centre-forward, always dangerous. Houghton and Vernon- combine well together for the Saints. Kimberley (late Crosfield's) played centre-half, in place of Challinor. 1 Fairfield United fairly routed the Fairfield , Athletic. Result, 9-0, fairly staggered the Athletic team Poulter helped himself to four. Millward, at centre-forward, scored twice. Bebbington played well at centre-half. Roberts, Jackson and Johnson were the pick of the Athletic team.. Pearson scored a couple of good goals for the Baptists. He did not get very good support from his partners. Sherratt was prominent for Longford United. Stevens was a keen tackler at centre-half. Longford have not yet lost a League match. They can boast they are the only undefeated. Will they retain their record against Fairfield United? It is said they have transferred Hunter to a neighbouring club. The Junior Medal Competition is due to com- mence in November. Now is a chance for Bank Quay Athletic, San- key Athletic and Emmanuel Institute to test their abilities. Entries close on November 2nd. MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. Longford United v. Fairfield United; A. Holt. Fairfield Athletic v. Newton Cons.; B. Howell. Earlestown Mission v. Elmwood Cons.; W. Dennett— Paddington v. Latchford Baptists; (I. Knight. Earlestown Y.M.CA. V. St. Barnabas'; Smith. Hollins Green v. Orford White Star; R. G. Brandreth. • West Timperley v. Stockton Heath (North Cheshire League). TEAMS FOR TO-DAY. EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. WALTON. At Walton : Emmanuel Institute: G. Bailey; E. Crozier and S. Hughes; T. Hughes, S. Banner and W. Ayres; W. Eaton, A. Oakes, S. Roberts (capt.), A. Bevan and W. Eaton, jun. Reserve, C. Burgess. ELMWOOD-AVENUE v. EARLESTOWN ST. JOHN'S. At Earlestown Elmwood-avenue: 11. Norke; Jos. Phoenix and James Mellor; Jos. Pritchard, E. B. Mellor and W. Hall; Jos. Smith, D. (Heave, W. C. Ashton, T. Wells, and W. Phoenix. ELMWOOD-AVENUE "A" v. PADGATE UNITED. On the Elmwood Ground, kick-off 3.15:— Elmwood Avenue "A": James Phoenix; R. Wallington and A. N. Other; H. Hazeldine, C. Ellam and F. Dutton; P. Podmore, W. Farrar, N. Ellain, I. Cooper and A. Lamb. PADDINGTON .v. LATCHFORD BAPTISTS. At Paddington. Paddington: George; Holmes and Spencer; Garnett, Wrench and Taylor; Scarisbrick, Bleasdale, Woodhead, Darbyshire and Craik. A FARVER AND HIS GUN. Attack on Harriers on Entering a Field The story of a curious shooting affair was ! related at Chester Assizes on Tuesday, before Mr. Justice Bankes. Hugh Waterworth, aged 70, v. farmer, of Broadbent Fold Farm, Dukin- field, was charged with shooting‘a youth named William Turner, of Millbrook, Stalybridge, with intent to do grievous bodily harm. He Pleaded not guilty. The evidence Showed that Turner is a mem- ber of the Dukinfield Harriers, who on Satur- day held a paper chase. Some of the runners crossed over the prisoner's farm, and he shouted to them to stop. Immediately Turner entered one of the prisoner's fields the prisoner put his gun to his shoulder and shot him. He was struck by nine pellets in the face and chest, but none of the wounds was serious. The prisoner said that he simply held his gun up in order-to frighten Turner, and it acciden- tally went off. He did not intend to shoot him. The jury found the prisoner guilty of un- lawfully wounding. The Judge told the prisoner he was not fit to be trusted with a gun. It was a monstrous thing for a man of his age to have done what lie did. He bound him over in £lO to come up for judgment if called upon, ordered him to pay £5, the costs of the prosecution, and also £lO compensation to the injured man.</p>
